var ATARI_EMU_LYNX = {"key":"lynx","label":"Atari Lynx","order":["readme","01-machine","02-cpu","03-memory","04-mikey","05-cartridge","06-sound","07-suzy","08-boot","09-uart","10-not-established","11-interface","opcodes65c02-py","interface"],"titles":{"readme":"Atari Lynx emulation: a build guide","01-machine":"1. The machine","02-cpu":"2. The CPU","03-memory":"3. Memory map","04-mikey":"4. Mikey: timers, interrupts, display","05-cartridge":"5. The cartridge","06-sound":"6. Sound","07-suzy":"7. Suzy","08-boot":"8. The boot","09-uart":"9. The UART","10-not-established":"10. What is not established","11-interface":"11. The interface and the board","opcodes65c02-py":"Generator: opcodes65c02.py","interface":"Core interface: Atari Lynx"},"head":"<section class=\"gbemu-hero\"><div class=\"gbemu-hero-num\"><b>Lynx</b><span>clean-room core</span></div><div class=\"gbemu-hero-txt\"><h3>How to build the Atari Lynx, exactly as our core runs it</h3><p>Both Lynx models tick by tick: the 65C02 and what an access costs, the memory map, Mikey's timers and display, the cartridge, sound, Suzy's sprite engine and maths, the boot, the UART, then the host interface. Every figure carries a label saying where it comes from, and chapter by chapter the guide says what is not established. Chapters this guide reads from another Atari guide follow it at the end of this tab.</p></div></section>\n<nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Atari Lynx build guide</b> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-readme\">Atari Lynx emulation: a build guide</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine\">1. The machine</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-02-cpu\">2. The CPU</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-03-memory\">3. Memory map</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-04-mikey\">4. Mikey: timers, interrupts, display</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge\">5. The cartridge</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-06-sound\">6. Sound</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-07-suzy\">7. Suzy</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-08-boot\">8. The boot</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-09-uart\">9. The UART</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-10-not-established\">10. What is not established</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-11-interface\">11. The interface and the board</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py\">Generator: opcodes65c02.py</a> <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\">Core interface: Atari Lynx</a></nav>","units":{"readme":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-readme-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-readme\">Atari Lynx emulation: a build guide</h3>\n<p>This folder describes the Atari Lynx (both models) as this project emulates it: enough to build a core, from nothing, that produces the same pictures, sounds, memory contents and instruction timings as ours. It does not assume the 2600 or 7800 guides: what the 65C02 shares with the NMOS 6502 (binary arithmetic, the flags each instruction sets, the stack and the status register's layout) is written out in chapter 2.</p>\n<p>Key: Rule Lynx:n.m: a binding rule; a box headed Explanation: background only, not needed to build.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:0.1</b> (the reading order). Read the chapters in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine\">The machine</a>: parts, the 16 MHz tick, what a CPU access costs (page mode, refresh, display DMA), sleep, power on, the run loop.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-02-cpu\">The CPU</a>: the 65C02, cycle by cycle, and the two models' differences.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-03-memory\">Memory map</a>: MAPCTL, the Suzy and Mikey windows, what a register access waits.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-04-mikey\">Mikey: timers, interrupts, display</a>.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge\">The cartridge</a>: the page shift register and counter, the .lnx header, AUDIN banks, BLL programs, the cartridge EEPROM, headerless Epyx images, cartridge RAM and the LNX2 header, SD card cartridges (not emulated).</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-06-sound\">Sound</a>: the four channels, the stereo mix, the output.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-07-suzy\">Suzy</a>: registers, the maths unit, the sprite engine, the sprite timing model.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-08-boot\">The boot</a>: the loader decryption and the project's own boot firmware.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-09-uart\">The UART</a>: ComLynx framing, the loopback, the interrupt, two consoles on one cable.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-10-not-established\">What is not established</a>.</li><li><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-11-interface\">The interface and the board</a>: the functions a core exposes, the trace format, the test programs, how two cores are compared.</li></ol>\n<p><a href=\"#atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py\"><code>opcodes65c02.py</code></a> generates the CPU's opcode table (both models).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-readme--what-a-build-must-implement\">What a build must implement</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:0.2</b> (what a build must implement). A build must implement each part below, as the section or rule named specifies it (the comparison build's paragraphs and the Explanation boxes can be skipped):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The clock, <code>t</code>, <code>stall</code> and what each CPU access costs (page mode, internal cycles, refresh, display DMA): <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine--what-a-cpu-access-costs\">What a CPU access costs</a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine--refresh-and-display-dma\">Refresh and display DMA</a>.</li><li>Sleep steps and the work <code>w</code> they give Suzy: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine--sleep\">Sleep</a>.</li><li>Power on and the run loop (<code>step_instruction</code>, <code>run_frame</code> and its 450,000-tick cut): <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine--power-on\">Power on</a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-01-machine--the-run-loop\">The run loop</a>.</li><li>The 65C02, both models, every opcode and its bus cycles, flags and the interrupt poll: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-02-cpu\">chapter 2</a> and <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py\"><code>opcodes65c02.py</code></a>.</li><li>The memory map, MAPCTL and the waits before Suzy and Mikey accesses: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-03-memory\">chapter 3</a>.</li><li>Mikey's units, the phase and the tick, clocks, borrows and service, timer register writes and interrupts: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-04-mikey--units\">Units</a> to <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-04-mikey--interrupts\">Interrupts</a>.</li><li>The display: line timing, pixels, DMA bursts, the pen buffer and the flip: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-04-mikey--the-display\">The display</a>.</li><li>SYSCTL1 (power off), IODIR and IODAT: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-04-mikey--other-mikey-registers\">Other Mikey registers</a>.</li><li>The cartridge port, the .lnx, raw and LNX2 files and their order of identification, AUDIN banks: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--the-port\">The port</a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--the-lnx-file\">The .lnx file</a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--audin-banks\">AUDIN banks</a>.</li><li>BLL programs and headerless Epyx images, started without the boot: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--bll-programs\">BLL programs</a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--headerless-epyx-images\">Headerless Epyx images</a>.</li><li>The cartridge EEPROM and its naming by the host: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--the-cartridge-eeprom\">The cartridge EEPROM</a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--naming-the-eeprom-from-the-host\">Naming the EEPROM from the host</a>.</li><li>Cartridge RAM, its three forms: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--cartridge-ram\">Cartridge RAM</a>.</li><li>Sound: the four channels, the stereo mix and the output samples: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-06-sound\">chapter 6</a>.</li><li>Suzy's registers, the maths unit and the buttons: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-07-suzy--registers\">Registers</a>.</li><li>Sleep and done, the parts of a list's time and the stop request: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-07-suzy--sleep-and-done\">Sleep and done</a>.</li><li>The sprite engine (sprites, quadrants, rows, pixels, collisions): <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-07-suzy--the-sprite-engine\">The sprite engine</a>.</li><li>The sprite timing model: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-07-suzy--the-timing-model\">The timing model</a>.</li><li>The boot: decrypting the loader frame, and a boot firmware with the entry points FE00, FE19 and FE4A and the state at 0200 (the host supplies the image, Rule Lynx:8.6): <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-08-boot\">chapter 8</a>.</li><li>The UART, its interrupt and two consoles on the ComLynx cable: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-09-uart\">chapter 9</a>.</li><li>The choices made where the console is not established: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-10-not-established\">chapter 10</a>.</li><li>The host interface: every function and constant, the trace line, the picture and the audio buffer: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-11-interface\">chapter 11</a> and \"Core interface: Atari Lynx\" (<a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE-LYNX.md</code></a>) at the end of this tab.</li></ol>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-readme--labels\">Labels</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:0.3</b> (provenance labels). Every rule carries one of these, usually in its Explanation box; a label says how sure a figure is, never what a core must do:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>HARDWARE-OBSERVED</code></td><td>measured on real consoles by others (for the Lynx, mostly the lynx-tests hardware test programs, whose expected values were recorded on Lynx I and Lynx II units, and their authors' guides)</td></tr><tr><td><code>INSTRUMENT-MEASURED</code></td><td>measured by this project's instruments</td></tr><tr><td><code>MECHANISM</code></td><td>follows from the circuit or the chip's documented design</td></tr><tr><td><code>CONVERGENCE</code></td><td>taken from the Lynx reference emulator (the reference emulator this project compares with, named in the project's internal records; it is the oracle the gates run) and confirmed by this project's trace, memory, picture and sound agreement with it. Where a rule comes from \"the second Lynx emulator\", that is another published Lynx emulator whose source was read, not run (SOURCE-READ: another emulator's source); \"both references\" means the two together</td></tr><tr><td><code>FITTED</code></td><td>chosen because it matched, with no deeper reason</td></tr><tr><td><code>NOT ESTABLISHED</code></td><td>not known; the value used is a choice</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-readme--words\">Words</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:0.4</b> (the glossary). Each term below is used on this tab only in the sense given here. Hexadecimal numbers are written <code>FD20</code> or <code>$FD20</code>; each chapter's \"How to read this chapter\" says which of its numbers are hexadecimal.</p>\n<p>Time:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning (unit; counted from)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>tick</td><td>one period of the 16 MHz system clock, 16,000,000 a second; everything in this guide is timed in ticks (unit: tick)</td></tr><tr><td>1/64 tick</td><td>the sprite timing model's unit of sums: 64 of them make a tick (chapter 7)</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>the CPU's time (unit: tick; counted from power on, t = 0 at the first opcode fetch after the reset sequence, chapter 1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>mikey_t</code></td><td>how far Mikey has been run (unit: tick; counted from power on as <code>t</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>stall</code></td><td>ticks the CPU owes to memory refresh and display DMA, paid before its next access (chapter 1)</td></tr><tr><td>sleep step</td><td>one step of 8 ticks (plus the stall paid in it) while the CPU sleeps (chapter 1, \"Sleep\")</td></tr><tr><td>work <code>w</code>, debt</td><td>the ticks of sprite work a sleep step gives Suzy, and the carried shortfall when <code>w</code> came out below 0 (chapter 1, \"Sleep\")</td></tr><tr><td>part</td><td>one item of the sprite engine's queue of time: a sprite's header, a row, its end, or the list's own 32 ticks (chapter 7, Rule Lynx:7.16)</td></tr><tr><td>exposure, E</td><td>the ticks of work the sprite engine loses to one 28-tick display DMA burst, by the part in progress (chapter 7, Rule Lynx:7.35)</td></tr><tr><td>frame</td><td>the display's frame: from the start of visible line 0 to the next (chapter 4)</td></tr><tr><td>host frame</td><td>one call of <code>lynx_run_frame</code>, completed or not (counted from 1 at power on in chapter 8's boot figures, from 0 in the runner's output)</td></tr><tr><td>bit time</td><td>one UART bit: 8 Timer 4 borrows (chapter 9, Rule Lynx:9.3)</td></tr><tr><td>common time</td><td>a linked console's <code>t</code> plus its offset, the time two consoles on one cable are compared in (chapter 9, Rule Lynx:9.16)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Machine:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>cartridge</td><td>the game's firmware image (a byte array, normally a .lnx file)</td></tr><tr><td>boot firmware</td><td>the console's own 512 bytes at FE00-FFFF (the core never needs it, chapter 8)</td></tr><tr><td>built-in firmware</td><td>the project's own 512-byte boot firmware, which our core runs when no boot firmware is supplied (chapter 8)</td></tr><tr><td>loader frame</td><td>the encrypted block count and blocks at the start of bank 0 that the boot decrypts to 0200 (chapter 8); \"every loader frame\" means each pass through FE4A</td></tr><tr><td>Lynx I, Lynx II</td><td>the original console, and the second model</td></tr><tr><td>the window</td><td>the screen's 160 x 102 pixels: 0 &lt;= x &lt; 160, 0 &lt;= y &lt; 102 (chapter 7)</td></tr><tr><td>source row, destination row</td><td>a row of a sprite's data in RAM, and a row of the screen it is drawn to (chapter 7)</td></tr><tr><td>depository, FRED</td><td>the byte at SCBADR + COLLOFF where a sprite's collision result is written, and the value written there (chapter 7)</td></tr><tr><td>loopback</td><td>a console's receiver hearing its own frames (chapter 9)</td></tr><tr><td>the reference, the oracle</td><td>the Lynx reference emulator (Rule Lynx:0.3)</td></tr><tr><td>the comparison build</td><td><code>build/lynx_refrun</code>, a tools-only build that reproduces the reference's scheduling (chapter 11); not the shipped core</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The CPU, Mikey, the cartridge and sound (chapters 1-6):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>cycle</td><td>one bus access of the CPU: opcode fetch, operand fetch, data read or write, or internal (Rule Lynx:1.4)</td></tr><tr><td>internal cycle</td><td>a dummy access costing 5 ticks that no register sees</td></tr><tr><td>instruction stream (open or closed)</td><td>whether the last access was a fetch; it decides page mode (Rules Lynx:1.6, 1.7)</td></tr><tr><td>page mode</td><td>a fetch costing 4 ticks instead of 5 (Rule Lynx:1.7)</td></tr><tr><td>phase</td><td>Mikey's tick within the 64 us prescaler cycle, 0-1023 (Rule Lynx:4.4)</td></tr><tr><td>slot</td><td><code>phase mod 16</code> below 12, the tick at which a unit is serviced (Rule Lynx:4.4)</td></tr><tr><td>unit</td><td>one of Mikey's twelve counters, timers 0-7 and sound channels 8-11 (Rule Lynx:4.1)</td></tr><tr><td>clock</td><td>a prescaled clock reaching a unit (Rule Lynx:4.6)</td></tr><tr><td>linked</td><td>a unit with clock bits 7, counting the borrows of the unit before it (Rule Lynx:4.2)</td></tr><tr><td>pending borrow</td><td>a borrow received from the linked-before unit, taken at the unit's service (Rule Lynx:4.8)</td></tr><tr><td>terminal borrow</td><td>a borrow into a unit whose COUNT is 0 (Rule Lynx:4.7)</td></tr><tr><td>service</td><td>what a linked unit does at its slot (Rule Lynx:4.8)</td></tr><tr><td>one-shot</td><td>a unit with reload disabled (CTLA bit 4 clear)</td></tr><tr><td>line length, render offset</td><td>Rule Lynx:4.13</td></tr><tr><td>line position</td><td>ticks since the line's start (Rule Lynx:4.14)</td></tr><tr><td>frame flag</td><td>set at the start of visible line 0; the end of <code>run_frame</code> (Rules Lynx:4.14, 1.22)</td></tr><tr><td>burst</td><td>one display DMA fetch of 8 bytes, 16 pens (Rule Lynx:4.17)</td></tr><tr><td>pen buffer</td><td>Mikey's 32 pens in two halves (Rules Lynx:4.16, 4.17)</td></tr><tr><td>REST</td><td>the display's signal seen through IODAT bit 3 (Rules Lynx:4.14, 4.22)</td></tr><tr><td>switched off</td><td>after a SYSCTL1 write with bit 1 clear (Rules Lynx:4.20, 4.21)</td></tr><tr><td>page, counter, strobe, data bit</td><td>the cartridge port's parts (Rule Lynx:5.1)</td></tr><tr><td>bank, page size, second (AUDIN) set</td><td>Rules Lynx:5.2, 5.10</td></tr><tr><td>AUDIN</td><td>IODAT/IODIR bit 4, the cartridge line used for the second set and the EEPROM's DI/DO (Rules Lynx:5.10, 5.16)</td></tr><tr><td>kept RAM</td><td>type-1 LNX2 banks, saved by the host (Rule Lynx:5.25)</td></tr><tr><td>raw image</td><td>a file without the \"LYNX\" header (Rule Lynx:5.6)</td></tr><tr><td>BLL program</td><td>a file with \"BS93\" at bytes 6-9 (Rule Lynx:5.11)</td></tr><tr><td>mix</td><td>the left and right sums of the channels' levels (Rule Lynx:6.4)</td></tr><tr><td>sample phase</td><td>the count advancing by 44,100 a tick against 16,000,000 (Rule Lynx:6.6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-readme--file-names-on-this-tab-are-not-things-you-are-given\">File names on this tab are not things you are given</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:0.5</b> (paths are pointers). Paths under <code>src/</code>, <code>tests/</code>, <code>tools/</code> and <code>docs/incoming/</code> name the project's own source, test programs and records, as evidence for a rule; they are not published with this guide and a builder needs none of them. The same goes for the project's documentation names (<a href=\"#atari2600emu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE.md</code></a>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE-LYNX.md</code></a>, <code>docs/emulation-lynx/...</code>): their text is this tab itself, and <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE-LYNX.md</code></a> is the \"Core interface\" at its end. Source names such as <code>src/rt.c</code>, <code>src/cpu65c02.c</code>, <code>lynxkey.h</code>, <code>boot.c</code> or the generated opcode header are pointers only: the opcode table comes from <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py\"><code>opcodes65c02.py</code></a> (printed on this tab), the modulus <code>lynxkey.h</code> holds is printed in chapter 8. The chapters themselves state every rule, and the host interface (on this tab, after the chapters) gives the C declarations. What a builder cannot get from this tab is a boot firmware image (chapter 8, \"What the host must supply\") and the test cartridges (the download of chapter 11 is a separate file on the site, not part of this tab's text).</p></section>","01-machine":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine\">1. The machine</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: every time is in <b>ticks</b> (one period of the 16 MHz system clock, the glossary's \"tick\"), counted from power on, where <code>t</code> = 0 is the first opcode fetch (Rule Lynx:1.19); register values and addresses are hexadecimal, every other number is decimal.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--parts\">Parts</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.1</b> (the parts). The machine must be built from these parts:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>A 65C02 CPU (chapter 2), inside Mikey.</li><li><b>Mikey</b>: the timers and interrupts, the display (a 160 x 102 LCD fed by DMA from RAM), four sound channels, the UART, the cartridge's address lines, the colour palette (chapters 4, 6, 9).</li><li><b>Suzy</b>: the sprite engine, the maths unit, the buttons, the cartridge's data port (chapter 7, 5).</li><li>64 KB of RAM. The CPU sees Suzy's registers at FC00-FCFF, Mikey's at FD00-FDFF and the boot firmware at FE00-FFFF, each of which MAPCTL can replace by RAM (chapter 3).</li><li>The cartridge, reached through Suzy's data port (chapter 5).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--time\">Time</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.2</b> (one clock). There is one clock: 16,000,000 ticks a second. The core must keep these three counts:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Unit</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>ticks since power on</td><td>the CPU's time</td></tr><tr><td><code>mikey_t</code></td><td>ticks since power on</td><td>how far Mikey has been run</td></tr><tr><td><code>stall</code></td><td>ticks</td><td>what the CPU owes to memory refresh and display DMA, paid before its next access</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.3</b> (Mikey runs lazily). Mikey must be brought up to date at these two points, and the result must be as if it were run one tick at a time (chapter 4 says what a tick does):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Before every CPU access (any address, RAM included, but not an internal cycle), after the access's waits (chapter 3): Mikey is run tick by tick up to the access's time.</li><li>After each instruction: Mikey is run up to <code>t</code>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> This is the model; how fast a core runs it is its own affair. The shipped core skips the ticks at which Mikey changes nothing the rest of the machine or a program can see, and runs them in one step when something needs Mikey's state, with identical results; a core built from this guide may simply run every tick.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--what-a-cpu-access-costs\">What a CPU access costs</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.4</b> (the four kinds of access). Every CPU cycle is a bus access (chapter 2 lists them). Each access is exactly one of: an <b>opcode fetch</b>, an <b>operand fetch</b> (a byte of the instruction stream), a <b>data</b> read or write, or an <b>internal</b> cycle (a dummy access whose value is not used).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.5</b> (an access, step by step). Each access must be made in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Any <code>stall</code> owed is added to <code>t</code>, and <code>stall</code> becomes 0 (capped by Rule Lynx:1.10).</li><li>The access's waits, for Suzy and Mikey registers only, are added to <code>t</code> (chapter 3: Suzy register reads wait 3 ticks, cartridge reads 10; a Mikey timer or sound register access waits for its unit's slot).</li><li>Mikey is run to <code>t</code> (Rule Lynx:1.3, step 1). An internal cycle skips steps 2 and 3: it pays the stall of step 1 and then only its cost.</li><li>The access reaches the hardware at <code>t</code> (it happens at the start of its cost).</li><li><code>t</code> advances by the access's cost (Rule Lynx:1.6).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.6</b> (the cost of each kind, in ticks). The cost must be:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Access</th><th>Cost (ticks)</th><th>Effect on the instruction stream</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>internal cycle</td><td>5</td><td>touches nothing: no register sees it (so a dummy read of a cartridge port does not advance the cartridge counter); a taken branch's internal cycle that goes to another address closes the stream; any other internal cycle leaves it as it is</td></tr><tr><td>opcode or operand fetch, page mode applying (Rule Lynx:1.7)</td><td>4</td><td>opens the stream</td></tr><tr><td>opcode or operand fetch, page mode not applying</td><td>5</td><td>opens the stream</td></tr><tr><td>data read or write</td><td>5</td><td>closes the stream</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> NOT ESTABLISHED for the console that no register sees an internal cycle; MECHANISM that it costs a full cycle.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.7</b> (page mode). A fetch is in page mode only when all three hold: MAPCTL bit 7 is clear, the stream is open, and the fetch address's low four bits are not 0 (the fetch is not at a 16-byte boundary). HARDWARE-OBSERVED.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> HARDWARE-OBSERVED by lynx-tests page-mode and its guide.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--refresh-and-display-dma\">Refresh and display DMA</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.8</b> (refresh). Mikey refreshes RAM every 256 Mikey ticks, counted by <code>refresh</code>, which is cleared at every line start (chapter 4). At each refresh access:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Display state</th><th>Cost to the CPU</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>display DMA off (DISPCTL bit 0 clear)</td><td>4 ticks, added to <code>stall</code></td></tr><tr><td>in vertical blank (DMA on or off)</td><td>4 ticks, added to <code>stall</code></td></tr><tr><td>a visible line with DMA on</td><td>nothing (the display's own fetches refresh the RAM)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE; HARDWARE-OBSERVED in its effect on lynx-tests refresh-rate and page-mode timings.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.9</b> (display bursts). Each display DMA burst made with DMA on must hold the bus for 28 ticks: 28 is added to <code>stall</code>. A burst made with DMA off fetches nothing and costs nothing. A visible line makes ten bursts; line 0 makes eleven (the one-off fetch of the frame's first 16 pens at the line's start, then its ten, chapter 4), so line 0 charges 11 x 28 = 308 ticks.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (the video timing guide measures the effect).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.10</b> (the stall cap). The stall paid at one access is capped at 90000 ticks (a fifth of the frame's cut of Rule Lynx:1.22): <code>t</code> advances by the smaller of <code>stall</code> and 90000, and <code>stall</code> then becomes 0, so any excess is dropped, not kept owing. The same cap applies to each of the two payments of a sleep step (Rule Lynx:1.11). The trace line (chapter 11) adds <code>stall</code> as it stands, uncapped.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> A program that sets the line timer far shorter than a line's display fetch would otherwise grow its wait without end, each payment letting Mikey run on and charge more fetch, so one instruction never ends. This is a guard, not emulation: what the display does with lines shorter than their fetch is NOT ESTABLISHED. Ordinary programs owe tens of ticks.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--sleep\">Sleep</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.11</b> (a sleep step). A write to CPUSLEEP (FD91) puts the CPU to sleep when Rule Lynx:7.15's four conditions hold. While asleep, the machine must advance in steps; each step is, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>t</code> += any <code>stall</code> (capped by Rule Lynx:1.10) + 8; <code>stall</code> = 0.</li><li>Mikey is run to <code>t</code>.</li><li>The <code>stall</code> Mikey added during step 2 is added to <code>t</code> too (capped by Rule Lynx:1.10); <code>stall</code> = 0.</li><li>If an enabled interrupt is now pending (the CPU's IRQ line is active), Suzy gets no work in this step and the CPU wakes: the next step runs an instruction, the interrupt first if I is clear. The step ends here.</li><li>Otherwise Suzy is given work <code>w</code> (Rule Lynx:1.12).</li></ol>\n<p>Suzy finishing wakes the CPU too (chapter 7). Sprite work happens only in sleep steps: the instruction that writes CPUSLEEP gives none of its ticks to the list.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.12</b> (Suzy's work in a step). In step 5 of Rule Lynx:1.11, with <code>refresh</code> and <code>dma</code> the refresh's and the display bursts' stall in the step (in ticks: what was owed before it and what Mikey added during it, uncapped), and E the exposure of the sprite part in progress (chapter 7), <code>w</code> is worked out in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>w = 8</code>.</li><li>If display DMA is on (DISPCTL bit 0): <code>w += floor(refresh x 12 / 28)</code>, then <code>w += trunc(dma x (28 - E) / 28)</code>, a C integer division that rounds toward zero (with E = 30 or 38 the product is negative: for example <code>dma</code> 28 and E 38 give -10).</li><li>If <code>w</code> is below 0, <code>-w</code> is added to a carried <b>debt</b> and Suzy gets nothing this step. Otherwise the debt is paid from <code>w</code> first (as much as <code>w</code> covers) and Suzy gets what is left.</li></ol>\n<p>Power on clears the debt; SPRGO does not.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Suzy goes on with work in its buffers while the display holds the bus, and a burst can cost it more than its 28 ticks (a grant handshake); the debt carries that loss into the next steps.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--power-on\">Power on</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.13</b> (the model). At power on the model (Lynx I or II) is chosen.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.14</b> (RAM). RAM is all 00. NOT ESTABLISHED.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The console's RAM holds whatever the chips power up with.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.15</b> (the firmware). The boot firmware is the user's own if supplied, else the project's own (chapter 8).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.16</b> (registers and display state). MAPCTL is 00. Mikey's and Suzy's registers are all 0 (so every timer is stopped), except that the UART reports TXRDY and TXEMPTY. The display state is: outside vertical blank, on line 0, no pixels drawn, <code>pix_next</code> and <code>dma_next</code> 0. NOT ESTABLISHED; CONVERGENCE.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.17</b> (the cartridge). The cartridge's shift register, counter, strobe and data bit are 0.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.18</b> (the CPU). The CPU starts with A, X, Y 0, S 00, P 20; then it makes its reset sequence (chapter 2): seven cycles, S ending at FD, I set, D clear, PC from FFFC-FFFD (the firmware, as MAPCTL is 0).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.19</b> (time zero). After the reset sequence, <code>t</code>, <code>mikey_t</code>, <code>stall</code>, Mikey's prescaler phase and the refresh count are set to 0, the sound output restarts (no partial sample, sample phase 0, the output buffer empty) and the stream is closed. So <code>t</code> = 0 is the first opcode fetch; the reset sequence's cycles are not timed. Nothing else of the reset sequence's Mikey run is undone (Rule Lynx:1.20).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.20</b> (what the reset sequence leaves in the display). The reset sequence's two vector reads, at 25 and 30 ticks (5 ticks a cycle), run Mikey the ordinary way for 30 ticks before Rule Lynx:1.19 sets the counts to 0. The display therefore stands at power on, from <code>pix_next</code> and <code>dma_next</code> 0, 30 ticks along line 0:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>What</th><th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>pixels drawn</td><td>three, at ticks 0, 12 and 24 of the line, pen 0 from the empty buffer, colour 0</td></tr><tr><td>bursts made</td><td>one, into the pen buffer's first half (offset 0); with DMA off it fills that half with pen 0 and costs nothing</td></tr><tr><td>next burst</td><td>due at tick 192 of the line, into the second half</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>NOT ESTABLISHED.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> NOT ESTABLISHED for the console (the relative phase of the CPU and Mikey at power on); the choice matches the reference's patched start (chapter 11).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-01-machine--the-run-loop\">The run loop</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.21</b> (<code>step_instruction</code>). One step must be, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If asleep: one 8-tick sleep step (Rule Lynx:1.11), and the step ends.</li><li>Otherwise the CPU runs one instruction (or takes a pending interrupt). The instruction's interrupt poll (chapter 2) happens inside this CPU step, so it sees the IRQ line as Mikey left it at the instruction's last access, before step 3's run.</li><li>Mikey is run to <code>t</code>.</li><li>Suzy's maths unit is given the instruction's ticks, as Rule Lynx:7.22 says.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:1.22</b> (<code>run_frame</code>). One call must be, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Clear the frame flag.</li><li>Before each step, test: stop if the display has started visible line 0 (the flag, chapter 4) or if at least 450,000 ticks have passed since the call began; otherwise make one step (Rule Lynx:1.21) and test again.</li><li>Count the frame either way (<code>lynx_frame_count</code> counts calls of <code>run_frame</code>).</li></ol>\n<p>A frame therefore ends at the start of line 0's fetch; the picture returned is the one completed then.</p></section>","02-cpu":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-02-cpu-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-02-cpu\">2. The CPU</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: a <b>cycle</b> is one bus access of the CPU, whose cost in ticks is chapter 1's (Rule Lynx:1.6); the tables list cycles in order from the instruction's opcode fetch. Opcodes, addresses, flag masks and byte values are hexadecimal; cycle counts and bit numbers are decimal.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.1</b> (the core and its opcode table). The Lynx's CPU is a CMOS 65C02 core. Its opcode table (both models) is generated by <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py\"><code>opcodes65c02.py</code></a> in this folder: mnemonic, addressing mode and kind (read, write, read-modify-write, other) for all 256 opcodes. This chapter gives what the table does not: every cycle's bus access, the flags, and the interrupt poll.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM throughout, checked cycle by cycle against the SingleStepTests 65x02 Rockwell 65C02 set (every file clean except the Lynx's no-ops, below) and Klaus Dormann's 6502 functional and 65C02 extended-opcode tests.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-02-cpu--the-two-models\">The two models</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.2</b> (the instruction set of each model).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Instruction set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Lynx II</b></td><td>the Rockwell 65C02 instruction set, except that CB and DB are one-byte, one-cycle no-ops like the rest of columns 3 and B</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lynx I</b></td><td>the same, and also every opcode in columns 7 and F (RMB, SMB, BBR, BBS) is a one-byte, one-cycle no-op</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Lynx II: HARDWARE-OBSERVED (lynx-tests cpu, test 8). Lynx I: HARDWARE-OBSERVED that they do nothing (lynx-tests cpu, test 7); their length is CONVERGENCE.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.3</b> (one-byte no-ops). A one-byte no-op (mode <code>nop1</code>) is the opcode fetch alone, and no interrupt poll follows it: an interrupt pending across one is taken after the next instruction. HARDWARE-OBSERVED.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> HARDWARE-OBSERVED (lynx-tests cpu, test 8).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-02-cpu--registers-and-flags\">Registers and flags</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.4</b> (registers and P). The registers are A, X, Y, S (the stack at 0100-01FF: a push writes 01S then decrements S, a pull increments S then reads), PC, P. P's bits: 7 N, 6 V, 5 (always 1), 4 B, 3 D, 2 I, 1 Z, 0 C. P bit 5 always reads 1; B exists only in pushed copies (1 from BRK and PHP, 0 from an interrupt); PLP and RTI load P from the stack with B cleared and bit 5 set.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.5</b> (what the part shares with the NMOS 6502). MECHANISM:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>N and Z: every instruction that loads or changes a register or a memory byte (LDA, LDX, LDY, TAX, TAY, TXA, TYA, TSX, INX, INY, DEX, DEY, PLA, PLX, PLY, ORA, AND, EOR, ADC, SBC, ASL, LSR, ROL, ROR, INC, DEC, INC A, DEC A) sets N to bit 7 of the result and Z to \"result is 0\". TXS, the stores, STZ, the pushes, branches and jumps change no flag.</li><li>Binary ADC: <code>r = A + v + C</code>; C = <code>r &gt; FF</code>; V = <code>~(A ^ v) &amp; (A ^ r) &amp; 80</code> is not 0; A = r's low byte. Binary SBC: <code>r = A - v - (1 - C)</code>; C = no borrow (<code>r &gt;= 0</code>); V = <code>(A ^ v) &amp; (A ^ r) &amp; 80</code> is not 0; A = r's low byte.</li><li>CMP, CPX, CPY: C = <code>reg &gt;= v</code> (unsigned), N and Z from <code>reg - v</code> (8 bits); the register is not changed.</li><li>BIT (not immediate): N = bit 7 and V = bit 6 of the memory byte, Z = <code>(A AND byte) = 0</code>.</li><li>ASL and ROL: C = the old bit 7; LSR and ROR: C = the old bit 0; ROL and ROR shift the old C in.</li><li>CLC, SEC, CLI, SEI, CLV, CLD, SED clear or set their flag.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.6</b> (differences from the NMOS 6502).</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>Decimal ADC and SBC set N and Z from the decimal result and take one extra cycle (Rule Lynx:2.8); C and V as the NMOS part forms them. ADC in decimal, in this order:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>low nibble <code>lo = (A &amp; F) + (v &amp; F) + C</code>;</li><li>if <code>lo &gt; 9</code> then <code>lo = ((lo + 6) &amp; F) + $10</code>;</li><li><code>r = (A &amp; F0) + (v &amp; F0) + lo</code>;</li><li>V from <code>~(A ^ v) &amp; (A ^ r) &amp; 80</code>;</li><li>if <code>r &gt;= A0</code> then <code>r += 60</code>;</li><li>C = <code>r &gt;= $100</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>SBC in decimal, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>C and V from the binary subtraction;</li><li><code>lo = (A &amp; F) - (v &amp; F) - borrow</code>, <code>r = A - v - borrow</code>;</li><li>if <code>r &lt; 0</code> then <code>r -= 60</code>;</li><li>if <code>lo &lt; 0</code> then <code>r -= 06</code>;</li><li>A = r's low byte.</li></ol></li><li>BIT #imm sets only Z.</li><li>TSB and TRB set Z from <code>A AND old</code>, then write <code>old OR A</code> or <code>old AND NOT A</code>.</li><li>BRK and interrupts clear D.</li><li>There are no undocumented operations: every unused opcode is a no-op of a fixed length and time (the table's modes).</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-02-cpu--cycles\">Cycles</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.7</b> (how the cycle tables read). Each line is one cycle, one bus access, in order. Access kinds (chapter 1's costs, Rule Lynx:1.6): <b>O</b> opcode fetch, <b>F</b> operand fetch (PC then advances), <b>R</b> data read, <b>W</b> data write, <b>I</b> internal (a dummy access of the address shown; costs time only). <code>pc</code> is the address after the bytes fetched so far. Every instruction starts with O at PC.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.8</b> (cycles by addressing mode, after the opcode fetch).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>After the opcode</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>imp, acc</td><td>I pc</td></tr><tr><td>imm</td><td>F; decimal ADC adds I 0059, decimal SBC I 0000</td></tr><tr><td>zp</td><td>F zp; then the kind's accesses at zp</td></tr><tr><td>zpx, zpy</td><td>F b; I b; then at <code>(b + X or Y) &amp; FF</code></td></tr><tr><td>abs</td><td>F lo, F hi; then at the address. The no-ops DC and FC: I pc-1 instead</td></tr><tr><td>absx, absy</td><td>F lo, F hi. Read: I pc-1 only if the index crosses a page. Write: always I pc-1. Read-modify-write: I pc-1 if the page is crossed, or always for INC and DEC. Then the kind's accesses at base + index</td></tr><tr><td>indx</td><td>F b; I b; R <code>(b+X)&amp;FF</code>, R <code>(b+X+1)&amp;FF</code>; then at that address</td></tr><tr><td>indy</td><td>F p; R p, R <code>(p+1)&amp;FF</code>; for a read, I pc-1 only if adding Y crosses a page; for a write or read-modify-write, always I pc-1; then at base + Y</td></tr><tr><td>izp</td><td>F p; R p, R <code>(p+1)&amp;FF</code>; then at that address</td></tr><tr><td>rel</td><td>F offset. If taken: I pc (an internal cycle that closes the stream when the offset is not 0); if the target is on another page, also I of <code>(pc &amp; FF00) | (target &amp; FF)</code></td></tr><tr><td>zpr (BBR, BBS)</td><td>F zp; R zp; I zp; F offset; if taken, as rel, except that a page crossing's extra cycle is I pc</td></tr><tr><td>ind (JMP (abs))</td><td>F lo, F hi; R p; I <code>(p &amp; FF00) | ((p+1) &amp; FF)</code>; R p+1</td></tr><tr><td>iax (JMP (abs,X))</td><td>F lo, F hi; I pc-2; R p, R p+1 (p = address + X)</td></tr><tr><td>nop1</td><td>nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The kind's accesses at the effective address: <b>read</b> R (decimal ADC and SBC add I at the same address); <b>write</b> W; <b>read-modify-write</b> R, I at the address, W.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.9</b> (special instructions and sequences).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instruction</th><th>Cycles after the opcode</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BRK</td><td>F (the padding byte); W 01S PCH, W PCL, W P with B set; R FFFE, R FFFF</td></tr><tr><td>JSR</td><td>F lo; I 01S; W 01S PCH, W PCL (of the address of the high byte); F hi</td></tr><tr><td>RTI</td><td>I pc; I 01S; R P, R PCL, R PCH (S incremented before each)</td></tr><tr><td>RTS</td><td>I pc; I 01S; R PCL, R PCH; I pc; then PC + 1</td></tr><tr><td>PHA, PHX, PHY, PHP</td><td>I pc; W 01S (PHP pushes B set)</td></tr><tr><td>PLA, PLX, PLY, PLP</td><td>I pc; I 01S; R 01S+1</td></tr><tr><td>JMP abs</td><td>F lo; F hi</td></tr><tr><td>5C</td><td>F, F (three bytes); I pc-1</td></tr><tr><td>interrupt entry</td><td>I pc; I pc; W PCH, W PCL, W P (B clear); I set, D clear; R vector, R vector+1</td></tr><tr><td>reset</td><td>I pc; I pc; I 01S three times (S decremented each); I set, D clear; R FFFC, R FFFD</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.10</b> (vectors). The IRQ vector is FFFE; NMI (unused on the Lynx) FFFA.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-02-cpu--the-interrupt-poll\">The interrupt poll</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.11</b> (the poll). At the end of each instruction (not a <code>nop1</code>) the CPU polls: if the IRQ line is active and I is clear, the next step enters the interrupt instead of fetching an opcode. The I tested is:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instruction just run</th><th>I tested</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CLI, SEI, PLP (they change I too late to affect this instruction's poll)</td><td>I as it was before the instruction</td></tr><tr><td>every other instruction (RTI therefore polls with the restored I)</td><td>I as it is after the instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM; HARDWARE-OBSERVED for CLI on the Lynx (lynx-tests cpu, test 1).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.12</b> (no poll after an entry). An interrupt entry is a step of its own, not an instruction: it makes no poll at its end (I is set by it in any case), and the handler's first instruction is fetched in the next step and polls at its own end as usual.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:2.13</b> (the IRQ line). The IRQ line is Mikey's: active while any interrupt bit is both pending and enabled (chapter 4). It is sampled as it stands at the poll.</p></section>","03-memory":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-03-memory-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-03-memory\">3. Memory map</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: addresses and register values are hexadecimal; waits are in ticks (chapter 1), added to <code>t</code> at the point Rule Lynx:1.5 gives; unit and phase numbers are decimal.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-03-memory--decode\">Decode</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.1</b> (the CPU's 64 KB). Each address must decode so:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Addresses</th><th>Reads</th><th>Writes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0000-FBFF</td><td>RAM</td><td>RAM</td></tr><tr><td>FC00-FCFF</td><td>Suzy (chapter 7), or RAM if MAPCTL bit 0 is set</td><td>the same</td></tr><tr><td>FD00-FDFF</td><td>Mikey (chapters 4, 6, 9), or RAM if MAPCTL bit 1</td><td>the same</td></tr><tr><td>FE00-FFF7</td><td>the boot firmware (byte <code>a - FE00</code>), or RAM if MAPCTL bit 2</td><td>RAM</td></tr><tr><td>FFF8</td><td>RAM</td><td>RAM</td></tr><tr><td>FFF9</td><td>MAPCTL</td><td>MAPCTL</td></tr><tr><td>FFFA-FFFF</td><td>the firmware's last six bytes, or RAM if MAPCTL bit 3</td><td>RAM</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Writes to the firmware's addresses always land in the RAM beneath. MAPCTL bit 7 turns page mode off (chapter 1, Rule Lynx:1.7); bits 6-4 have no effect here.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM (the documented MAPCTL).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.2</b> (reading MAPCTL).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>A read of FFF9 returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lynx I</td><td>the register</td></tr><tr><td>Lynx II</td><td><code>(MAPCTL OR 70) XOR 80</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (lynx-tests memio's values).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-03-memory--waits-before-an-access\">Waits before an access</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.3</b> (Suzy's wait). Before a read of Suzy's window (when it is Suzy, not RAM): 3 ticks, or 10 for the cartridge ports FCB2 and FCB3 (3 plus 7). Writes to Suzy do not wait.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (the reference's figures; lynx-tests memio and the sprite tests agree).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.4</b> (Mikey's wait). Before any access to a Mikey timer register (FD00-FD1F, units 0-7: unit <code>(a &gt;&gt; 2) &amp; 7</code>) or sound register (FD20-FD3F, units 8-11: <code>8 + ((a - FD20) &gt;&gt; 3)</code>), the access waits until Mikey's phase (chapter 4) reaches the unit's slot: the wait is <code>(u - phase_at_the_access) mod 16</code> ticks, where the phase at the access is Mikey's phase plus the ticks from <code>mikey_t</code> to the access. These do not wait:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Access</th><th>Wait</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>any other Mikey register</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>any address of FD00-FDFF while MAPCTL bit 1 makes it RAM</td><td>none (as for Suzy's window, the wait belongs to Mikey, not to the address)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE; HARDWARE-OBSERVED in its effect on lynx-tests timers and timers2.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.5</b> (when the waits are paid). The waits are added to <code>t</code> before Mikey is run to the access and before the access's own 4 or 5 ticks (the order of Rule Lynx:1.5).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.6</b> (waits by address, not by kind). These waits go by the address and the direction, not the kind of access: an opcode or operand fetch from Suzy's or Mikey's window (code running in FCxx or FDxx with that window mapped in) waits exactly as a data read of the same address does, and has the same side effects. Only internal cycles (chapter 1) wait for nothing and touch nothing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-03-memory--suzys-and-mikeys-unused-addresses\">Suzy's and Mikey's unused addresses</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.7</b> (Suzy's unused addresses). Reads of FC30-FC3F, FC70-FC7F and unlisted Suzy registers give FF; writes to them are ignored.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:3.8</b> (Mikey's readable registers and the rest).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mikey address or register</th><th>A read gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the timer and sound registers</td><td>as stored</td></tr><tr><td>ATTEN, MPAN and MSTEREO</td><td>as stored on the Lynx II; FD on the Lynx I</td></tr><tr><td>the palette: GREEN</td><td>its stored 4 bits in bits 3-0, bits 7-4 reading 0</td></tr><tr><td>the palette: BLUERED</td><td>its byte</td></tr><tr><td>INTRST and INTSET</td><td>the pending bits</td></tr><tr><td>IODAT</td><td>chapter 4</td></tr><tr><td>SERCTL and SERDAT</td><td>chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>FD84, FD85</td><td>00</td></tr><tr><td>FD86</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>FD87 (SYSCTL1, write-only)</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>FD88 (the hardware revision)</td><td>01 on the Lynx I, 02 on the Lynx II</td></tr><tr><td>FD98</td><td>FD</td></tr><tr><td>everything else, including the write-only DISPCTL, PBKUP, DISPADR, IODIR, SDONEACK and CPUSLEEP</td><td>FF</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Writes to unlisted registers are ignored.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (lynx-tests memio).</p></div></section>","04-mikey":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey\">4. Mikey: timers, interrupts, display</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: times are in ticks (chapter 1); <code>phase</code> counts ticks within the 64 us prescaler cycle (0-1023, from power on, Rule Lynx:1.19); the line position counts ticks from the line's start (timer 0's terminal borrow); <code>c</code> and <code>b</code> are timer 2's COUNT and BACKUP in lines. Addresses, register values and bit masks are hexadecimal; every other number is decimal.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--units\">Units</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.1</b> (the twelve units). Mikey has twelve counting units: timers 0-7 and the four sound channels as units 8-11. Each has BACKUP (the reload value), CTLA, COUNT and CTLB, and a count of <b>pending</b> borrows received from the unit linked before it.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>BACKUP</th><th>CTLA</th><th>COUNT</th><th>CTLB</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>timer n (0-7)</td><td>FD00 + 4n</td><td>+1</td><td>+2</td><td>+3</td></tr><tr><td>sound channel c (unit 8 + c)</td><td>FD24 + 8c</td><td>FD25 + 8c</td><td>FD26 + 8c</td><td>FD27 + 8c</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.2</b> (CTLA and CTLB bits).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Register</th><th>Bit</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>7</td><td>interrupt enable (timers; for a sound channel, a feedback tap, chapter 6)</td></tr><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>6</td><td>RESET_DONE</td></tr><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>5</td><td>(sound: integrate)</td></tr><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>4</td><td>reload enable</td></tr><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>3</td><td>count enable</td></tr><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>2-0</td><td>the clock: 0-6 select 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64 us; 7 means <b>linked</b> (counts the borrows of the unit before it)</td></tr><tr><td>CTLB</td><td>3</td><td>DONE</td></tr><tr><td>CTLB</td><td>2</td><td>last clock</td></tr><tr><td>CTLB</td><td>1</td><td>borrow in</td></tr><tr><td>CTLB</td><td>0</td><td>borrow out</td></tr><tr><td>CTLB (sound channel)</td><td>7-4</td><td>its shift register's top bits (chapter 6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.3</b> (the links). The links: 0 → 2 → 4; 1 → 3 → 5 → 7 → sound 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → timer 1. Timer 4 and timer 6 link to nothing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--the-phase-and-the-tick\">The phase and the tick</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.4</b> (one Mikey tick). Mikey keeps <code>phase</code>, 0-1023: ticks within the 64 us prescaler cycle. Each Mikey tick must be, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>mikey_t</code> += 1.</li><li>Sound: the mix is added to the sample being formed, and the sample point is tested (chapter 6, Rule Lynx:6.6).</li><li>Display: the line position advances by 1 and any pixels and DMA bursts now due are done (Rule Lynx:4.15).</li><li>Refresh: <code>refresh</code> += 1; at 256 it returns to 0 and, if DMA is off or in vertical blank, the CPU owes 4 ticks (chapter 1, Rule Lynx:1.8).</li><li><code>phase</code> = (phase + 1) mod 1024.</li><li>The prescaled clocks (Rule Lynx:4.5). Each unit 0-11 in order whose CTLA has count enable and a clock 0-6 that fired receives a <b>clock</b> (Rule Lynx:4.6).</li><li>The slot: if <code>slot = phase mod 16</code> is below 12, unit <code>slot</code>'s CTLB bits 1-0 clear, and the unit is <b>serviced</b> (Rule Lynx:4.8).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (the reference's author fitted these phases to the hardware tests); checked by lynx-tests timers and timers2.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.5</b> (which prescaled clocks fire). With <code>phase</code> the value after step 5 of Rule Lynx:4.4:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th><code>phase mod 16</code></th><th>Clock</th><th>Fires when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1 us</td><td>always</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2 us</td><td><code>phase mod 32 = 17</code></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>64 us</td><td><code>phase = 897</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>4 us</td><td><code>phase mod 64 = 56</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>8 us</td><td><code>phase mod 128 = 120</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>16 us</td><td><code>phase mod 256 = 120</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>32 us</td><td><code>phase mod 512 = 376</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--clock-borrow-service\">Clock, borrow, service</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.6</b> (a clock). A prescaled clock reaching unit u, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Clear CTLB bits 1-0.</li><li>If RESET_DONE (CTLA bit 6) is set, clear DONE (RESET_DONE acts as a level).</li><li>If the unit is a one-shot (CTLA bit 4 clear) and DONE is set, stop here.</li><li>Otherwise set borrow in (bit 1) and <b>borrow into</b> the unit (Rule Lynx:4.7).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.7</b> (borrow into unit u).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>COUNT before</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>above 0</td><td>COUNT -= 1 and, for a prescaled unit, clear CTLB bit 2; done</td></tr><tr><td>0 (a terminal borrow)</td><td>the steps below, in order</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A terminal borrow:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Set CTLB borrow out and DONE (bits 0 and 3), and bit 2 for a prescaled unit.</li><li>The linked unit, if any, gets one more pending borrow and its CTLB borrow in (bit 1) set.</li><li>If reload is enabled, COUNT = BACKUP.</li><li>A timer: if its interrupt is enabled and it is not timer 4, its interrupt becomes pending; timer 0 starts a display line (Rule Lynx:4.14); timer 4 clocks the UART (chapter 9). A sound channel: its shift register steps (chapter 6).</li></ol>\n<p>The borrow reports whether the unit is still counting: a one-shot unit with DONE set has stopped.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.8</b> (service of unit u at its slot). Only a linked unit (clock 7) with count enable is serviced. Let <code>reset_done</code> = RESET_DONE and DONE both set; <code>blocked</code> = a one-shot with DONE set and not <code>reset_done</code>. In this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If neither <code>reset_done</code> nor (pending &gt; 0 and not <code>blocked</code>) holds, nothing happens; stop.</li><li>(CTLB bits 1-0 are already clear: Rule Lynx:4.4 step 7 clears them just before every service.)</li><li>If RESET_DONE, clear DONE.</li><li>If it is a one-shot with DONE still set, stop.</li><li>Take all the pending borrows (pending = 0); if there were any, set borrow in.</li><li>Borrow into the unit (Rule Lynx:4.7) once for each borrow taken, stopping if it stops.</li></ol>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--timer-register-writes\">Timer register writes</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.9</b> (writes to a unit).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Register written</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BACKUP</td><td>stored. Timer 0's line length is recomputed (Rule Lynx:4.13)</td></tr><tr><td>CTLA</td><td>stored. If the clock bits changed, or count enable went from 0 to 1, the pending borrows are dropped (and for timer 0 the line length is recomputed). For timers other than 4, bit 7 sets the timer's interrupt enable. If bit 6 is set, DONE clears at once</td></tr><tr><td>COUNT</td><td>stored</td></tr><tr><td>CTLB</td><td>if the write sets bit 1 while it was clear, the unit borrows once (Rule Lynx:4.7). Then CTLB becomes the written value's bit 3 (a timer) or bits 7-3 (a sound channel)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every write to a unit ends by updating the IRQ line.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--interrupts\">Interrupts</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.10</b> (INTSET and INTRST). INTSET (FD81) reads the pending bits, and writing ones sets them; INTRST (FD80) reads the same, and writing ones clears them, after which the UART's level may set bit 4 again (chapter 9).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.11</b> (the IRQ line). The enable bits are the timers' CTLA bit 7 (bit n for timer n) and, for bit 4, the UART's interrupt enables (SERCTL bits 7 or 6). The IRQ line is active while <code>pending AND enable</code> is not 0. The line is updated after every Mikey register write and after every run of Mikey.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--the-display\">The display</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.12</b> (display registers and colours). Registers: DISPCTL (FD92, bit 0: DMA on, bit 1: flip), PBKUP (FD93, stored), DISPADR (FD94 low, FD95 high), GREEN0-F (FDA0-FDAF, 4 bits), BLUERED0-F (FDB0-FDBF: blue in bits 7-4, red in bits 3-0). A pen's colour is <code>(GREEN &lt;&lt; 8) | BLUERED</code>: green 11-8, blue 7-4, red 3-0.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.13</b> (line length and render offset). Timer 0 is the line timer: its <b>line length</b> is <code>(BACKUP + 1) x period</code> ticks, the period being 16 &lt;&lt; clock (so 16, 32, ..., 1024 ticks; linked: 0), at most 65535. The <b>render offset</b> is the line length less 1920 (0 if the line is shorter), in ticks from the line's start. Timer 2 counts lines.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.14</b> (line start: timer 0's terminal borrow). In this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If not in vertical blank, the rest of the current line's 160 pixels are output.</li><li><code>refresh</code> = 0.</li><li>With <code>c</code> = timer 2's COUNT and <code>b</code> its BACKUP (timer 2 has not yet counted this borrow: it does so at its slot), and <code>first</code> = 102 if <code>b &gt;= 104</code>, else <code>b - 2</code>, the first row of this table that applies is done:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>c = 0</code></td><td>vertical blank begins; the REST signal (IODAT bit 3's input, Rule Lynx:4.22) becomes 1. If <code>b &lt; 104</code>, the picture's rows from <code>b - 2</code> (0 if <code>b &lt;= 2</code>) to 101 are cleared to 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>c = b</code></td><td>REST becomes 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>c = b - 1</code></td><td>DISPADR, with its low two bits cleared, plus 3 if DISPCTL bit 1 (flip) is set, is latched as the frame's start address</td></tr><tr><td><code>1 &lt;= c &lt;= first</code></td><td>a visible line, <code>line = first - c</code>. On line 0 the display fetches the frame's first 16 pens at once (a DMA burst, Rule Lynx:4.17, from the latched address, into the buffer's first half), leaves vertical blank and sets the <b>frame flag</b> (the end of <code>run_frame</code>). On line 1, REST becomes 1. Then: bursts done this line = 0, the next burst goes into the buffer's second half, due at <code>render_offset + 12</code>; the next pixel is due at <code>render_offset</code>; pixels drawn = 0; the pen buffer's read position 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\" start=\"4\"><li>The line position restarts at 0.</li></ol>\n<p>REST is 0 from power on until the first line start that sets it to 1.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.15</b> (the line). Each tick, outside vertical blank, until 160 pixels and 10 bursts are done, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>While fewer than 160 pixels are drawn and the next is due (<code>position &gt;= pix_next</code>), draw one (Rule Lynx:4.16) and add 12 to <code>pix_next</code>.</li><li>While fewer than 10 bursts are done and the next is due (<code>position &gt;= dma_next</code>), do one (Rule Lynx:4.17) and add 192 to <code>dma_next</code>.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.16</b> (a pixel). Take the pen at the pen buffer's read position (read position advances, modulo 32) and, if the line is below 102 and the pixel below 160, store its colour (as the palette stands now) in the picture. A palette write therefore shows from the pixel being drawn as it lands.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE with the reference to the pixel (tests/programs-lynx/palrace, gate 8, which walks a write through lines 0 to 2 a few pixels a frame).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.17</b> (a burst).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>DISPCTL when the burst is made</th><th>What the burst does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bit 0 (DMA) set, bit 1 (flip) clear</td><td>read 8 bytes from the DMA address upwards into the pen buffer's current half (16 pens, each byte's high nibble first) and charge the CPU 28 ticks (chapter 1, Rule Lynx:1.9)</td></tr><tr><td>bit 0 set, bit 1 set (flipped)</td><td>read the 8 bytes downwards from the DMA address (the address, the address less 1, ...), each byte's low nibble first, and charge the CPU 28 ticks</td></tr><tr><td>bit 0 (DMA) clear</td><td>the half becomes pen 0, and it costs nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In every case the DMA address then advances by 8 (goes back by 8 when flipped) and the half alternates, so a program that turns DMA on part-way through a frame is read from where the address would have been.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.18</b> (the picture buffer). The picture is one buffer of 160 x 102 colours, written pixel by pixel as the display draws them; there is no second copy. At line 0's start (the frame flag) line 101's remaining pixels have just been drawn, so the buffer then holds the whole frame just finished, and that is what <code>lynx_raster</code> shows when <code>lynx_run_frame</code> returns; read during a frame, it holds the new frame's lines above the beam and the old one's below.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.19</b> (the flip). With DISPCTL bit 1 set, the picture is turned half a turn, its first pixel being the last byte's low pen. Nothing else in Mikey or Suzy turns: the sprite engine draws into the buffer as before, and the directions of the joystick are exchanged by Suzy's own left-hand bit (SPRSYS bit 3, chapter 7). A flip changed in the middle of a frame takes effect at the next burst.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The flip is so the console can be held upside down (for left-handed play). A program that flips sets DISPADR to its buffer's last byte (buffer + 8159; with a buffer on a 4-byte boundary the latch's \"clear the low two bits, add 3\" gives exactly that byte), and sets the left-hand bit with the flip. The address rule and the pen order are CONVERGENCE with the second Lynx emulator's reading of the console's documentation (read, not run: the second Lynx emulator is not an instrument here, and the oracle, the Lynx reference emulator, ignores the bit, so no picture can be compared with it); no hardware measurement is known (NOT ESTABLISHED on the console, as is a flip changed in the middle of a frame). Checked by <code>tests/lynx/homebrew_checks.c</code>: a flipped frame equals the upright one turned half a turn, pixel for pixel.</p></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE for the timing (the reference, and the lynx-tests video timing guide's measurements); MECHANISM for the rest (the documented display).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-04-mikey--other-mikey-registers\">Other Mikey registers</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.20</b> (SYSCTL1, FD87). Bit 0 is the cartridge's address strobe (chapter 5). Bit 1 holds the power on: a write with it clear switches the console off, taking effect at the write. In this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The write (the last bus cycle of any instruction that can make it: a store, or a read-modify-write's final write, so the instruction ends with it) clears the picture to 0.</li><li>Mikey is brought to the instruction's end as after any instruction (Rule Lynx:1.21, step 3): the few ticks of that last cycle run normally, display included.</li><li>Every later step is a switched-off step (Rule Lynx:4.21), until the next power on: nothing runs, the sound silent, time still passing for the host.</li></ol>\n<p>Every write to FD87 acts on bit 1, whatever else it is for: SYSCTL1 powers on 0 (it reads 80 whatever was written), but only a write with bit 1 clear switches the console off, so every write, the cartridge strobes included, must keep bit 1 set. Firmware and loaders strobe with 03 (strobe high) then 02 (strobe low); a strobe written as 01 then 00 switches the console off at the 00.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM: Atari's description of the register; both references act on it, the Lynx reference emulator stopping at the end of that frame, the second Lynx emulator resetting at the write. How long the console's supply takes to fall is NOT ESTABLISHED.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.21</b> (switched off). Switched off, the core runs no CPU, no timers, no display and no Suzy: each step adds 1024 ticks to <code>t</code> and makes silent sound samples (the output's rate is kept). <code>lynx_run_frame</code> then never sees a frame start, so each call runs such steps until Rule Lynx:1.22's cut (440 steps, 450,560 ticks), draws nothing (the picture stays all 0), returns 0 and counts the frame.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.22</b> (IODIR and IODAT). IODIR (FD8A): stored. IODAT (FD8B): writing stores it, and bit 1 is the cartridge's address data (chapter 5); bit 4 drives AUDIN when IODIR bit 4 is set (chapter 5). A read of IODAT gives, bit by bit:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>IODAT bit</th><th>IODIR bit set (an output)</th><th>IODIR bit clear (an input)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>the written value</td><td>1 (external power present)</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>the written value</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>the written value</td><td>1 (no expansion cable; 0 while a second console is on the ComLynx cable, chapter 9)</td></tr><tr><td>3 (REST)</td><td>the written bit AND REST (1 only when both are 1)</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>the written value</td><td>1 (AUDIN pulled high, or the cartridge EEPROM's DO while it drives it, chapter 5)</td></tr><tr><td>7-5</td><td>0 (not read back even as outputs)</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Bit 3 is the one exception to \"written value where an output\": REST is seen only through an output set to 1.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> This is what the core does on purpose, not a swap (CONVERGENCE; the console's own behaviour is NOT ESTABLISHED). CONVERGENCE for the rest.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:4.23</b> (the other registers). SDONEACK (FD90), CPUSLEEP (FD91): chapter 7. SERCTL (FD8C), SERDAT (FD8D): chapter 9. Sound: chapter 6.</p></section>","05-cartridge":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge\">5. The cartridge</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: header offsets, register values, addresses and bytes are hexadecimal where written as such (80 08, FCB2, 0C) and decimal in the header tables' \"Bytes\" column (0-63); sizes are in bytes (KB = 1024 bytes); the counter counts accesses of RCART0 and RCART1 from the last strobe; times are in ticks (chapter 1).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--the-port\">The port</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.1</b> (the two parts Mikey drives). The cartridge is addressed by two parts Mikey drives, and read through two Suzy ports:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Width</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>page</b> shift register</td><td>8 bits</td><td>on a rising edge of the address strobe (SYSCTL1, FD87, bit 0 going from 0 to 1) it shifts left by one, taking the <b>data bit</b> (IODAT, FD8B, bit 1, as last written) into bit 0</td></tr><tr><td><b>counter</b></td><td>11 bits</td><td>0 while the strobe is high. While the strobe is low, every read of RCART0 (FCB2) or RCART1 (FCB3), and every write to either, advances it by one (modulo 2048) after the access</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM (the cartridge's counter and shift register).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.2</b> (which byte a port reads). Bank 0 is read at FCB2, bank 1 at FCB3. A bank with page size <code>s</code> (a power of two up to 2048 bytes; commercial cartridges use 256 to 2048) holds 256 pages; the byte read is the bank's byte <code>(page &lt;&lt; log2(s)) | (counter AND (s - 1))</code>, or FF if that is past the bank's size or the bank has no page size.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.3</b> (writes to the port). A write to FCB2 or FCB3 stores the byte only in a bank of cartridge RAM (\"Cartridge RAM\" below: an LNX2 file's RAM banks, or RAM the host adds); elsewhere it changes nothing. Either way it clocks the counter, which is how a program drives a cartridge EEPROM (\"The cartridge EEPROM\" below).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--the-lnx-file\">The .lnx file</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.4</b> (the .lnx header). A 64-byte header, then the image:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-3</td><td>\"LYNX\"</td></tr><tr><td>4-5</td><td>bank 0 page size, little endian</td></tr><tr><td>6-7</td><td>bank 1 page size (0: no bank 1)</td></tr><tr><td>8-9</td><td>version, little endian: must be 1 (Rule Lynx:5.8)</td></tr><tr><td>10-41</td><td>name</td></tr><tr><td>42-57</td><td>manufacturer</td></tr><tr><td>58</td><td>rotation: 0 none, 1 left, 2 right (other values: none)</td></tr><tr><td>59</td><td>bit 0: the cartridge uses AUDIN as an address line</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>the EEPROM (Rule Lynx:5.15): bits 2-0 the part, bit 7 8-bit organisation, bit 6 an SD card cartridge</td></tr><tr><td>61-62</td><td>spare</td></tr><tr><td>63</td><td>the SD card cartridge's interface (read with bit 6 of byte 60; \"SD card cartridges\" below)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A loaded file is identified in this order, the first that applies:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>\"LNX2\" at bytes 0-3 and more than 64 bytes: an LNX2 file (Rule Lynx:5.23);</li><li>\"BS93\" at bytes 6-9 and at least 10 bytes: a BLL program (Rule Lynx:5.11);</li><li>\"LYNX\" at bytes 0-3, at least 64 bytes, version 1: a .lnx file;</li><li>otherwise a raw image (Rule Lynx:5.6; a \"LYNX\" header of another version too, Rule Lynx:5.8).</li></ol>\n<p>After 1, 3 or 4 is accepted, the headerless Epyx test of Rule Lynx:5.20 runs; a BLL program never gets it.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (the community's format; the reference reads it the same way).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.5</b> (the image after the header). The image is bank 0's 256 pages, then bank 1's; an image shorter than that reads FF beyond its end. An image longer than its banks (two, or four with AUDIN) is accepted: the extra bytes are kept with the cartridge but no read ever reaches them. Only the 2 MB limit of Rule Lynx:5.7 refuses a file for its size. A page size that is 0, over 2048 or not a power of two is refused.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.6</b> (a raw image). A file that does not start with \"LYNX\" is a raw image: bank 0 only, and, since a bank is 256 pages, its page size is its size / 256:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Raw image size</th><th>Page size (bytes)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>64 KB</td><td>256</td></tr><tr><td>128 KB</td><td>512</td></tr><tr><td>256 KB</td><td>1024</td></tr><tr><td>512 KB</td><td>2048</td></tr><tr><td>any other size</td><td>1024 (1 KB pages)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two raw images take another board from a short table the core keeps by the image's SHA-256 (the hashes are not printed on this tab; the rows are checked only for a raw image of the row's size):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Raw image size</th><th>Board the table gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>512 KB (one image)</td><td>1 KB pages, AUDIN banks (a second set, Rule Lynx:5.10), a 93C46 EEPROM (header byte 60 code 01)</td></tr><tr><td>128 KB (one image)</td><td>1 KB pages</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A build without the table differs from ours only on those two images; none of the test programs of chapter 11 is one of them.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM (256 pages to a bank).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.7</b> (the size limit). Images over 2 MB (four banks of 512 KB: both banks in both AUDIN sets at 2 KB pages) are refused.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE with the Lynx reference emulator's limit.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.8</b> (the version). A file whose header says \"LYNX\" but whose version is not 1 is read as having no header: the whole file, header bytes included, is a raw image as in Rule Lynx:5.6.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (both references do so, the second Lynx emulator after its checksum table, which is not used here).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.9</b> (name and manufacturer). Bytes 10-41 and 42-57 are text up to the first 0 byte; the host reads them with <code>lynx_cart_name</code> and <code>lynx_cart_maker</code>. A full 32-byte name has no 0 byte and is kept whole.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The references cut it at 31 and 15: NOT ESTABLISHED which a front end wants; nothing in the machine depends on it.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--audin-banks\">AUDIN banks</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.10</b> (the second set). With header byte 59 bit 0 set, the image holds a second set of banks after the first (bank 0 again, then bank 1 again), and a read uses the second set while AUDIN is driven high: IODIR bit 4 set (an output) and IODAT bit 4 set.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (the format's convention; the cartridge's wiring).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--bll-programs\">BLL programs</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.11</b> (the BLL file). A BLL program (the <code>.o</code> files of the BLL development kit, still made by cc65's <code>lynx-bll</code> configuration) is not a cartridge but a program to be put straight into RAM. The file is a 10-byte header, then the code:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-1</td><td>80 08: a 65C02 BRA over the rest of the header</td></tr><tr><td>2-3</td><td>the load address, big endian</td></tr><tr><td>4-5</td><td>the size, header included, big endian</td></tr><tr><td>6-9</td><td>\"BS93\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A file with \"BS93\" at bytes 6-9 is taken as one. A size of 10 or less is refused; a file shorter than its size is loaded as far as it goes.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> On the console a BLL program arrives over ComLynx through an uploader. Both reference emulators detect it so: CONVERGENCE.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.12</b> (the BLL start, at every power on). At every power on the core, instead of the boot, does this, in order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The whole of chapter 1's \"Power on\" (Rules Lynx:1.13-1.20), reset sequence included, so the display already stands 30 ticks along line 0 (three pixels drawn, one burst made) and <code>t</code> = 0, exactly as for a cartridge. The firmware's code never runs (the CPU's registers and PC are simply replaced in step 5).</li><li>RAM is cleared and the file, header included, is copied to the load address less 10 (wrapping from FFFF to 0000), so the code lands at the load address.</li><li>MAPCTL = 0C (the firmware's space and the vectors are RAM).</li><li>Six register values are written through Mikey's ordinary write at <code>t</code> = 0, in this order: Timer 0 backup 9E, Timer 0 CTLA 18 (1 us, reload, count: a 159 us line), Timer 2 backup 68, Timer 2 CTLA 1F (linked, 105 lines), DISPCTL 09, PBKUP 29. The palette is 0.</li><li>The CPU is set to A = X = Y = 00, S = FF, P = 24 (I and bit 5 set, every other flag clear), and PC as in this table:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Header bytes 0-1</th><th>PC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>80 08</td><td>the header's first byte (the load address less 10), which branches over the header to the load address</td></tr><tr><td>anything else</td><td>the load address</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The first trace line is that first opcode fetch at <code>t</code> = 0. The cartridge ports then read FF (no cartridge). The same start holds for a headerless Epyx image (Rule Lynx:5.20), with PC = 0200.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The PC: MECHANISM, that is what the header's first two bytes are for; the second Lynx emulator starts there too; the Lynx reference emulator starts at the load address, one instruction later. MAPCTL 0C: as cc65's start-up code, which the uploader runs inside, sets them; the Lynx reference emulator starts programs so, the second Lynx emulator with 00. The display: started as both references do (CONVERGENCE); the second Lynx emulator's source notes programs that never set the display timers themselves. What the uploader leaves in every other register is NOT ESTABLISHED; a program that sets up the machine itself, as cc65's do, depends on none of it. Checked by <code>tests/lynx/gate6.sh</code>: the project's BLL program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/bll</code>, built to <code>build/lynx/bll.o</code>) gives the same result bytes and picture as the oracle, and <code>tests/lynx/homebrew_checks.c</code> checks the start state.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--the-cartridge-eeprom\">The cartridge EEPROM</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.13</b> (the parts). Some cartridges carry a serial EEPROM: a 93C46, 93C56, 93C66, 93C76 or 93C86. The .lnx header's byte 60 names it:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bits 2-0</th><th>Part</th><th>Bytes</th><th>Address bits, 16-bit words</th><th>Address bits, 8-bit bytes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>93C46</td><td>128</td><td>6</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>93C56</td><td>256</td><td>8</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>93C66</td><td>512</td><td>8</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>93C76</td><td>1024</td><td>10</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>93C86</td><td>2048</td><td>10</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Homebrew above all uses them, to keep high scores and saved games. CONVERGENCE: both references read the byte so. The address widths are the parts' datasheets' (MECHANISM: the 93C56 and 93C66 share one command format, as do the 93C76 and 93C86, the top address bit of the smaller part unused); the second Lynx emulator uses the same, the Lynx reference emulator one bit fewer for the 93C56 and 93C76.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.14</b> (organisation). Bit 7 set: the chip is organised in bytes, else in 16-bit words. Bit 6 (an SD card cartridge, whose card is not modelled) is ignored.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.15</b> (bytes that name no part).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Byte 60</th><th>In the header</th><th>Passed to <code>lynx_set_eeprom</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>part 1-5, bits 5-3 clear</td><td>that part</td><td>that part</td></tr><tr><td>00</td><td>no EEPROM</td><td>accepted: no EEPROM</td></tr><tr><td>any other byte with part 0, 6 or 7, or any of bits 5-3 set (80 and C0 apart, next row)</td><td>no EEPROM</td><td>refused (-1); so is a value below 0 or above 255</td></tr><tr><td>80 (or C0): bit 7 with no part</td><td>a 93C46 in bytes, taken as the byte 81 (or C1); <code>lynx_eeprom_type</code> then reports 81 (or C1)</td><td>refused (-1), as any byte naming no part; the host names the 93C46 in bytes as 81</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE with the Lynx reference emulator, which reads 80 so; the second Lynx emulator gives no EEPROM: the header's writers set 80 for \"8-bit\" without a part, and the 93C46 is the part homebrew uses.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.16</b> (the wiring). Chip select is the cartridge's address line A7 and the clock A1, that is bits 7 and 1 of the ripple counter; DI and DO are tied together on AUDIN:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>AUDIN (IODIR bit 4)</th><th>DI</th><th>IODAT bit 4 reads</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>an output (bit set)</td><td>the console drives it from IODAT bit 4</td><td>the written value</td></tr><tr><td>an input (bit clear)</td><td>DO's level as it stands just before the rising clock edge (1 when the chip drives nothing)</td><td>DO while the chip drives it, else 1 (pulled high)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So an idle chip (or one showing a ready status) clocked while AUDIN is an input takes that 1 as a start bit and begins a command. Since chip select falls when the counter passes 255, one command lasts at most 32 clock pulses.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM: the diagram in the EEPROM routines cc65 carries, by the BLL kit's author; both references wire it the same. So a program lowers chip select with a strobe (the counter goes to 0), raises it with 128 accesses to RCART0 (the counter reaches 128), and makes each clock pulse with 4 more (A1 rises after 2, falls after 4). Programs avoid the stray start bit by making AUDIN an output before clocking.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.17</b> (the chip's commands). The chip acts at each rising clock edge while chip select is high; chip select low ends a command. Idle, a 1 on DI is the start bit; then two opcode bits and the address, most significant bit first:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Opcode bits</th><th>Address top two bits</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>any</td><td>READ: DO gives a dummy 0 at once, then at each rising edge the next data bit (8 or 16), most significant first, running on into the next address</td></tr><tr><td>01</td><td>any</td><td>WRITE, then the data</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>any</td><td>ERASE (all ones)</td></tr><tr><td>00</td><td>11</td><td>EWEN (erase and write enabled)</td></tr><tr><td>00</td><td>00</td><td>EWDS (disabled)</td></tr><tr><td>00</td><td>10</td><td>ERAL (erase all)</td></tr><tr><td>00</td><td>01</td><td>WRAL (write all, then the data)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM: the parts' datasheets.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.18</b> (erase, write and the busy status). Erase and write are refused unless enabled; enabling is off at power on.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>ERASE, WRITE, ERAL or WRAL</th><th>What follows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>refused (not enabled)</td><td>changes nothing, starts no busy time and gives no status: the chip simply goes idle (DO undriven, reading 1) and waits for a start bit</td></tr><tr><td>accepted</td><td>takes effect at the last bit, and the chip is then busy for 2 ms, 32,000 ticks counted in Mikey's time (NOT ESTABLISHED); from then until the next start bit, while chip select is high, DO is the status: 0 busy, 1 ready</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The status survives chip select going low and high again (DO reads 1 while it is low); only a start bit (a 1 on DI at a rising clock with chip select high) ends it, and that start bit begins the next command.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The 2 ms: NOT ESTABLISHED, the datasheets give maxima of 5 to 10 ms; programs poll. The references agree on all the commands programs use (read, write, erase, EWEN, EWDS); they count busy time in reads of IODAT (2 in the second Lynx emulator, 100 in the Lynx reference emulator) where this core counts it in time.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.19</b> (the contents). The contents are kept by the host (<code>lynx_eeprom_read</code> and <code>lynx_eeprom_write</code>, <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE-LYNX.md</code></a>), as bytes: a 16-bit word n is bytes 2n (low) and 2n + 1 (high). A cartridge load starts the chip erased (all FF); a power cycle keeps the contents and resets the chip's command state.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The byte layout is the one both references save, so their save files load. Checked by <code>tests/lynx/gate6.sh</code>: the project's EEPROM program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/eeprom</code>, cc65's EEPROM routines, the way homebrew drives the chip) on a 93C46, 93C66 and 93C86 gives the oracle's result bytes and leaves the oracle's contents, fresh and again from those contents; <code>tests/lynx/homebrew_checks.c</code> drives every part in both organisations through the counter and AUDIN: widths, the dummy bit, reads running on, EWEN and EWDS, ERASE, ERAL, WRAL, the busy status, the byte layout, the contents over a power cycle.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--headerless-epyx-images\">Headerless Epyx images</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.20</b> (finding the frame and starting it). Some images of Epyx's development cartridges begin not with the boot's loader frame but with zero bytes: 410 of them, or 512. The core tests every accepted LNX2, .lnx or raw file at load (never a BLL program), in this order; \"bank 0\" and \"the image\" are the file's cartridge image after any header:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Bank 0 is at least 512 bytes and its first 410 bytes are zero; otherwise the image is an ordinary cartridge.</li><li>The frame is looked for at offset 410, or at 512 when all of the first 512 bytes are zero.</li><li>\"Decrypts\" test, a size test only: the frame's first byte gives a block count <code>256 - byte</code> of 1 to 5, and the image from the frame's start holds the whole frame (1 + 51 x count bytes). The blocks are not checked against the modulus; each is decrypted as Rule Lynx:8.3 says. A frame that fails the test leaves the image an ordinary cartridge.</li></ol>\n<p>An image that passes: its format becomes 4 (<code>lynx_cart_format</code>), and every power on puts the frame's plaintext at 0200 and starts the CPU there in the BLL start state (Rule Lynx:5.12). The plaintext is the core's own decryption, Rule Lynx:8.3's second table (for a block below the modulus it is exactly <code>c^3 mod N</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The console's boot cannot start such images (it would read a block count of 256). Both reference emulators start them without the boot, the second Lynx emulator through a separate loader file, the Lynx reference emulator directly (CONVERGENCE with it). What the development hardware did is NOT ESTABLISHED. Checked by <code>tests/lynx/gate6.sh</code> (two synthetic images, a cc65 loader frame after 410 or 512 zeros, against the oracle) and <code>homebrew_checks.c</code> (the thresholds, the placement, the restart).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--naming-the-eeprom-from-the-host\">Naming the EEPROM from the host</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.21</b> (<code>lynx_set_eeprom</code>). The host can name the part (<code>lynx_set_eeprom</code>, the byte 60 form of Rule Lynx:5.15; the test page has an EEPROM choice next to the model).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Older .lnx files leave byte 60 at 0 even for cartridges that carry an EEPROM; the references find those by a table of image checksums, which this project does not copy.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--cartridge-ram\">Cartridge RAM</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.22</b> (three forms). Cartridge RAM comes in three forms: the LNX2 header's banks (Rule Lynx:5.23), their kept RAM (Rule Lynx:5.25), and RAM the host adds (Rule Lynx:5.26).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> No released cartridge is known to carry RAM on the port, and the console's documentation describes none: everything in this section is the references' invention, taken on as CONVERGENCE with the reference that invented it (the tier of the behaviour here is agreement with that one source; whether any cartridge was ever wired so is NOT ESTABLISHED). All three forms are present because homebrew files and tools use them.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.23</b> (the LNX2 header). A 64-byte header like the .lnx one, which declares four banks instead of two page sizes:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-3</td><td>\"LNX2\"</td></tr><tr><td>4-7</td><td>four bank descriptors: bank 0, bank 0 of the second (AUDIN) set, bank 1, bank 1 of the second set</td></tr><tr><td>8-9</td><td>version, little endian: must be 1 (another version: the file is refused)</td></tr><tr><td>10-41, 42-57</td><td>name, manufacturer</td></tr><tr><td>58</td><td>rotation, as the .lnx header's</td></tr><tr><td>59</td><td>flags: bit 0, made for the Lynx II</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>the EEPROM, as the .lnx header's</td></tr><tr><td>61, 62</td><td>spare, custom</td></tr><tr><td>63</td><td>the SD card cartridge's interface</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The Lynx reference emulator's format, CONVERGENCE with it, run: the oracle reads it.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.24</b> (an LNX2 descriptor and its bank). A descriptor:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7-6</td><td>the type: 0 unused, 1 RAM kept by a battery, 2 RAM, 3 read only</td></tr><tr><td>5-3</td><td>the block count, 2 &lt;&lt; n (2 to 256)</td></tr><tr><td>2-0</td><td>the block size, 16 &lt;&lt; n (16 to 2048 bytes)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A bank is addressed as a .lnx bank is, with its block size as the page size, wrapping at its size (a bank of 16 blocks answers pages 16 to 255 with its pages 0 to 15). The image holds every used bank, one after another in descriptor order, RAM banks too: their contents at power on (FF where the file ends). An unused bank of the second set reads the first set's bank (a .lnx file's reads FF). The AUDIN second set is used when any second-set bank is declared. Flags bit 0 is reported to the host (<code>lynx_cart_model</code>), which chooses the model; the core does not switch model on its own.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.25</b> (kept RAM). The type-1 banks are the cartridge's kept RAM, which the host keeps between sessions as it keeps the EEPROM: <code>lynx_cart_ram_size</code>, <code>lynx_cart_ram_read</code>, <code>lynx_cart_ram_write</code> (the kept banks one after another in descriptor order, that is bank 0, bank 0 of the second set, bank 1, bank 1 of the second set, skipping the banks that are not kept: the order the file holds them in and the Lynx reference emulator saves them) and <code>lynx_cart_ram_changes</code> (a count of writes to them). A load starts them from the file; a power cycle keeps them.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The test page keeps them in the browser's local storage, one entry per cartridge.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.26</b> (RAM the host adds to a .lnx or raw file, <code>lynx_set_cart_ram</code>, after loading).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What is added</th><th>Rules</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 (<code>LYNX_CART_RAM_8K</code>)</td><td>8 KB of kept RAM as bank 1, 256 blocks of 32 bytes, zero until the host restores it</td><td>only for a file with no bank 1. It is exactly an LNX2 file declaring bank 1 as type 1, 256 blocks of 32 bytes</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>a file with no bank 1 gets 64 KB of RAM there (256 pages of 256 bytes, FF at power on); a file with a bank 1 gets nothing added</td><td>the write enable of Rule Lynx:5.27. Not kept: the host is not given it to save between sessions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Either kind adds RAM only as bank 1 of the first set. On a file with an AUDIN second set, while AUDIN is driven high the ports reach the second set's banks instead, and a .lnx or raw file's missing second-set bank 1 reads FF and stores nothing; the added RAM is reached only with AUDIN low or an input.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Kind 2 is the Lynx reference emulator's 8 KB of kept RAM; it gives it to one family of commercial titles, found by a checksum table (not copied); here the host asks for it; it is checked against the oracle running such a file. Kind 1 is the second Lynx emulator's bank 1; its source calls the write enable its own private patch. CONVERGENCE with the second Lynx emulator, read not run (no oracle runs it); checked directly only. The test page's \"Cartridge RAM\" choice offers both.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.27</b> (kind 1's write enable). With kind 1, bank 1 is written while a write enable is on:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The write enable starts on when the 64 KB is added (a file with no bank 1), off when the file has its own bank 1; a power on does not change it.</li><li>Every IODAT write made while AUDIN is an output (IODIR bit 4 set) sets the write enable to that write's bit 4: 1 turns it on, 0 turns it off. IODAT writes with AUDIN an input leave it alone. (So a file that has a bank 1 becomes writable too.)</li><li>While it is on, writes to the port that reaches bank 1 store into it whatever it holds (RAM or the file's own bytes), and so do writes to the second set's bank 1 when the file has one. A file's own bank 1 always has its 256 pages, also where the file ends sooner: past the file's end it reads FF (Rule Lynx:5.5), and a write there while the enable is on stores, and reads back.</li></ol>\n<p>Within a session nothing is restored at a power on: the added RAM, and any bytes written into the file's own bank 1, stay as written until the next cartridge load (the same holds for an LNX2 file's type-2 RAM banks).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Checked by <code>tests/lynx/gate7.sh</code>: the project's cartridge RAM program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/cartram</code>; the LNX2 files are made from its .lnx file by <code>tests/programs-lynx/build.sh</code>) on three files and the host's 8 KB bank, against the oracle and against what the declared banks make the results (<code>tests/lynx/cartram_expect.py</code>), fresh and from kept RAM; direct checks in <code>tests/lynx/cart2_checks.c</code>; mutations caught.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-05-cartridge--sd-card-cartridges\">SD card cartridges</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:5.28</b> (SD card cartridges are not emulated). The header's byte 60 bit 6 with byte 63 naming an interface (0 GameDrive, 1 \"El Cheapo SD\") marks a flash cartridge with an SD card slot. Bit 6 and byte 63 are read (<code>lynx_load_reason</code> names an SD cartridge) and the file runs as an ordinary cartridge, so the menu program such a file holds starts and finds no card.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Only the Lynx reference emulator emulates them (read, not run: the oracle harness gives it no card). The cartridge's microcontroller answers a command protocol spoken through the cartridge port (open and read directories, open, seek, read and write files, program a file into the cartridge's flash banks), and the Lynx reference emulator serves it from the host's file system, the directory the cartridge file came from standing for the card. Doing the same here would need the interface to carry a file tree from the host (the core fetches and keeps nothing, and the page has no directory), and the protocol would rest on one source's reading of the makers' firmware, with no documentation or hardware result to check it by, and no project program that uses it. Not done. A proposal in <code>docs/incoming/lynx-2.md</code>.</p></div></section>","06-sound":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-06-sound-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-06-sound\">6. Sound</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: times are in ticks (chapter 1), counted from power on (Rule Lynx:1.19); register addresses and bit masks are hexadecimal; levels (OUTPUT, VOLUME, the mix and the samples) are signed decimal numbers.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-06-sound--the-channels\">The channels</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.1</b> (the channel registers). There are four channels, each a counting unit (chapter 4, units 8-11) with four more registers:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Channel c</th><th>Register</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FD20 + 8c</td><td>VOLUME (signed 8-bit)</td></tr><tr><td>FD21 + 8c</td><td>FEEDBACK (tap select)</td></tr><tr><td>FD22 + 8c</td><td>OUTPUT (signed 8-bit; the channel's current level)</td></tr><tr><td>FD23 + 8c</td><td>SHIFT (the shift register's bits 7-0)</td></tr><tr><td>FD24-FD27 + 8c</td><td>BACKUP, CTLA, COUNT, CTLB (chapter 4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.2</b> (the shift register and register access). Each channel has a 12-bit shift register: SHIFT is bits 7-0, CTLB bits 7-4 are bits 11-8. Writing SHIFT, VOLUME, FEEDBACK or OUTPUT stores the byte; reading returns it. Writing CTLB stores bits 7-3 (the top of the shift register and DONE).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.3</b> (a terminal borrow of the channel's counter). At each one, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The taps: FEEDBACK bits 5-0 select shift register bits 5-0; FEEDBACK bits 6 and 7 select bits 10 and 11; CTLA bit 7 selects bit 7.</li><li>The new bit is the inverse of the XOR of the selected bits (all bits clear: 1).</li><li>The register shifts left by one (12 bits), taking the new bit into bit 0.</li><li>OUTPUT is set by this table (results as signed 8-bit):</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>CTLA bit 5 (integrate)</th><th>New bit 1</th><th>New bit 0</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>clear (off)</td><td>OUTPUT = <code>+VOLUME</code></td><td>OUTPUT = <code>-VOLUME</code></td></tr><tr><td>set (on)</td><td>OUTPUT = <code>OUTPUT + VOLUME</code>, clipped to -128..127</td><td>OUTPUT = <code>OUTPUT - VOLUME</code>, clipped to -128..127</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> HARDWARE-OBSERVED: lynx-tests audio (the shift register and output after known sequences, both clipping rails) and audio2 (linked channels, speed, feedback and register changes mid-run).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-06-sound--the-mix\">The mix</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.4</b> (the mix of each model).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Left</th><th>Right</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lynx I</td><td>the sum of the four OUTPUTs (signed)</td><td>the same sum</td></tr><tr><td>Lynx II</td><td>the sum over the channels of each channel's left level (below)</td><td>the sum of the right levels</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Lynx II, with its stereo registers ATTEN_A-D (FD40-FD43), MPAN (FD44) and MSTEREO (FD50), channel c's level on each side, the first row that applies:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Condition (left side; right side)</th><th>Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MSTEREO bit <code>4 + c</code> set (left); bit <code>c</code> set (right)</td><td>silent (0)</td></tr><tr><td>MPAN bit <code>4 + c</code> set (left); bit <code>c</code> set (right)</td><td><code>OUTPUT x n / 15</code> (integer division, towards zero), n its ATTEN nibble: high nibble left, low right</td></tr><tr><td>otherwise</td><td>full level (OUTPUT)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>On the Lynx I the stereo registers read FD and ignore writes.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE (the reference; Atari's Lynx II register notes as it records them); the n/15 scale is NOT ESTABLISHED.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.5</b> (when the mix changes). The mix is recomputed whenever an OUTPUT changes (a borrow or a write) or a stereo register is written.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-06-sound--the-output\">The output</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.6</b> (the samples, tick by tick). The core produces 44,100 stereo pairs a second. Within each Mikey tick (step 2 of Rule Lynx:4.4), in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The tick count goes up by one.</li><li>The mix as it stands (set by earlier ticks) is added to both sums, left and right, and the tick is counted in the sample.</li><li>The phase goes up by 44,100.</li><li>If the phase has reached 16,000,000: 16,000,000 is taken off it (the remainder kept); the sample is each sum times 64 divided by the ticks summed (integer division, towards zero), clipped to 16 bits; the sums and the tick count of the sample restart at 0.</li></ol>\n<p>Only then does the tick's own work run (the display, refresh, and the timers and channels whose slot it is: steps 3-7 of Rule Lynx:4.4), so a channel that changes its output in a tick is heard from the next tick on. The phase starts at 0 at power on (Rule Lynx:1.19), so the first sample is made at the 363rd tick (363 x 44,100 is the first multiple to reach 16,000,000). So four channels at full level reach the 16-bit limit.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The console's analogue output (its filtering and level) is NOT ESTABLISHED.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:6.7</b> (the output buffer). The output buffer holds 8,192 pairs' places, a ring that holds at most 8,191 pairs; when a new pair finds it full, the oldest pair is dropped.</p></section>","07-suzy":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-07-suzy-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-07-suzy\">7. Suzy</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: every time is in <b>ticks</b> (periods of the 16 MHz system clock, the glossary's \"tick\") unless it says 1/64 tick; the timing model's sums are kept in 1/64 tick. A part's ticks count from the start of that part; a sleep step's work counts from the start of that step (chapter 1, \"Sleep\"). Addresses, register values, bit masks and the 16-bit sizes such as 80.00 are hexadecimal; counts, coordinates (x 0 to 159, y 0 to 101, in pixels from the screen's top-left corner), costs and constants are decimal; \"40 (hex)\" says so where a hexadecimal number could be mistaken. Bits are numbered from 0, the least significant.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-07-suzy--registers\">Registers</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.1</b> (the register file). FC00-FC2F must be 24 sixteen-bit registers, register <code>i</code> at FC00 + 2i (low byte first):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>i</th><th>Name</th><th>i</th><th>Name</th><th>i</th><th>Name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>TMPADR</td><td>8</td><td>SCBNEXT</td><td>16</td><td>SPRDOFF</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>TILTACUM</td><td>9</td><td>SPRDLINE</td><td>17</td><td>SPRVPOS</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>HOFF</td><td>10</td><td>HPOSSTRT</td><td>18</td><td>COLLOFF</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>VOFF</td><td>11</td><td>VPOSSTRT</td><td>19</td><td>VSIZACUM</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>VIDBAS</td><td>12</td><td>SPRHSIZ</td><td>20</td><td>HSIZOFF</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>COLLBAS</td><td>13</td><td>SPRVSIZ</td><td>21</td><td>VSIZOFF</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>VIDADR</td><td>14</td><td>STRETCH</td><td>22</td><td>SCBADR</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>COLLADR</td><td>15</td><td>TILT</td><td>23</td><td>PROCADR</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.2</b> (byte writes and reads). Writing a register's low byte must set the whole register to that byte (the high byte becomes 0); writing its high byte must set only the high byte. Reads return the byte. FC40-FC6F read as FC00-FC2F (the maths registers are the same storage, Rule Lynx:7.3); FC30-FC3F and FC70-FC7F read FF and ignore writes.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.3</b> (the maths letters). The maths registers are letters naming bytes of those registers:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Letters</th><th>Register</th><th>Letters</th><th>Register</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D C (FC52, FC53)</td><td>SPRDLINE low, high</td><td>H G (FC60, FC61)</td><td>SPRDOFF</td></tr><tr><td>B A (FC54, FC55)</td><td>HPOSSTRT</td><td>F E (FC62, FC63)</td><td>SPRVPOS</td></tr><tr><td>P N (FC56, FC57)</td><td>VPOSSTRT</td><td>M L (FC6C, FC6D)</td><td>SCBADR</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>K J (FC6E, FC6F)</td><td>PROCADR</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.4</b> (writes in FC40-FC6F). A write to a listed letter must act as this table says (a low letter clears its partner as usual; a high letter sets only the high byte); a write to any other address in FC40-FC6F acts as a write to the register 40 (hex) bytes lower (as for reads: FC40 is TMPADR's low byte).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Letter written</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D</td><td>CD = D</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>the high byte; in signed mode (SPRSYS bit 7), then the <b>sign</b> of CD is taken (negative if its top bit is set, except that 0 counts as negative and 8000 as positive: the multiplier's sign bug, HARDWARE-OBSERVED by lynx-tests math) and a negative nonzero CD is replaced by its two's complement</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>AB = B</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>the high byte, the same sign treatment of AB, then a <b>multiply</b> starts</td></tr><tr><td>P, N</td><td>NP as usual</td></tr><tr><td>H, G</td><td>GH as usual</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>EF = F</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>the high byte of EF, then a <b>divide</b> starts</td></tr><tr><td>M</td><td>LM = M, and the maths overflow bit clears</td></tr><tr><td>L, K, J</td><td>as usual</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.5</b> (the kept signs). The two signs must be kept until the next C or A write in signed mode takes them again; both are positive at power on. A write of C or A in unsigned mode leaves the kept sign as it was, so a multiply in signed mode after such a write uses the sign last taken (a program that switches modes between writing C and A gets that mix).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.6</b> (multiply). In this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The carry clears.</li><li><code>r = AB x CD</code> (32 bits).</li><li>In signed mode, if exactly one sign was negative, the carry is <code>r != 0</code> and r is negated.</li><li>EFGH = r.</li><li>If accumulate (SPRSYS bit 6) is on, JKLM += r (32 bits) and both the carry and the overflow bit become the carry out of that add.</li></ol>\n<p>The unit is busy for 44 ticks, 54 in signed or accumulate mode.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.7</b> (divide). Carry and overflow clear. If NP is 0: ABCD = FFFFFFFF, overflow and carry set, remainder 0; else ABCD = EFGH / NP, remainder EFGH mod NP, carry = remainder != 0. Then JK = 0, LM = the remainder. Busy for 176 ticks plus 14 per leading zero bit of NP (16 for NP = 0).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.8</b> (the unsafe bit). The <b>unsafe</b> bit must be set in two ways, both as written, and stays set until a SPRSYS write with bit 2:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>starting a multiply or divide (Rules Lynx:7.6, 7.7) always sets it;</li><li>any read or write of FC00-FC83 while the sprite engine is busy (SPRSYS bit 0) or the maths unit is busy (SPRSYS bit 7) sets it, the access then going ahead as usual.</li></ol>\n<p>So case 2 adds the sprite engine's case and the case of touching the maths registers before a result is ready; a program that waits out the busy time and starts no new operation leaves the bit as it was.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE for the times (the reference's author measured them; lynx-tests math test 8); MECHANISM for the arithmetic, HARDWARE-OBSERVED by lynx-tests math.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.9</b> (the other registers).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Write</th><th>Read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FC80</td><td>SPRCTL0</td><td>FF</td></tr><tr><td>FC81</td><td>SPRCTL1</td><td>FF</td></tr><tr><td>FC82</td><td>SPRCOLL</td><td>FF</td></tr><tr><td>FC83</td><td>SPRINIT</td><td>FF</td></tr><tr><td>FC88</td><td></td><td>SUZYHREV: 01</td></tr><tr><td>FC90</td><td>SUZYBUSEN (bit 0: Suzy may use the bus)</td><td>FF</td></tr><tr><td>FC91</td><td>SPRGO (Rule Lynx:7.12)</td><td>FF</td></tr><tr><td>FC92</td><td>SPRSYS (Rule Lynx:7.10)</td><td>SPRSYS status (Rule Lynx:7.11)</td></tr><tr><td>FCB0</td><td></td><td>the buttons (Rule Lynx:7.14)</td></tr><tr><td>FCB1</td><td></td><td>bit 0: pause</td></tr><tr><td>FCB2, FCB3</td><td>the cartridge (chapter 5)</td><td>the cartridge</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.10</b> (SPRSYS write). Stored; if bit 2 is set, the unsafe bit clears. Bits used: 7 signed maths, 6 accumulate, 5 no collisions for the whole list, 4 vertical stretch, 3 left-handed (Rule Lynx:7.14), 1 stop at the end of the current sprite (Rule Lynx:7.21).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.11</b> (SPRSYS read). Bit 0 sprites busy, bit 1 the stop request (written bit 1), bit 2 unsafe, bit 3 left-handed (written), bit 4 vertical stretch (written), bit 5 the last carry, bit 6 the maths overflow bit, bit 7 maths busy.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.12</b> (SPRGO write). Stored, and the stop request clears. Then:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>SPRGO bit 0</th><th>Engine</th><th>SCBNEXT's high byte</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>any</td><td>not 0</td><td>the engine becomes busy with its work cleared</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>any</td><td>0</td><td>the engine becomes busy with its work cleared, then is done at once</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>busy</td><td>any</td><td>done at once</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>not busy</td><td>any</td><td>nothing more</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>\"Done at once\" must be the same as a list's normal end: not busy, SPRGO bit 0 clear, the done signal pending (so a CPUSLEEP before the next SDONEACK does not sleep), the CPU awake. Bit 2 enables EVERON (Rule Lynx:7.32).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.13</b> (power on). At power on Suzy's registers, SPRSYS, the done signal and the sprite palette (the pen map of Rule Lynx:7.27 step 5) must all be 0: until a sprite loads a palette, every pen index maps to pen 0.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.14</b> (the buttons). FCB0: bit 7 up, 6 down, 5 left, 4 right, 3 option 1, 2 option 2, 1 B, 0 A (1 = pressed). With SPRSYS bit 3 (left-handed) set, up and down swap and left and right swap. FCB1 bit 0: pause; bits 7-1 read 0.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-07-suzy--sleep-and-done\">Sleep and done</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.15</b> (CPUSLEEP and SDONEACK). A write to CPUSLEEP (Mikey, FD91) must put the CPU to sleep only if all four hold: the engine is busy, SUZYBUSEN bit 0 is set, the done signal is not pending, and no enabled Mikey interrupt is pending. A write to SDONEACK (FD90) clears the done signal.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE; the conditions are the sprite performance guide's (HARDWARE-OBSERVED there).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.16</b> (parts). The engine must work only while the CPU is asleep and SUZYBUSEN bit 0 is set (chapter 1's sleep steps give it its ticks). Its time is a queue of <b>parts</b>, each a whole number of ticks, counted down by the ticks the steps give it:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>A sprite's first part is its header and reload: the header (and the reload, Rule Lynx:7.27 steps 1 to 8) is read at the part's start, and the part lasts the sprite's fixed cost (64, plus the reload costs) as rounded by Rule Lynx:7.17. A skipped sprite is one part of 10 ticks, its header read at the start.</li><li>Each later part begins by drawing one destination row, drawn or not (a row outside the window, Rule Lynx:7.36, is a part of its own; only source rows of zero height are folded into the next part) (every pixel of it, the depository not yet), after reading any source records and quadrant changes that come before it, and lasts that row's cost plus 20 for each source row of zero height read on the way to it.</li><li>The sprite's last part begins when its terminating offset 0 is read: the depository and EVERON are written then (Rule Lynx:7.27 step 10), and it lasts the ticks still owed (zero-height rows read after the last drawn row, and the rounding).</li><li>After the last sprite, the list's own part of 32 ticks; when it is used up the list is <b>done</b>: not busy, SPRGO bit 0 clear, the done signal pending, and the CPU wakes at the end of that sleep step.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.17</b> (rounding the parts). The sprite's running sum must be kept in 1/64 tick, and a part lasts <code>round(sum so far) - round(sum before it)</code> ticks, <code>round(x) = floor((x + 32) / 64)</code> (halves up), so a sprite's parts add up to its whole cost rounded once. A part can therefore last 0 ticks.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.18</b> (the list's first part). The list's first part (the first header read) must begin in the first sleep step after SPRGO in which Suzy is given work at all, whatever the amount: a step whose <code>w</code> came to 0 (or below 0, the deficit going to the debt) still begins it, since parts begin before any tick is counted. Only a step that gives no work because an enabled interrupt is pending begins nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.19</b> (when the next part begins). When a part's ticks are used up the next part must begin at once, in the same step, even when the step's work ends exactly there (the next row is drawn in that step, not the following one), and a part of 0 ticks is followed at once by the next. When the step's work runs out in the middle of a part, the rest of the part waits for the next step. While the CPU is awake (woken by an interrupt), no time runs.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.20</b> (the list's total time). Giving the time out in parts must not change the list's total time: it is the same as when the whole list was drawn at its first tick. A row's pixels land as Rule Lynx:7.37 says.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Gate 3's forty timings are the same as when the whole list was drawn at its first tick.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.21</b> (the stop request, SPRSYS bit 1). Written 1 while a list is being drawn, the engine must finish the sprite it is drawing and then end the list as if it had reached its last sprite (the list's own time still follows). In detail:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>When bit 1 was written 1</th><th>What is drawn</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>during a sprite's time</td><td>that sprite finishes; no more are drawn</td></tr><tr><td>before the first sprite is drawn (after SPRGO, before the CPU sleeps)</td><td>the first sprite, then the list ends</td></tr><tr><td>before SPRGO</td><td>everything (SPRGO withdraws the request, so it has no effect)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>\"Sprite\" here counts a skipped sprite as well: the request is looked at whenever one sprite's time is over and the next would begin, after at least one sprite, drawn or skipped. Every SPRSYS write stores bit 1 as written, so writing SPRSYS with bit 1 clear (for the maths unit, say) withdraws a request made before. The bit reads back as written until then.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE with the Lynx reference emulator (run: the oracle), which acts on the bit so; the second Lynx emulator stores it and ignores it; the console's documentation describes the bit as \"stop at the end of the current sprite\", which is what both the oracle and this core do (the exact instant the request is taken within a sprite's time is NOT ESTABLISHED). Checked by <code>tests/lynx/gate7.sh</code>: the project's sprite stop program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/sprstop</code>: the stop asked after SPRGO, from a timer interrupt during the first sprite, and before SPRGO) against the oracle and against what the request must make the screen, and direct checks of the list's time.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.22</b> (the maths unit's busy time). The maths unit's busy time must count down by every instruction's ticks (<code>t</code> after it less <code>t</code> before it, stall payments included, all of them, even the ticks before the access that started a maths operation; this is step 4 of Rule Lynx:1.21) and, in a sleep step, by the step's Suzy work <code>w</code> of chapter 1's \"Sleep\" (8, plus the shares of DMA and refresh, less any debt taken; never negative), not by the ticks the step lasts. A sleep step that gives Suzy no work because an enabled interrupt is pending after its Mikey run counts the busy time down by nothing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-07-suzy--the-sprite-engine\">The sprite engine</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.23</b> (RAM only). Every read the engine makes (the SCB, the palette, the source data, the depository's old value) and every write (video, collision, depository) must go to the 64 KB of RAM itself, never through MAPCTL: a list or a buffer at FC00-FFFF is read and drawn from RAM even while the CPU sees Suzy, Mikey or the firmware there. Addresses wrap from FFFF to 0000.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.24</b> (the list). While SCBNEXT's high byte is not 0, the engine must draw one sprite, up to 4096 sprites, and only while the list's time so far, by the timing model (Rules Lynx:7.36 onward), is under 16,000,000 ticks; then the list is finished. \"So far\" is the rounded time of the sprites already done, skipped ones included, without the list's own 32, which comes after the last sprite.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.25</b> (the bound within a sprite). Within a sprite, before each row drawing, if the cost of its row drawings so far (the 1/64-tick sum divided by 64, rounded down) exceeds what is left of those 16,000,000 ticks, the sprite must stop there (its depository and EVERON are still written).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> No working program comes near this bound: it keeps a broken list from stalling the host (NOT ESTABLISHED: the console would go on drawing).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.26</b> (the header's fields). SPRCTL0: bits 7-6 bits per pen minus 1; bit 5 horizontal flip; bit 4 vertical flip; bits 2-0 the type. SPRCTL1: bit 7 totally literal source; bits 5-4 the reload depth; bit 3 reuse the palette; bit 2 skip; bit 1 start up; bit 0 start left.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.27</b> (one sprite). In this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>SCBADR = SCBNEXT. From SCBADR: SPRCTL0, SPRCTL1, SPRCOLL, then SCBNEXT (16 bits). If SPRCTL1 bit 2 (skip) is set, this sprite is done.</li><li>Decode SPRCTL0 and SPRCTL1 (Rule Lynx:7.26).</li><li>Collisions happen for this sprite unless SPRSYS bit 5 or SPRCOLL bit 5 is set; its collision number is SPRCOLL bits 3-0. The depository value (FRED) = 0.</li><li>Read SPRDLINE, HPOSSTRT, VPOSSTRT (16 bits each). STRETCH = TILT = 0. Depth 1 or more: read SPRHSIZ, SPRVSIZ; depth 2 or more: STRETCH; depth 3: TILT.</li><li>Unless SPRCTL1 bit 3: read the palette, 8 bytes, each giving pens 2i (high nibble) and 2i+1; if the palette's first byte is at an address whose low byte is FA (xxFA, any page), only 6 bytes are read (pens 12-15 keep their old values: HARDWARE-OBSERVED, the palette page bug). TMPADR = the address after the palette's 8 bytes (the palette's first byte's address + 8, also when only 6 were read); with SPRCTL1 bit 3 (no palette read), TMPADR = the address after the header and its reload fields. A skipped sprite leaves TMPADR as it was.</li><li>EVERON's flag clears. <code>h = HPOSSTRT - HOFF</code>, <code>v = VPOSSTRT - VOFF</code> (signed 16-bit). TILTACUM = 0.</li><li>Quadrants: the start quadrant <code>start</code> is bit 0 = SPRCTL1 bit 0 (left), bit 1 = SPRCTL1 bit 1 (up); <code>flip</code> has bit 0 = H flip, bit 1 = V flip. Quadrant number <code>q</code> (0 to 3) is drawn with direction <code>pos = SEQ[start][q] XOR flip</code> and size corner <code>size = SEQ[start][q]</code>, where SEQ = {0,2,3,1}, {1,0,2,3}, {2,3,1,0}, {3,1,0,2} (0 down-right, 2 up-right, 3 up-left, 1 down-left: counter-clockwise). <code>dx</code> = -1 if <code>pos</code> bit 0, else 1; <code>dy</code> = -1 if <code>pos</code> bit 1, else 1. MECHANISM, HARDWARE-OBSERVED by lynx-tests sprites.</li><li>Start quadrant 0: <code>first = pos</code>; <code>y = v</code>; VSIZACUM = 0 if <code>size</code> bit 1, else VSIZOFF.</li><li>Walk the source: repeatedly read the offset byte at SPRDLINE; SPRDLINE and PROCADR = SPRDLINE + offset. By the offset:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>the sprite ends</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>the next quadrant (Rule Lynx:7.28)</td></tr><tr><td>2 or more</td><td>a source row (Rule Lynx:7.29)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></li><li>The depository at SCBADR + COLLOFF: if collisions happen and the type is 2, 3, 4, 6 or 7, it becomes FRED. If SPRGO bit 2 (EVERON): its bit 7 is set if no pixel of the sprite was on screen, else cleared.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.28</b> (offset 1: the next quadrant). q becomes q + 1, modulo 4: new <code>pos</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>dx</code>, <code>dy</code>; <code>y = v</code>, plus <code>dy</code> if <code>pos</code>'s bit 1 differs from <code>first</code>'s; VSIZACUM as in Rule Lynx:7.27 step 8 (start quadrant 0). Nothing else starts again: <code>h</code> keeps the value the tilt has moved it to, and TILTACUM, SPRHSIZ (with its stretch) and SPRVSIZ carry on as they stand.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.29</b> (a source row). Its data runs from the byte after the offset up to (not including) the new SPRDLINE. In this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>acc = VSIZACUM + SPRVSIZ</code>; the row is drawn <code>acc &gt;&gt; 8</code> times (acc taken as 16 bits, so the count is its high byte, 0 to 255; 0: not drawn; a size of 80.00 or more draws 128 or more rows, as Desert Strike's screen clear needs); VSIZACUM = acc AND FF.</li><li>For each drawing, in order:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the row starts at <code>x = h</code>, plus <code>dx</code> if <code>pos</code>'s bit 0 differs from <code>first</code>'s, with the horizontal accumulator at 0 if <code>size</code> bit 0, else HSIZOFF;</li><li>draw it (Rule Lynx:7.30) at <code>y</code>;</li><li><code>y += dy</code>;</li><li><code>TILTACUM + TILT</code> (16 bits): add its high byte (signed) to <code>h</code>, keep its low byte in TILTACUM;</li><li>SPRHSIZ += STRETCH.</li></ol></li><li>After the drawings (when the next record is read), if SPRSYS bit 4 (vertical stretch): SPRVSIZ += STRETCH x the count, STRETCH taken as a signed 16-bit number and the count as computed in step 1 (0 to 255, never negative), the sum kept to 16 bits. A source row drawn 0 times adds nothing.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.30</b> (drawing a row at <code>y</code> from <code>x</code>).</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If <code>y</code> is outside 0..101 the row draws nothing. If <code>x</code> is past the right edge (x &gt;= 160) and <code>dx</code> is 1, or left of 0 and <code>dx</code> is -1, it draws nothing either.</li><li>The source is read through a bit reader starting at the row's first byte, most significant bit first. A source row always has at least one data byte (its offset is 2 or more), and the reader loads that first byte as the row starts, so every row that is decoded counts B = 1 at least (Rule Lynx:7.39), even when no pen is read from it. Asking for <code>n</code> bits when <code>n</code> or fewer remain before the row's end ends the row: the last bit of a row can never be used. HARDWARE-OBSERVED (the sprite tests; Atari's documented bug).</li><li>Decode by the source kind:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Decoding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Totally literal</b></td><td>repeatedly read a pen index of <code>bpp</code> bits; add SPRHSIZ to the horizontal accumulator; output <code>acc &gt;&gt; 8</code> pixels of <code>palette[index]</code>; keep <code>acc AND FF</code>. Unlike the vertical one, the horizontal sum is not cut to 16 bits: it is at most FFFF + FFFF (a starting HSIZOFF plus SPRHSIZ), so one pen can give up to 511 pixels</td></tr><tr><td><b>Packed</b></td><td>repeatedly read a 5-bit header; 0 ends the row. Header bit 4 set: a literal packet of <code>(header AND F) + 1</code> pens, each read and output as for totally literal. Clear: a run: one pen index read, then output <code>(header AND F) + 1</code> times as for totally literal (each time adding SPRHSIZ)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></li><li>Outputting a pixel at <code>x</code>: if <code>x</code> has moved past the edge in the direction of drawing (x &gt;= 160 with dx 1, x &lt; 0 with dx -1), the row ends here. Otherwise, if 0 &lt;= x &lt; 160, the pixel is drawn (Rule Lynx:7.31); then <code>x += dx</code>.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.31</b> (a pixel). A pixel at (x, y) with pen <code>p</code> (0-15), inside the window (0 &lt;= x &lt; 160, 0 &lt;= y &lt; 102): EVERON's flag sets (Rule Lynx:7.32). The destination byte is <code>VIDBAS + y x 80 + x / 2</code>, its high nibble for even x; the collision byte is <code>COLLBAS</code> plus the same offset. By the type:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Name</th><th>Video</th><th>Collision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>background</td><td>every pen drawn</td><td>pen 14: preserved; others: written</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>background, no collision</td><td>every pen drawn</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>boundary-shadow</td><td>pens 0 and 15 not drawn</td><td>pen 0: none; 14: preserved; others: detected</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>boundary</td><td>pens 0 and 15 not drawn</td><td>pen 0: none; others: detected</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>normal</td><td>pen 0 not drawn</td><td>pen 0: none; others: detected</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>non-collidable</td><td>pen 0 not drawn</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>XOR</td><td>pen 0 not drawn; others XOR the destination nibble</td><td>pen 0: none; 14: preserved; others: detected</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>shadow</td><td>pen 0 not drawn</td><td>pen 0: none; 14: preserved; others: detected</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Without collisions for this sprite, every \"written\", \"detected\" and \"preserved\" is \"none\". <b>Written</b>: the collision nibble becomes the sprite's collision number. <b>Detected</b>: FRED = max(FRED, the old collision nibble), then the nibble becomes the collision number. <b>Preserved</b>: the nibble is kept.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM (Atari's sprite types); HARDWARE-OBSERVED by the video and collision CRCs of lynx-tests sprites1-5.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.32</b> (EVERON's flag). EVERON's flag must set for every pixel inside the window, whatever the pen, transparent pens included.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> It records that the sprite reached the screen, not that it drew anything.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-07-suzy--the-timing-model\">The timing model</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.33</b> (the list's time). The list's time, in ticks, must be 32 plus each sprite's cost. Every figure in this section is in <b>ticks</b> unless it says 1/64 tick. The core keeps a sprite's sum in 1/64 tick as integers: each constant is first rounded to the nearest 1/64 (70.5 is 4512, 1.944 is 124, 0.0675 is 4, 2.77 is 177, 2.48 is 159, 45.7 is 2925, and so on) and then multiplied by its count. A sprite's cost is that sum rounded to the nearest tick, halves up, and is given out in parts as Rules Lynx:7.16 and 7.17 say.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> FITTED to the forty hardware timings of lynx-tests sprites1-5 (every test within its own 16 us window), its shape from the sprite performance guide's pipeline description.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.34</b> (a sprite's fixed cost). A skipped sprite: 10 ticks. Otherwise 64, plus 8 if the sizes are reloaded (depth 1 or more), plus 8 if the palette is, plus 4 at depth 2 or more, plus 4 at depth 3, plus 20 for each source row drawn zero times, plus each row drawing's cost.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> A skipped sprite's 10: its five header bytes at the two ticks a byte the guide measured for the size reload; CONVERGENCE with the reference, whose skipped SCB takes 9.6 in the project's sprite list timing program; no hardware timing of a skipped sprite. The 8s are the sprite guide's measured size and palette reload costs (HARDWARE-OBSERVED as the guide reports them; 8.5 each in tests/programs-lynx/scbtime, as on the reference); every one of the forty timed tests reloads both, so they fix the 80 of a full reload and say nothing of a sprite that reloads less.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.35</b> (the display's DMA: the exposure E). While the display runs, each of its 28-tick bursts holds the bus (chapter 1's sleep steps). The sprite engine must lose E ticks of work to a burst, E being the <b>exposure</b> of the part in progress when the step's work is worked out (the counts O, P, S, <code>nm</code> are those of the part's row, Rule Lynx:7.39). E is 30 during a header part (and from power on); in the first sleep step after SPRGO, before the list's first header part begins, E is the value it already has (30 from power on, else the last value the previous list left: SPRGO does not set it); after a row is drawn, its row's E holds through the parts that follow until the next header. A skipped sprite's part is a header part (E = 30). The list's own 32-tick part keeps the E of the part before it (the last row drawn, or 30 if the last sprite drew no row or was skipped). For a row, the first of these that applies:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Row</th><th>E (ticks)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>the row was not decoded (outside the window) or output nothing inside the window (O = 0)</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>a packed row expanded (O &gt; S)</td><td>21 (CONVERGENCE, the packed expansion grid, Rule Lynx:7.41)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>any other expansion (O &gt; P)</td><td>11 + floor(O / 4), at most 28</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>a 1-bpp row with <code>nm</code> &gt; 0 (destination bytes both read and written)</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>a downscaled row (2 O &lt; P)</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>a totally literal row at 2 bits per pen</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>otherwise</td><td>30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The sprite guide (from hardware) gives the shape: about 28 ticks of RAM ownership a burst, a grant handshake of about 10 that can show, work already in Suzy's buffers going on, and no fixed DMA percentage. One published timing has the display running (lynx-tests sprites4 test 8, an expansion to 24 outputs: 834 us; 833 here); the expansion rule is FITTED to it and to the reference, the others to the reference alone (tests/programs-lynx/dmagrid: eighteen kinds of row, each within 9 % of the reference's time and its burst cost within 7 ticks: CONVERGENCE where no hardware figure exists). The comparison build loses none.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.36</b> (rows not drawn). A row not drawn because <code>y</code> is outside the window costs 4 if it moves away from the window (y &lt; 0 with dy -1, or y &gt; 101 with dy 1), else 45.7. A row not drawn because <code>x</code> starts past the edge costs 45.7. These rows take only that figure: none of the group, XOR, clipping or depth terms (Rule Lynx:7.43) apply to them.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.37</b> (when the pixels land). A sprite's time must be given out as it is drawn: the header and reload first, then each destination row, all its pixels at once, at the start of that row's own time (with the rounding carried so that the sprite's whole time is as Rule Lynx:7.33 says to the tick). So the display's DMA reads rows the sprite has reached and not those it has not, and an interrupt that wakes the CPU in the middle of a sprite finds the rows drawn so far (Suzy pauses while the CPU is awake).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The order of the writes within a row is NOT ESTABLISHED (the reference writes pixel by pixel; the project's beam race program crosses the display within a line of the reference's, tests/lynx/gate8.sh).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.38</b> (decoded rows). Every row that is decoded (not one of Rule Lynx:7.36) costs by its kind: totally literal (Rule Lynx:7.40), packed and expanded (Rule Lynx:7.41), packed otherwise (Rule Lynx:7.42); then the terms of Rule Lynx:7.43 are added.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.39</b> (the counts of a drawing). For each drawing, count:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Count</th><th>What it counts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P</td><td>pen indexes read (a run counts one; a literal packet one each)</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td>source pixels (a run counts each of its repeats; a literal packet one each; totally literal rows: S = P)</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>source bytes the bit reader has loaded. It loads the row's first byte when the row starts, and loads the next byte only when a read needs bits from it (a read that takes exactly the bits left in the current byte does not load the next, and a read that fails because too few bits remain loads nothing)</td></tr><tr><td>O</td><td>pixels output inside the window (0 &lt;= x &lt; 160), transparent pens included</td></tr><tr><td>K</td><td>packets: headers read other than the terminating 0 header</td></tr><tr><td><code>nb</code></td><td>the destination bytes touched by pixels inside the window (transparent pens included)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nm</code></td><td>of those, the ones that must be read and written: a byte that got at least one pixel that is drawn and at least one transparent one, or any XOR pixel that is drawn (a byte whose touched pixels are all drawn, not XOR, is written only, whether one nibble or both was touched; one whose touched pixels are all transparent is read only)</td></tr><tr><td><code>xorb</code></td><td>of those, the ones where an XOR pixel (type 6, a pen that is drawn) was written</td></tr><tr><td><code>cw</code>, <code>cp</code>, <code>cd</code></td><td>the counts of collision groups (the screen's 8-pixel groups, x / 8) of each class: <b>detect</b> if any pixel was detected or both written and preserved, <b>write</b> if only written, <b>preserve</b> if only preserved</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.40</b> (totally literal rows), in ticks:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>decode = 1.944 P + 0.0675 B</code>; <code>build = 2 O</code> if O &gt; P, else 0; <code>ram = 2.77 nb + 2 nm + 2.48 B - 20</code>.</li><li><code>core</code> = the largest of the three.</li><li>Contention: if <code>ram &gt; 100</code> ticks and <code>decode &gt;= ram</code>, let <code>d = decode - ram</code> (ticks) and add entry <code>floor(d / 4)</code> of this table, whose values are in 1/64 tick (about <code>14.6 exp(-d / 35)</code> ticks): 934, 833, 743, 663, 592, 528, 471, 420, 375, 334, 298, 266, 237, 211, 189, 168, 150, 134, 119, 107, 95, 85, 76, 67, 60, 54, 48, 43, 38, 34, 30, 27, 24, 22, 19, 17, 15, 14, 12, 11 (entries 0 to 39; nothing from entry 40, d &gt;= 160). In the core's 1/64-tick integers the same test reads <code>ram &gt; 6400</code> and the entry <code>(decode - ram) / 256</code>, rounded down.</li><li>Cost = 70.5 + core; plus 6 if all three hold: the row was not clipped (Rule Lynx:7.43), O &gt; 1 (more than one output inside the window), and its last output inside the window was the first half of a destination byte in the direction drawn, <code>dx</code> (even x when dx is 1, odd x when dx is -1; with O &gt; 1 this is the same as the direction from its first output inside the window to its last).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.41</b> (packed rows, expanded: O &gt; S). Cost = 21 + 3.65 K + 2.45 O.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> No hardware timing covers a packed expansion (the guide's expansion rule, about 72 + 2 O a warm row, is measured on totally literal rows, and this core keeps it there); the reference charges such a row by its outputs alone, far less than a literal one for a few outputs and more for many, and this is CONVERGENCE with it (tests/programs-lynx/packexp: 23 expanded kinds, cc65's screen clear, one packed pen stretched to 160 by 102, and its bars among them, each within 6 % of the reference's with the display's DMA off and on; gate 8). cc65's graphics driver clears the screen this way on every frame a program redraws, so the clear decides much of a cc65 program's pace.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.42</b> (packed rows, otherwise). Cost = 69 + 3.65 K + max(2 O, 2.28 P).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:7.43</b> (terms added to every decoded row). To the cost of Rules Lynx:7.40 to 7.42 add:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Ticks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>each write or preserve group</td><td>+ 4.1</td></tr><tr><td>a row with XOR pixels (xorb &gt; 0: at least one XOR pixel that is drawn, inside the window)</td><td>+ 10.7 per detect group + 2 xorb + 13.6</td></tr><tr><td>a row without XOR pixels</td><td>+ 13.25 per detect group</td></tr><tr><td>the row was clipped (it ended because drawing left the window: an output at x &gt;= 160 moving right or x &lt; 0 moving left; outputs outside the window on the way in do not count) with fewer than 8 outputs (O)</td><td>- 5</td></tr><tr><td>depth 2 or more</td><td>+ 8</td></tr><tr><td>depth 3</td><td>a further + 18 (26 in all)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A total below 0 counts as 0. (Constants as 1/64-tick integers, rounded to nearest: 70.5 → 4512, and so on.)</p></section>","08-boot":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot\">8. The boot</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: times are in <b>ticks</b> (the 16 MHz system clock) counted from power on (t = 0 at the first opcode fetch, chapter 1), or in host frames (calls of <code>lynx_run_frame</code>, counted from 1 at power on); seconds are given beside ticks at 16,000,000 ticks a second. Addresses, bytes and register values are hexadecimal; counts of blocks and bytes are decimal.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot--what-the-consoles-firmware-does\">What the console's firmware does</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.1</b> (the documented boot). At reset the CPU must run the boot firmware at FE00-FFFF (MAPCTL 0). The firmware selects cartridge page 0, reads the encrypted <b>loader frame</b> from bank 0, decrypts it into RAM at 0200 and jumps there; the loader then loads the rest of the program however it likes.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM (the documented boot).</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot--the-loader-frame\">The loader frame</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.2</b> (the frame's layout). The frame's first byte is <code>256 - n</code>, <code>n</code> (1 to 5) the number of blocks; then <code>n</code> blocks of 51 bytes. Each block, read least significant byte first, is a number <code>c</code> below the public modulus <code>N</code> (51 bytes, printed below).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.3</b> (decrypting a block). The plaintext block must be <code>m = c^3 mod N</code> (RSA with public exponent 3). Its bytes are emitted from the least significant, skipping the most significant, as a running sum: each output byte is the previous output byte plus the next byte of <code>m</code>, modulo 256 (the sum starts at 0 and runs on across blocks). So each block gives 50 bytes.</p>\n<p>A block at or above the modulus (<code>c &gt;= N</code>, for example 51 bytes of FF, which an empty cartridge slot gives) is outside the documented boot. What it becomes depends on who decrypts it:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Decrypted by</th><th><code>c &gt;= N</code> gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the built-in firmware (the boot)</td><td>exactly <code>c^3 mod N</code>, as for any block (measured below)</td></tr><tr><td>the core itself, for a headerless Epyx image (Rule Lynx:5.20)</td><td>the result of this procedure, which equals <code>c^3 mod N</code> only for <code>c &lt; N</code>: <code>m = mul(c, mul(c, c))</code>, where <code>mul(a, b)</code> starts from <code>acc = 0</code> and, for each bit of <code>b</code> from its most significant (51 bytes, 408 bits), first sets <code>acc = 2 acc mod 2^408</code> and subtracts N once if <code>acc &gt;= N</code>, then, if the bit is 1, sets <code>acc = (acc + a) mod 2^408</code> and subtracts N once if <code>acc &gt;= N</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Worked example, one block of 51 FF bytes (frame byte FF), the 50 output bytes in order (hexadecimal):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Decrypted by</th><th>Bytes at 0200 onward</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the built-in firmware, and <code>c^3 mod N</code></td><td>4E 30 BC 7F 1D FE 60 C9 C4 5B 08 1F 30 BB D4 3E 93 29 14 2F 42 6F 58 F4 4D 71 D6 64 C0 1B 65 00 2C B5 E8 5A FB A3 28 5E 70 D4 C0 90 1C 50 90 5B BA 3F</td></tr><tr><td>the core's own procedure</td><td>5F A2 86 41 86 D2 0F 33 89 01 8C 25 5A 3C BA 3C 03 E7 19 67 A4 8A B4 FA 92 C1 99 44 63 14 64 5B 65 D8 F9 2E 20 5E A7 80 1A 6C E1 92 59 B5 46 F1 18 CF</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Measured on 2 Nov 2026: a new context with no cartridge (Rule Lynx:H.9) run to 0200 with the built-in firmware, the core's <code>lynx_decrypt_frame</code> on the same block, and <code>c^3 mod N</code> computed directly. What the console's firmware gives for such a block is NOT ESTABLISHED.</p></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The modulus is published widely, and this project's copy is in its source, <code>lynxkey.h</code>, with the decryption in <code>boot.c</code>. MECHANISM; the byte order and the running sum are CONVERGENCE with the published description, checked by decrypting cc65's encrypted loader to its documented plaintext (<code>tests/lynx/boot_check.c</code>).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.4</b> (the modulus). The modulus <code>N</code>, most significant byte first (51 bytes, hexadecimal):</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>35 B5 A3 94 28 06 D8 A2 26 95 D7 71 B2 3C FD 56 1C\n4A 19 B6 A3 B0 26 00 36 5A 30 6E 3C 4D 63 38 1B D4\n1C 13 64 89 36 4C F2 BA 2A 58 F4 FE E1 FD AC 7E 79</code></pre>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.5</b> (booting with no firmware image). A core built from this guide alone can boot a cartridge with no firmware image at all, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>read the frame from page 0 of bank 0;</li><li>decrypt it as Rule Lynx:8.3 says, to 0200;</li><li>leave the machine in the state of Rule Lynx:8.13;</li><li>start at 0200.</li></ol>\n<p>A commercial loader then calls FE00 and FE4A (Rules Lynx:8.10, 8.11), so those two entry points must exist; the project's own firmware (Rule Lynx:8.8) is one way to provide them.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot--what-the-host-must-supply-no-firmware-image-is-given-here\">What the host must supply: no firmware image is given here</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.6</b> (what the host supplies). A core built from this guide runs some 512-byte boot firmware at FE00-FFFF. This tab does not carry any firmware image, and none is published on this site (neither the console's nor the project's): do not look for one. The host must supply one of:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the console's own boot firmware (512 bytes), from the user;</li><li>the project's own firmware, which our core has built in (made by the project's generator, not published), and which a core built elsewhere does not have;</li><li>firmware the builder writes from this chapter (the entry points and the state at 0200, Rules Lynx:8.10 to 8.13).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.7</b> (comparing two cores' boots). The firmware's code decides every instruction from power on to 0200, so two cores booting through different firmware differ in the trace from the first instruction and in the boot's length (ticks, and frames to the first picture), even when both are exact. To compare a core with ours tick for tick, both must run through the same firmware image (<code>lynx_set_firmware</code>); the figures in this chapter are for our built-in firmware. A program started without the boot (a BLL program, a headerless Epyx image, chapter 5) runs no firmware and compares from its first instruction.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> So a builder who writes its own firmware cannot expect the figures of \"How it decrypts quickly\" below (3,420,103 ticks to FE4A, 4,822,449 to 0200): they hold only for our firmware's code. What any firmware must match is the list of entry points and the state at 0200 (\"The state it leaves at 0200\", below), which is all a cartridge's loader sees.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot--the-projects-own-boot-firmware\">The project's own boot firmware</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.8</b> (what the built-in firmware does). The core never needs the console's firmware: without the user's own, it must run 512 bytes written for this project, which do this:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Reset: the reset vector points at FE19 (the reset sequence has set I and cleared D).</li><li>FE19: clear RAM 0000-FBFF; start the display (Timer 0 backup 9E, reload and count at 1 us; Timer 2 backup 68, reload and count, linked to Timer 0; DISPCTL 09: colour, DMA on); IODIR = 02 (the cartridge address data line an output); store 0200 in 0005/0006 (the load address); select page 0 through FE00; go on at FE4A.</li><li>FE4A: S = FF; read the frame's first byte from RCART0; decrypt each block as Rule Lynx:8.3 says and store the output bytes from the load address in 0005/0006 (which it leaves as it found them); clear its work space; JMP 0200.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> It imitates nothing of the console's firmware but the documented boot, the loaders' entry points, and the state the references agree a boot leaves (Rule Lynx:8.13).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.9</b> (the user's firmware). The user's own firmware, if supplied (512 bytes), replaces the built-in one at the next power on; the core must treat both the same way. The built-in firmware's interrupt vectors point at an RTI.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.10</b> (FE00: select a page). FE00 selects the cartridge page in A: eight bits, most significant first, each put on IODAT bit 1 (IODAT written whole: the bit in bit 1, every other bit 0) and shifted in by a rising strobe on SYSCTL1 (written 03, then 02, keeping the power bit set as Rule Lynx:4.20 requires), leaving the strobe low (so the counter starts at 0). It returns with RTS. It changes A and X (both 00 on return) and the flags, keeps Y, and uses one byte of stack below the return address; it touches no other RAM. A loader that keeps something in X across <code>JSR FE00</code> loses it.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.11</b> (the three entry points). FE00, FE19 and FE4A are an interface, not a detail: cartridge loaders call into the boot firmware. A commercial loader selects pages through FE00 and jumps back to FE4A for its next frame, having set 0005/0006.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The first Lynx compatibility ladder: every official loader ends JMP FE4A, and none boots on a firmware without it. The addresses and what they do are CONVERGENCE with the second Lynx emulator's published high-level boot, which provides the same three; nothing of the console's code is used. The loader cc65 builds (and the lynx-tests programs use) reads the program from the cartridge and starts it.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot--how-it-decrypts-quickly\">How it decrypts quickly</h5>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.12</b> (the built-in firmware's arithmetic). The built-in firmware decrypts with Suzy's maths unit (chapter 7): Montgomery multiplication in 16-bit words (26 words, R = 2^416), column by column, each 16 x 16 product added by the unit into its 32-bit accumulator (SPRSYS bit 6), the overflow bit read after each product to count the carries out. Three Montgomery products a block (c times c, times c, times the constant R^3 mod N) give c^3 mod N below 2N, and one subtraction of N finishes it. The two constants it needs (R^3 mod N and -1/N mod 2^16) follow from the public modulus. Every access to the unit comes at least 54 ticks after the product it follows started (the busy time with accumulate), so the firmware does not depend on the core computing products at once.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> MECHANISM for the arithmetic (it is the documented multiply and accumulate); the busy time is CONVERGENCE (chapter 7).</p></div>\n<p>Measured by <code>tests/lynx/boot_time.c</code> (gate 5), from power on to the first instruction at 0200, cc65's one-block loader frame (the lynx-tests cpu.lnx), on 1 Nov 2026, both models:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Firmware</th><th>Boot to 0200 (ticks from power on)</th><th>Five blocks through FE4A (ticks)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>built in</td><td>4,822,449 ticks (0.301 s), host frame 14</td><td>7,023,171 ticks (0.439 s)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Of the boot, 3,420,103 ticks come before FE4A (almost all of it the RAM clear, 50 ticks a byte) and 1,402,346 are the decryption (FE4A to 0200), 274,371 instructions from power on to 0200: a block, with the display's DMA running, costs about 1.4 million ticks (5.3 display frames). The console's own firmware shows the first picture of a commercial cartridge after 7 frames. How many blocks a commercial boot decrypts is not known here (no commercial cartridge is used), so the comparison for those is still to be made. The gate holds the boot to 16 host frames.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-08-boot--the-state-it-leaves-at-0200\">The state it leaves at 0200</h5>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.13</b> (the state at 0200). When 0200 first runs, the machine must be in this state (the third column says how sure each item is):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Left</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RAM 0000-FBFF</td><td>00, but for 0005/0006 (00 02 after the boot) and the loaded frame</td><td>CONVERGENCE: the second Lynx emulator's high-level boot clears all RAM at FE19; the Lynx reference emulator clears it when it starts a program without the boot. A web search's summary of a public page on the Lynx (sizecoding.org; the page itself could not be read from here) also says the console's boot clears main memory after decrypting, apart from its own work area</td></tr><tr><td>Display timers</td><td>Timer 0: backup 9E, reload and count, 1 us; Timer 2: backup 68, reload and count, linked to Timer 0</td><td>CONVERGENCE (both references, the same values)</td></tr><tr><td>DISPCTL</td><td>09 (colour, DMA on)</td><td>CONVERGENCE (both references)</td></tr><tr><td>IODIR</td><td>02</td><td>MECHANISM (the page select needs the data line as an output)</td></tr><tr><td>IODAT</td><td>00 after selecting page 0 (the last data bit 0)</td><td>follows from the page select</td></tr><tr><td>0005/0006</td><td>the load address, as set before FE4A (0200 after the boot)</td><td>CONVERGENCE (the second Lynx emulator's high-level boot)</td></tr><tr><td>MAPCTL</td><td>00 (as after reset)</td><td>CONVERGENCE (the second Lynx emulator's high-level boot leaves it); the loaders need the firmware visible for FE00 and FE4A</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td>FF</td><td>The Lynx reference emulator's start without the boot sets FF; NOT ESTABLISHED for the console</td></tr><tr><td>SPRSYS</td><td>04 written (accumulate and signed maths off, the unsafe bit cleared)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (the references' boots do not use the maths unit); chosen to leave the unit as at power on</td></tr><tr><td>Suzy's maths registers</td><td>JKLM 0; AB, CD, EFGH as the last product left them</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td></tr><tr><td>Work space</td><td>page 1 (0100-01FF) and zero page AA-AF cleared at the end of every loader frame (each pass through FE4A, just before its JMP 0200; not a display frame)</td><td>chosen so that no byte of the firmware's own work is left behind</td></tr><tr><td>PBKUP, palette, DISPADR</td><td>not written (power-on values)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED: only the Lynx reference emulator writes PBKUP (29) and the palette (00)</td></tr><tr><td>A, X, Y</td><td>A 04, X FF, Y the frame's total bytes stored (every block's), less 1, as a byte (hexadecimal 31, decimal 49, for a one-block frame of 50 bytes; hexadecimal 63 for two blocks); P B5 as the trace prints it (N, I and C set)</td><td>what our built-in firmware's last instructions leave; NOT ESTABLISHED for the console (the Lynx reference emulator's start without the boot sets 0)</td></tr><tr><td>I, D</td><td>I set, D clear (from reset)</td><td>MECHANISM (the reset sequence)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:8.14</b> (byte 0001). The zero-page byte at 0001 must be 00 when 0200 runs (the RAM clear; the clear uses 0005/0006 as its pointer, so it leaves nothing behind).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The lynx-tests timers program adds a count to that byte without clearing it (chapter 10); with this boot it reads as on hardware.</p></div></section>","09-uart":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart\">9. The UART</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: the UART's own unit is the <b>bit time</b> (Rule Lynx:9.3), counted from the bit time at which a countdown is set; other times are in <b>ticks</b> (the 16 MHz system clock) or microseconds (16 ticks each), counted in Mikey's time (the glossary's <code>mikey_t</code>) or, with two consoles, in the pair's common time (Rule Lynx:9.16). Register addresses and values are hexadecimal; bit numbers, counts and rates are decimal.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.1</b> (the wire and the loopback). The UART sends and receives ComLynx frames on one open-collector wire shared by every console on the cable. With one console, only its own frames are on the wire, and its receiver must hear them (the <b>loopback</b>).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The rules here are HARDWARE-OBSERVED through lynx-tests uart, uart2 and uart3 unless marked; the structure is CONVERGENCE with the reference.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--registers\">Registers</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.2</b> (SERCTL and SERDAT).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bit</th><th>SERCTL (FD8C) write</th><th>SERCTL read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7</td><td>TXINTEN</td><td>TXRDY</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>RXINTEN</td><td>RXRDY</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td></td><td>TXEMPTY</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>PAREN</td><td>PARERR</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>RESETERR</td><td>OVERRUN</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>TXOPEN</td><td>FRAMERR</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>TXBRK</td><td>RXBRK</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>PAREVEN</td><td>PARBIT (the received parity bit)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>PARERR, FRAMERR, RXBRK and PARBIT are those of the byte in the data register, and read 0 while no byte is held. SERDAT (FD8D): a write sends a byte; a read takes the received byte.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--the-bit-clock\">The bit clock</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.3</b> (bit times). Timer 4's terminal borrows clock the UART. While TXBRK is set, the clock stops (and TXEMPTY is 0): nothing samples the wire and the prescaler holds its count, so the receiver does not see the break itself; RXBRK arises only from a frame that ends with a low stop bit and all data 0. Otherwise a prescaler counts the borrows modulo 8; each time it returns to 0 is a <b>bit time</b>, and at each bit time, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the receiver samples the wire (Rule Lynx:9.9);</li><li>the transmitter steps (Rule Lynx:9.7).</li></ol>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--frames\">Frames</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.4</b> (a frame). 11 bits, least significant first: start (0), data bits 0-7, the parity bit, stop (1). The parity bit:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>PAREN</th><th>PAREVEN</th><th>Parity bit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>set</td><td>set</td><td>even parity of the data (the bit makes the count of ones even)</td></tr><tr><td>set</td><td>clear</td><td>odd parity of the data</td></tr><tr><td>clear</td><td>either</td><td>PAREVEN itself</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--transmitter\">Transmitter</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.5</b> (state and the wire). State: a holding register (with a full flag), whether a frame is going, its 11 bits and the index of the bit on the wire, and three countdowns in bit times: start wait, ready wait, empty wait. The wire is low while TXBRK is set; otherwise high unless a frame is going and its start wait is over, in which case it carries the frame's current bit.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.6</b> (a SERDAT write). If no frame is going and TXBRK is clear, the frame starts at once: index 0, start wait 1, ready wait 2, not chained, TXRDY 0, TXEMPTY 0. Otherwise the byte goes to the holding register (full), TXRDY 0, TXEMPTY 0.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.7</b> (the transmitter at a bit time).</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>No frame going:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If the holding register is full: if a ready wait is running it counts down and, unless it reaches 0, nothing more happens; otherwise the held byte's frame starts (index 0, no start wait), chained, and TXRDY becomes 1.</li><li>Otherwise (the holding register is empty), if an empty wait is running, it counts down and TXEMPTY becomes 1 when it reaches 0.</li></ol></li><li>A frame going:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If TXRDY is 0 and a ready wait is running, it counts down and TXRDY becomes 1 at 0.</li><li>Then, if the start wait is running, it counts down and nothing more happens.</li><li>Otherwise the index advances; when it reaches 11 the frame is over: if the holding register is full, its byte's frame starts at once (chained, TXRDY 1, waits cleared); else TXRDY is 1, and TXEMPTY becomes 1 at this bit time if this frame was chained, or after an empty wait of 1 bit time if not.</li></ol></li></ul>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.8</b> (a SERCTL write). The register is stored. RESETERR clears OVERRUN and the error bits (PARERR, FRAMERR, RXBRK) of both received bytes, keeping PARBIT. Then the first row that applies:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TXBRK is set</td><td>TXRDY and TXEMPTY 0</td></tr><tr><td>TXBRK was set before this write, no frame is going and the holding register is full</td><td>a ready wait of 3 bit times</td></tr><tr><td>no frame is going and the holding register is empty</td><td>TXRDY and TXEMPTY 1</td></tr><tr><td>otherwise</td><td>nothing more</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>TXOPEN (driving the cable) matters only with a second console (Rule Lynx:9.14); the loopback is heard either way.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--receiver\">Receiver</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.9</b> (receiving a frame). At each bit time the receiver samples the wire: idle, a low sample starts a frame; then 8 data bits, least significant first; then the parity bit; then the stop bit, which completes the frame. The completed byte's flags:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PARBIT</td><td>the parity bit received</td></tr><tr><td>PARERR</td><td>it differs from the parity bit expected for the data, the expected bit being the one this console's own SERCTL would send with that data (with PAREN clear, PAREVEN itself). It is compared on every frame, so a PARERR is possible with PAREN clear too (another console sending with other settings)</td></tr><tr><td>FRAMERR</td><td>the stop bit was low</td></tr><tr><td>RXBRK</td><td>the stop bit was low and the data 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.10</b> (where a completed byte goes). The receiver holds up to two bytes: the data register and one waiting behind it. Each completed frame's time is the Mikey time of its stop bit's sample, and the receiver remembers only the time of the last frame completed (whatever became of that frame; a read of SERDAT does not change it). The first row that applies:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bytes held</th><th>Last frame completed</th><th>The new byte</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>none</td><td>any</td><td>goes to the data register</td></tr><tr><td>one (the data register's)</td><td>at least 800 us (12,800 ticks) before this one</td><td>waits behind it</td></tr><tr><td>one, too recent; or two</td><td></td><td>replaces the newest byte held (the waiting one when there are two, else the data register's), and OVERRUN is set</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Either way its time becomes the last frame's time.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The 800 us is CONVERGENCE (the reference fitted it); the circuit behind it is NOT ESTABLISHED.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.11</b> (RXRDY and reading SERDAT). RXRDY is 1 while a byte is held. Reading SERDAT returns the data register's byte and moves the waiting byte, if any, forward; with no byte held it returns the last byte the data register held.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--the-interrupt\">The interrupt</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.12</b> (the UART's interrupt). The UART's interrupt is Mikey's bit 4 (timer 4 never interrupts itself). After any UART change, a SERCTL or SERDAT access, and a write to INTRST: if (TXINTEN and TXRDY) or (RXINTEN and RXRDY), pending bit 4 is set (a level: clearing it through INTRST while the condition holds sets it again). Its enable is TXINTEN or RXINTEN (either one).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-09-uart--two-consoles-the-comlynx-cable\">Two consoles: the ComLynx cable</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.13</b> (linking). <code>lynx_link(a, b)</code> puts two contexts on one cable (<a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE-LYNX.md</code></a>).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.14</b> (the wire). The cable is one open-collector wire: low while either console pulls it low. A console drives it only with TXOPEN (SERCTL bit 2) set: low while TXBRK is set, else the level of its frame going out, as Rule Lynx:9.5 says (high between frames). A console's own receiver hears its own frames either way (the loopback), and the other console's through the cable: at each bit time the receiver's level is its own transmitter's AND the other console's.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE with the Lynx reference emulator (read, not run: its two-console link lives in its desktop front end, which the oracle harness does not build), which puts a console's frames on the cable only with TXOPEN; MECHANISM for the wired AND.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.15</b> (where the other console is sampled). A receiver must take the other console's level as it stood half a bit time before its own sample, half a bit being 4 Timer 4 periods: <code>4 x (BACKUP + 1) x (16 &lt;&lt; clock)</code> ticks with Timer 4's own BACKUP and clock; with Timer 4 linked, clock 7, the half bit is 0 and the other console is taken as it stands at the sample. The sample's time is this console's <code>mikey_t</code> plus its offset (Rule Lynx:9.16), less the half bit; a sample time below 0 is taken as 0. The level before the oldest of the 16 recorded changes is the opposite of that oldest change's level (each record is a change); a record at time 0 counts as standing at time 0, so a cleared record (Rule Lynx:9.16) always gives its level.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The receiver is taken to sample mid-bit: the loopback's hardware-observed timing is untouched (its own frames change exactly at its own bit times), and a sample of the other console never falls on that console's bit edge, however the two bit clocks lie. NOT ESTABLISHED on hardware (a real receiver presumably finds the start bit's edge and samples the middle of each bit; this is the same for any offset of the two clocks under half a bit).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.16</b> (lockstep: common time). Each console records the times its driven level changes (the last 16), in the pair's common time. The joining is by an offset, never by running: each console's common time is its own <code>t</code> plus an offset. The recorded changes and the samples (Rule Lynx:9.15) happen inside Mikey's run, so they use <code>mikey_t</code> plus the offset; the choice of which console steps next (Rule Lynx:9.17) uses <code>t</code> plus the offset.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Offset set</th><th>Records cleared</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>lynx_link</code></td><td>both offsets, so that both common times equal the larger of the two <code>t</code> (the console behind joins the other's time)</td><td>both consoles'</td></tr><tr><td>a power on of a linked console (its <code>t</code> back to 0)</td><td>its offset, to the other's common time</td><td>that console's own</td></tr><tr><td>a state load of a linked console</td><td>its offset, so that its loaded <code>t</code> lands on the other's common time</td><td>that console's own</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_unlink</code></td><td>none</td><td>none (they are not read without a cable)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Nothing is run by any of these. A cleared record holds the console's present driven level as standing since the beginning of time (16 entries at time 0 with that level), so the other console reads that level for any earlier moment.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.17</b> (stepping a pair). <code>lynx_link_step</code> runs one instruction (or one step of sleep) of whichever console is behind in common time (the first on a tie), so the two part by at most one step; <code>lynx_link_run_frame(a)</code> runs the pair so until <code>a</code> starts a frame, then brings the other up to the same time.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> A sample at common time <code>s</code> needs the other console to have reached <code>s - half a bit</code>; an ordinary instruction is under 100 ticks (the checks measure 42 at most for consoles idling with the display on), and half a bit is never shorter than 128 ticks at the usual rates, so this holds. A bit is 8 Timer 4 periods, <code>8 x (BACKUP + 1) x (16 &lt;&lt; clock)</code> ticks, half a bit 4 of them:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Timer 4 clock, BACKUP</th><th>Ticks a bit</th><th>Half a bit (ticks)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>62500 baud</td><td>1 us (clock 0), 1</td><td>256</td><td>128</td></tr><tr><td>31250 baud</td><td>clock 0, 3</td><td>512</td><td>256</td></tr><tr><td>9600 baud (about 9,615 at the nearest BACKUP)</td><td>clock 0, 12</td><td>1664</td><td>832</td></tr><tr><td>slower</td><td></td><td>larger still</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>INSTRUMENT-MEASURED exception: a program that rewrites the display timers can be charged a burst of display DMA at once (up to 1344 ticks in one instruction, seen in cc65's loader); while the other console catches up from such a step its samples may see the line late. Measured over 40 million steps of uart4 and sprites4 on a linked pair: about 300 steps with a gap over 128 ticks, all at such moments. It matters only if a frame is on the wire just then.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:9.18</b> (NOEXP). IODAT bit 2 read as an input tells whether a cable is in: Rule Lynx:4.22 (its bit 2 row) gives the values.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> CONVERGENCE with the Lynx reference emulator; the second Lynx emulator reads it the other way round; the name, \"no expansion\", agrees with the Lynx reference emulator.</p></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation (checked).</b> <code>tests/lynx/gate6.sh</code>: lynx-tests uart4, the two-console hardware test, on two linked consoles switched on at several offsets, both models: every judged byte as recorded on hardware on both consoles except byte 3 (below); the project's link program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/link</code>: sixteen bytes each way at 62500 baud with parity, each console eating its own echoes) at four offsets and both models; and <code>homebrew_checks.c</code>: bytes each way at three rates, a frame without TXOPEN not reaching the other console, parity judged by the receiver's setting, the link kept over a power on and a state load, unlink, and the gap between the consoles.</p></div>\n<p><b>Known deviation: uart4 byte 3</b> (test 2, OWN ECHO). Both consoles send eight bytes back to back at the same moment, draining once per byte sent, and count the frames they then hold: the hardware counted at least eight on each console; this core counts seven on both, at every offset tried.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The reason is the receiver's two-byte rule (Rule Lynx:9.10: a frame lands behind the data register only if that byte has been there 800 us): with the other console's frames lying on its own, each console's wire looks exactly as with one console, where uart3 test 3 (BURST ECHO, HARDWARE-OBSERVED) shows the last frame overwriting the one before, so the same seven. The hardware must therefore keep a frame the other console contributed to; the Lynx reference emulator's source shows the fit its author made for this (a frame during which the other console pulled the line low while this one's own level was high is always kept behind the data register), but here the two consoles' frames start together (both start from the same received frame, their bit clocks well under half a bit apart), and with the other console sampled mid-bit no such bit arises; the Lynx reference emulator samples the other console at the very edge, so there the console behind sees the other's next bit and keeps its frames, the console ahead does not. The circuit is NOT ESTABLISHED; the gate holds the byte at 01 so that a change is noticed. The choice is left open.</p></div></section>","10-not-established":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-10-not-established-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-10-not-established\">10. What is not established</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: each row is a choice this core makes where the console's behaviour is not known to this project; the rule that binds it is in the chapter named in the last column, in that chapter's units. A different choice is not wrong; it would only make a core differ from ours. Result bytes are hexadecimal; byte numbers and counts are decimal.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:10.1</b> (the choices). A core that is to match ours must make these choices:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Our choice</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RAM at power on</td><td>all 00</td><td>chapter 1</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial cartridges on the project's own firmware</td><td>FE00, FE19 and FE4A as chapter 8 says</td><td>chapter 8. Every title boots, but two of Atari's service cartridges (testaudio, testcontrols) boot only on the console's firmware. The firmware leaves the state the references agree on (RAM cleared, the display started); whether that is what those two need is not known without them</td></tr><tr><td>What the project's firmware leaves beyond the references' common state</td><td>SPRSYS 04, JKLM 0, its work space (page 1, zero page AA-AF) cleared, A, X, Y as they fall, PBKUP, palette and DISPADR not written</td><td>chapter 8</td></tr><tr><td>DISPCTL bit 1 (flip)</td><td>the display reads its buffer backwards from the latched address + 3, each byte's low pen first (the second Lynx emulator's reading; the oracle ignores the bit): the picture turned half a turn. A flip changed mid-frame takes effect at the next DMA burst</td><td>chapter 4</td></tr><tr><td>The CPU's and Mikey's relative phase at power on</td><td>t = 0 at the first opcode fetch, Mikey's phase 0, refresh 0</td><td>chapter 1</td></tr><tr><td>The display state at power on</td><td>outside vertical blank, line 0, nothing drawn</td><td>chapter 1</td></tr><tr><td>Whether internal (dummy) CPU cycles reach registers</td><td>they do not</td><td>chapter 1</td></tr><tr><td>The decimal-mode extra cycle's address on the Lynx</td><td>0059 (ADC), 0000 (SBC): the Rockwell part's</td><td>chapter 2</td></tr><tr><td>The Lynx I's bit-instruction no-ops' length</td><td>one byte</td><td>chapter 2</td></tr><tr><td>CPUSLEEP's value</td><td>any write sleeps (programs write 0; the reference sleeps only on 0)</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>The order of a sprite's writes against the display</td><td>row by row, each row's pixels at the start of that row's time</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>Sprite timing outside the forty tested cases</td><td>the fitted model</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>A packed row expanded (cc65's screen clear and bars)</td><td>the reference's rule: 21 + 3.65 a packet + 2.45 an output (ticks), 21 ticks lost to each display burst</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>The sprite engine's loss to each display DMA burst, by row kind</td><td>fitted to the reference's grid (one hardware timing, an expansion row, constrains it)</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>A destination byte both read and written</td><td>2 ticks more on the RAM path (the guide's measured read/write merge, stated for 2-4 bpp literal rows; applied to every row)</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>A skipped sprite's time</td><td>10 ticks (the reference's, and its header at the guide's reload rate)</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>When within a sprite's time SPRSYS's stop request is taken</td><td>at the end of that sprite, however late in its time the request comes (as the oracle)</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>A sprite list longer than one second</td><td>stopped at one second of its time</td><td>chapter 7</td></tr><tr><td>The stereo attenuation's scale</td><td>n/15</td><td>chapter 6</td></tr><tr><td>The analogue sound output</td><td>the mix averaged per sample, no filter</td><td>chapter 6</td></tr><tr><td>The UART's 800 us receive rule</td><td>as the reference fitted it</td><td>chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>Where a receiver samples the other console on the cable</td><td>half a bit before its own sample (mid-bit)</td><td>chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>Which frames the receiver keeps behind a full data register when two consoles transmit</td><td>the one-console rule (uart4 byte 3 differs: Rule Lynx:10.2)</td><td>chapter 9</td></tr><tr><td>Cartridge RAM (writable banks)</td><td>the references' three forms (LNX2 banks, the Lynx reference emulator's 8 KB, the second Lynx emulator's bank 1), as they define them; no cartridge is known to carry any</td><td>chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>An LNX2 file of another version</td><td>refused (the Lynx reference emulator reads such a file as headerless)</td><td>chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>SD card cartridges</td><td>the card not emulated; the file runs as an ordinary cartridge</td><td>chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>A cartridge EEPROM's write time</td><td>2 ms</td><td>chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>How Epyx's development hardware started headerless images</td><td>the loader decrypted to 0200, run as a BLL program (the Lynx reference emulator's way)</td><td>chapter 5</td></tr><tr><td>What the BLL uploader leaves in the machine</td><td>the references' common start (display timers, MAPCTL 0C, S FF)</td><td>chapter 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-10-not-established--known-deviations-from-the-hardware-tests\">Known deviations from the hardware tests</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:10.2</b> (the one deviation). One result byte of the lynx-tests programs differs from the value recorded on hardware, and a core that matches ours gives the same value:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Byte</th><th>Ours</th><th>Hardware</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>uart4 (two consoles)</td><td>3</td><td>01</td><td>00</td><td>whether a console accounts for its own eight frames while the other console sends eight at the same moment (chapter 9)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> It is an open question, and <code>tests/lynx/gate6.sh</code> holds it exactly as it is so that any change is noticed.</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:10.3</b> (two tests that depend on the boot). Two tests depend on the boot as well as on what they measure. With the project's built-in firmware every judged byte of both is as on hardware, and <code>tests/lynx/gate5.sh</code> requires that:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>What else it depends on</th><th>With the built-in firmware</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>timers</td><td>it counts timer 5's interrupts, in a polling loop, into a zero-page byte (0001) that it never clears, so its result is the count plus whatever the boot left there, and the count itself depends on where in the timers' phase the program starts</td><td>the firmware leaves 00 at 0001 (it clears RAM, as the references do), the count is 13, and byte 14 reads 0D, as on hardware: timers 16/16</td></tr><tr><td>uart</td><td>its timings depend on when the program starts relative to the UART's bit clock</td><td>every judged byte as on hardware (24/24)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> With a boot of about the console's shape the count is 13 and 0001 is 00, so the reading that the console's firmware might leave 01 at 0001 is not needed. That the two tests agree is evidence about the boot's length and start state only in the weak sense that a boot of a similar length gives the hardware's values; a test that moves with the boot's length cannot by itself settle what the console's firmware does.</p></div></section>","11-interface":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface\">11. The interface and the board</h3>\n<p>How to read this chapter: <code>ticks</code> are periods of the 16 MHz system clock counted from power on (chapter 1); frames are calls of <code>lynx_run_frame</code>, counted from 0 at power on in the runner's output; instructions are counted from power on. Hex digits in the trace and the hashes are as each rule says; every other number is decimal.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--the-functions\">The functions</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.1</b> (the functions). A core must expose the functions of the host interface, which is on this tab after the chapters (\"Core interface: Atari Lynx\", <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE-LYNX.md</code></a>): create, load a cartridge, supply firmware, choose the model, power on, run a frame or an instruction, the picture (<code>LYNX_W</code> x <code>LYNX_H</code> = 160 x 102, 12-bit colours as the palette holds them: green 11-8, blue 7-4, red 3-0), the buttons, the sound (44,100 stereo pairs a second), save states, a trace line and RAM peeks. Its section \"The C header\" gives every C type, constant name and value, so a core compiled against its own header is called the same way ours is.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--the-trace-line\">The trace line</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.2</b> (the trace line's text). <code>lynx_trace_line(ctx, out, cap)</code> must write, for the instruction about to run (or the sleep step or interrupt entry about to happen), seven fields separated by single spaces, with nothing before the first or after the last:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ticks</code></td><td>decimal with no leading zeros: the time of the next access, <code>t</code> plus any <code>stall</code> owed, uncapped</td></tr><tr><td>PC</td><td>four hex digits</td></tr><tr><td>A, X, Y, S</td><td>two hex digits each</td></tr><tr><td>P</td><td>two hex digits, printed with bits 5 and 4 both set (P OR 30), whatever B would be</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hex digits are upper case. The text ends with a NUL byte and no newline; the function returns the number of characters before the NUL, or -1 (writing nothing) if <code>cap</code> is less than that number plus 1. Two cores that agree tick for tick produce identical trace files.</p>\n<p>Worked example: our core's first line after a cartridge load is <code>0 FE19 00 00 00 FD 34</code> (the built-in firmware's reset target, S after the reset sequence, I set). The runner writes each line followed by one newline (<code>\\n</code>).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--the-runner\">The runner</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.3</b> (what the runner calls). The project's runner (a command-line program, not published; described here so that a builder can write the same) drives a core from the command line: <code>lynx_run &lt;cart&gt; &lt;command&gt; ... [model=1|2] [firmware=FILE] [input=FILE]</code>. It uses only the interface's functions and constants, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>lynx_create</code>;</li><li><code>lynx_set_model</code> and <code>lynx_set_firmware</code> for the options;</li><li><code>lynx_load_cartridge</code> (which powers on);</li><li>as the command needs it, <code>lynx_eeprom_write</code>, <code>lynx_set_cart_ram</code> and <code>lynx_cart_ram_write</code> for the <code>_in</code> and <code>cartram_kind</code> options;</li><li>the command.</li></ol>\n<p>Before each frame f it applies the input file's line for f (<code>lynx_set_buttons</code>), then calls <code>lynx_run_frame</code>. <code>trace</code> alternates <code>lynx_trace_line</code> (the line written with a newline) and <code>lynx_step_instruction</code>, from power on. <code>ram</code> reads the 64 KB with <code>lynx_peek</code>. <code>hashes</code> is Rule Lynx:11.5. (Our runner also has a <code>start</code> command, for our own records, which looks inside the context; nothing scored uses it.)</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.4</b> (the commands).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>frames &lt;from&gt; &lt;to&gt; &lt;dir&gt;</code></td><td><code>frame_NNNNN.rgb</code>, 160 x 102 x 3 bytes, each component 0-15 (red, green, blue)</td></tr><tr><td><code>trace &lt;n&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>n trace lines</td></tr><tr><td><code>ram &lt;frames&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>the 64 KB after that many frames</td></tr><tr><td><code>audio &lt;frames&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>the sound, int16 stereo pairs</td></tr><tr><td><code>audregs &lt;n&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>the sound registers at each of n instructions where one changed: a text file, one line for the first instruction and one for each later instruction at which the ten bytes of <code>lynx_audio_regs</code> differ from the last line's, each line the trace line's <code>ticks</code> field, then the ten bytes as two upper-case hex digits each, every field after a single space, a newline at the end. Not used by the comparison of Rule Lynx:11.6</td></tr><tr><td><code>eeprom &lt;frames&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>the cartridge EEPROM's contents after that many frames (<code>eeprom_in=FILE</code> starts from given contents)</td></tr><tr><td><code>cartram &lt;frames&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>the kept cartridge RAM after that many frames (<code>cartram_in=FILE</code> starts from given contents; <code>cartram_kind=N</code> calls <code>lynx_set_cart_ram(N)</code> after loading)</td></tr><tr><td><code>info</code></td><td>the header's facts: rotation, model, EEPROM, format, cartridge RAM, the model the file asks for, the load reason</td></tr><tr><td><code>link &lt;frames&gt; &lt;ramA&gt; &lt;ramB&gt;</code></td><td>two consoles on the ComLynx cable: the first runs <code>delay=F</code> frames and <code>delayi=N</code> instructions alone, then the second (<code>peer=FILE</code>, default the same file; <code>inputb=FILE</code> its buttons) is switched on and linked; the pair runs <code>&lt;frames&gt;</code> frames of the first; both RAMs are written</td></tr><tr><td><code>hashes &lt;frames&gt;</code></td><td>per frame: <code>&lt;frame&gt; &lt;picture&gt; &lt;sound&gt; &lt;RAM&gt; &lt;ticks&gt;</code>, FNV-1a 64 (hex) of the picture's bytes (uint16 little endian), the frame's sound pairs, and the 64 KB of <code>lynx_peek</code>; ticks decimal (Rule Lynx:11.5)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>An input file holds lines <code>&lt;frame&gt; &lt;buttons&gt;</code>, applied before that frame.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.5</b> (the <code>hashes</code> line). One per frame after that frame's <code>lynx_run_frame</code>: <code>&lt;frame&gt; &lt;picture&gt; &lt;sound&gt; &lt;RAM&gt; &lt;ticks&gt;</code>, single spaces, a newline at the end. <code>frame</code> counts from 0, decimal. The three hashes are FNV-1a style, 64 bits, one byte at a time (XOR the byte, then multiply by 1099511628211 modulo 2^64), each starting from 1469598103934665603 (hexadecimal 14650FB0739D0383: note that this is not the usual FNV offset basis, which has one more digit; the runner uses this value and the comparisons depend on it), each printed as exactly 16 lower-case hex digits:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Hash</th><th>Bytes, in order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>picture</td><td>the 160 x 102 picture as <code>lynx_raster</code> holds it, row by row, each pixel's uint16 as two bytes, low byte first (32,640 bytes)</td></tr><tr><td>sound</td><td>one <code>lynx_audio_read</code> of up to 8,192 pairs made after the frame, each pair left then right, each int16 low byte first (4 bytes a pair; the pairs waiting, normally the frame's)</td></tr><tr><td>RAM</td><td>the 64 KB from <code>lynx_peek(0)</code> to <code>lynx_peek(FFFF)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>ticks</code> is <code>lynx_ticks</code> after the frame, decimal.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--the-test-programs\">The test programs</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The programs the project runs (paths are the project's own, not published):</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The lynx-tests hardware test programs (<code>build/lynx/hw/*.lnx</code>, built by <code>tests/programs-lynx/build.sh</code> from the pinned sources): cpu, memio, page-mode, timers, timers2, math, sdoneack, sprites1-5, audio, audio2, uart, uart2, uart3, uart4 (two consoles: <code>lynx_run</code>'s link command) and refresh-rate (interactive). Each stores its measurements in <code>g_results</code> (its symbol file gives the address) and draws a verdict screen: green PASS or red failure numbers. <code>tests/lynx/hwexpect.py</code> checks the bytes against the values recorded on hardware in the test's source; <code>tools/compare/lynx_hwscore.py</code> reads a verdict screen; <code>tests/lynx/sprite_timing.py</code> scores the forty sprite timings and CRCs.</li><li>The project's sound program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/sound</code>), built to <code>build/lynx/sound.lnx</code>.</li><li>The project's BLL program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/bll</code>, built to <code>build/lynx/bll.o</code>), EEPROM program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/eeprom</code>, built to <code>build/lynx/eeprom46.lnx</code>, <code>eeprom66.lnx</code>, <code>eeprom86.lnx</code>, the header's byte 60 set for each part) and ComLynx link program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/link</code>, <code>build/lynx/link.lnx</code>), chapters 5 and 9.</li><li>The project's cartridge RAM program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/cartram</code>, built to <code>build/lynx/cartram.lnx</code> and the LNX2 files <code>cartram8k.lnx</code> and <code>cartramx.lnx</code> made from it) and sprite stop program (<code>tests/programs-lynx/sprstop</code>, <code>build/lynx/sprstop.lnx</code>), chapters 5 and 7. The oracle harness has <code>cartram &lt;frames&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code> and <code>cartram_in=FILE</code> to match.</li><li>The project's boot firmware, built into our core (chapter 8; not published, and no firmware image is on this tab). A run that names <code>firmware=FILE</code> boots through that file instead, which is how two cores are compared through the same firmware.</li></ul></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--the-instruments-and-the-gates\">The instruments and the gates</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The project's gates:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>What it checks</th><th>Script</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>the CPU: the per-cycle suite (both models), Klaus's tests, the no-op and interrupt-poll checks</td><td><code>tests/lynx/gate1.sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Suzy: the sprite tests' CRCs and timings, maths, sleep, the verdict screens</td><td><code>tests/lynx/gate3.sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>sound: audio and audio2, the sound registers tick for tick against the reference, the mix</td><td><code>tests/lynx/gate4.sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>the UART, the cartridge port, the firmware choice</td><td><code>tests/lynx/gate5.sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>homebrew and the rarer cartridges: BLL programs, the display flip, cartridge EEPROMs, two consoles on the ComLynx cable</td><td><code>tests/lynx/gate6.sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>cartridge RAM (LNX2 banks, the host's 8 KB and the second Lynx emulator's bank 1), the LNX2 and newer .lnx header fields, SPRSYS's stop request</td><td><code>tests/lynx/gate7.sh</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each gate also runs its mutations (deliberate errors, each of which must fail the gate), and the two machines (native and wasm) are compared by <code>tests/machines_lynx.sh</code>.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--comparing-two-cores\">Comparing two cores</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.6</b> (when two cores agree). Two cores agree when <code>lynx_run hashes</code> (or an equivalent) gives identical lines for every test program of \"The test programs\" on both models, over the same number of frames. The clean-room rounds use 450 frames of every program, on both models: per-frame hashes (picture, sound, RAM, ticks) for frames 0 to 449, instruction traces of the first 200,000 instructions, and RAM after 450 frames. (The 120 frames of the test cartridges' read-me, Rule Lynx:11.8, is when their expected bytes are read, not the comparison's length.)</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--the-comparison-build-tools-only\">The comparison build (tools only)</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> <code>build/lynx_refrun</code> is the same sources compiled with <code>LYNX_REFERENCE</code>: it reproduces the reference emulator's instruction-level scheduling so that traces compare tick for tick with the reference's. It is not the shipped core and a clean-room core need not reproduce it. It differs in: page mode off until a MAPCTL write changes MAPCTL; the reference's CPU simplifications (no page-crossing cycle on indexed reads and shifts, JSR's high byte before the pushes, BRK's padding byte not fetched, the reserved zero page no-ops making no read, 5C taking 8 cycles where the per-cycle suite, and the shipped core, take 4); Mikey run once per instruction with its accesses timed from the instruction's end; refresh paid per instruction; one 28-tick display burst charged per instruction, and, while the CPU sleeps, one per 8-tick sleep step (the step lengthened by it, the sprite engine losing its exposure to it, chapter 7), so that bursts met in a sleep are not left for the CPU to pay when it wakes; CPUSLEEP sleeping only on a write of 0. The reference itself runs with a patch (in <code>tools/oracle-lynx/</code>) that fixes its random power-on phase at 0 and loads the project's firmware.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-11-interface--what-a-build-from-this-page-can-run\">What a build from this page can run</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.7</b> (what can be fetched). The lynx-tests hardware test programs are published by their author (Ignacio Sanchez Gines, MIT licence, github.com/drhelius/lynx-tests), with the values recorded on a real Lynx in each test's source. Our own Lynx test cartridges are published here, with their expected results:</p>\n<p><b>Download:</b> <a href=\"/downloads/atarilynx-tests/atarilynx-test-cartridges.zip\" download>our 10 Lynx test cartridges in one zip</a>, MIT licence, with their source (they build with cc65 2.19, byte for byte).</p>\n<p>The zip is a separate file on the site, not part of this tab's text: a copy of the tab alone (as a clean-room builder is given) does not hold it, nor the lynx-tests programs, which are fetched from their author. Without them, the worked values in each chapter and the boot state of chapter 8 are what a build can be checked against.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:11.8</b> (the test cartridges in the zip).</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The BLL program <code>bll.o</code> and the cartridge EEPROM (<code>eeprom46.lnx</code>, <code>eeprom66.lnx</code>, <code>eeprom86.lnx</code>), cartridge RAM (<code>cartram.lnx</code>, <code>cartram8k.lnx</code>, <code>cartramx.lnx</code>), ComLynx link (<code>link.lnx</code>, two consoles), sprite stop (<code>sprstop.lnx</code>) and power-off (<code>poweroff.lnx</code>) programs of chapters 5, 7 and 9.</li><li>They are not self-checking: each leaves its readings in RAM, in <code>g_results</code> (the address is in its <code>.sym</code> file), and the zip's read-me lists the bytes each must hold after 120 frames (the BLL program's after 60). No real console has been measured with them; our core and the reference give these bytes, except the power-off program's byte 2 (the reference runs on to the end of the frame).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The other programs of \"The test programs\" (the sound, timing and boot-state programs) are measurements compared with the reference, with no expected result of their own, and are not published. A build can also check the boot (chapter 8 prints the modulus and the state the boot leaves) and the worked values in each chapter.</p></div></section>","opcodes65c02-py":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-opcodes65c02-py\">Generator: <code>opcodes65c02.py</code></h3><p>65C02 opcode table generator (the Lynx CPU). The full source follows; run it with Python 3 (<code>python3 opcodes65c02.py</code>). It is part of the guide: the tables it prints are the tables the core uses.</p><pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"65C02 opcode table generator (the Lynx CPU).\n\nPart of the documentation and the single source of the table: run it to\nprint the table as text, or with --c to emit the C header the core compiles\n(src/generated/opcode_table_65c02.h).\n\nHow to read this listing: opcodes are hexadecimal ($x3 is any opcode whose\nlow digit is 3); a \"cycle\" is one CPU cycle, one bus access (chapter 2);\nlengths are in bytes. Each model's rule comes first, its evidence\n(Explanation) in brackets after it.\n\nThe Lynx CPU is a CMOS 65C02 core. Both models are listed:\n\n  Lynx II  the Rockwell 65C02 instruction set (the 65C02 additions plus the\n           bit instructions RMB, SMB, BBR, BBS), except that CB and DB are\n           one-byte, one-cycle no-ops like the rest of columns 3 and B\n           (HARDWARE-OBSERVED: lynx-tests cpu, test 8, runs every $x3 and\n           $xB opcode as a one-byte no-op on both models).\n  Lynx I   the same, except that the 32 bit-instruction opcodes (columns 7\n           and F) are one-byte, one-cycle no-ops too (HARDWARE-OBSERVED:\n           lynx-tests cpu, test 7: on a Lynx I RMB0, SMB0, BBR0 and BBS0 do\n           nothing; that they are one byte long is CONVERGENCE with the\n           reference emulator, the test being written so that either length\n           passes).\n\nModes:\n  imp acc imm zp zpx zpy abs absx absy indx indy  as the 6502\n  izp   (zp)       the 65C02's zero page indirect, no index\n  ind   (abs)      JMP only; the pointer's high byte is read from the\n                   next address, crossing a page (the 6502's page wrap\n                   does not happen)\n  iax   (abs,X)    JMP only\n  rel              branches, and BRA\n  zpr   zp,rel     BBR and BBS: test a zero page bit, then branch\n  nop1             one byte, one cycle, no bus access after the fetch\n  spc              stack and flow specials, and 5C (see the bus listing)\nKinds: R read, W write, M read-modify-write, - other.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\n\nROWS = \"\"\"\n00 BRK spc  01 ORA indx 02 NOP imm  03 NOP nop1 04 TSB zp   05 ORA zp   06 ASL zp   07 RMB0 zp\n08 PHP spc  09 ORA imm  0A ASL acc  0B NOP nop1 0C TSB abs  0D ORA abs  0E ASL abs  0F BBR0 zpr\n10 BPL rel  11 ORA indy 12 ORA izp  13 NOP nop1 14 TRB zp   15 ORA zpx  16 ASL zpx  17 RMB1 zp\n18 CLC imp  19 ORA absy 1A INC acc  1B NOP nop1 1C TRB abs  1D ORA absx 1E ASL absx 1F BBR1 zpr\n20 JSR spc  21 AND indx 22 NOP imm  23 NOP nop1 24 BIT zp   25 AND zp   26 ROL zp   27 RMB2 zp\n28 PLP spc  29 AND imm  2A ROL acc  2B NOP nop1 2C BIT abs  2D AND abs  2E ROL abs  2F BBR2 zpr\n30 BMI rel  31 AND indy 32 AND izp  33 NOP nop1 34 BIT zpx  35 AND zpx  36 ROL zpx  37 RMB3 zp\n38 SEC imp  39 AND absy 3A DEC acc  3B NOP nop1 3C BIT absx 3D AND absx 3E ROL absx 3F BBR3 zpr\n40 RTI spc  41 EOR indx 42 NOP imm  43 NOP nop1 44 NOP zp   45 EOR zp   46 LSR zp   47 RMB4 zp\n48 PHA spc  49 EOR imm  4A LSR acc  4B NOP nop1 4C JMP spc  4D EOR abs  4E LSR abs  4F BBR4 zpr\n50 BVC rel  51 EOR indy 52 EOR izp  53 NOP nop1 54 NOP zpx  55 EOR zpx  56 LSR zpx  57 RMB5 zp\n58 CLI imp  59 EOR absy 5A PHY spc  5B NOP nop1 5C NOP spc  5D EOR absx 5E LSR absx 5F BBR5 zpr\n60 RTS spc  61 ADC indx 62 NOP imm  63 NOP nop1 64 STZ zp   65 ADC zp   66 ROR zp   67 RMB6 zp\n68 PLA spc  69 ADC imm  6A ROR acc  6B NOP nop1 6C JMP ind  6D ADC abs  6E ROR abs  6F BBR6 zpr\n70 BVS rel  71 ADC indy 72 ADC izp  73 NOP nop1 74 STZ zpx  75 ADC zpx  76 ROR zpx  77 RMB7 zp\n78 SEI imp  79 ADC absy 7A PLY spc  7B NOP nop1 7C JMP iax  7D ADC absx 7E ROR absx 7F BBR7 zpr\n80 BRA rel  81 STA indx 82 NOP imm  83 NOP nop1 84 STY zp   85 STA zp   86 STX zp   87 SMB0 zp\n88 DEY imp  89 BIT imm  8A TXA imp  8B NOP nop1 8C STY abs  8D STA abs  8E STX abs  8F BBS0 zpr\n90 BCC rel  91 STA indy 92 STA izp  93 NOP nop1 94 STY zpx  95 STA zpx  96 STX zpy  97 SMB1 zp\n98 TYA imp  99 STA absy 9A TXS imp  9B NOP nop1 9C STZ abs  9D STA absx 9E STZ absx 9F BBS1 zpr\nA0 LDY imm  A1 LDA indx A2 LDX imm  A3 NOP nop1 A4 LDY zp   A5 LDA zp   A6 LDX zp   A7 SMB2 zp\nA8 TAY imp  A9 LDA imm  AA TAX imp  AB NOP nop1 AC LDY abs  AD LDA abs  AE LDX abs  AF BBS2 zpr\nB0 BCS rel  B1 LDA indy B2 LDA izp  B3 NOP nop1 B4 LDY zpx  B5 LDA zpx  B6 LDX zpy  B7 SMB3 zp\nB8 CLV imp  B9 LDA absy BA TSX imp  BB NOP nop1 BC LDY absx BD LDA absx BE LDX absy BF BBS3 zpr\nC0 CPY imm  C1 CMP indx C2 NOP imm  C3 NOP nop1 C4 CPY zp   C5 CMP zp   C6 DEC zp   C7 SMB4 zp\nC8 INY imp  C9 CMP imm  CA DEX imp  CB NOP nop1 CC CPY abs  CD CMP abs  CE DEC abs  CF BBS4 zpr\nD0 BNE rel  D1 CMP indy D2 CMP izp  D3 NOP nop1 D4 NOP zpx  D5 CMP zpx  D6 DEC zpx  D7 SMB5 zp\nD8 CLD imp  D9 CMP absy DA PHX spc  DB NOP nop1 DC NOP abs  DD CMP absx DE DEC absx DF BBS5 zpr\nE0 CPX imm  E1 SBC indx E2 NOP imm  E3 NOP nop1 E4 CPX zp   E5 SBC zp   E6 INC zp   E7 SMB6 zp\nE8 INX imp  E9 SBC imm  EA NOP imp  EB NOP nop1 EC CPX abs  ED SBC abs  EE INC abs  EF BBS6 zpr\nF0 BEQ rel  F1 SBC indy F2 SBC izp  F3 NOP nop1 F4 NOP zpx  F5 SBC zpx  F6 INC zpx  F7 SMB7 zp\nF8 SED imp  F9 SBC absy FA PLX spc  FB NOP nop1 FC NOP abs  FD SBC absx FE INC absx FF BBS7 zpr\n\"\"\"\n\nREAD = set(\"ORA AND EOR ADC SBC CMP CPX CPY BIT LDA LDX LDY NOP\".split())\nWRITE = set(\"STA STX STY STZ\".split())\nRMW = set(\"ASL LSR ROL ROR INC DEC TSB TRB\".split())\n\n\ndef table():\n    toks = ROWS.split()\n    ops = {}\n    for i in range(0, len(toks), 3):\n        ops[int(toks[i], 16)] = (toks[i + 1], toks[i + 2])\n    assert len(ops) == 256, len(ops)\n    out = []\n    for op in range(256):\n        m, mode = ops[op]\n        base = m.rstrip(\"01234567\")\n        bit = int(m[-1]) if base in (\"RMB\", \"SMB\", \"BBR\", \"BBS\") else 0\n        if mode in (\"spc\", \"rel\", \"imp\", \"acc\", \"nop1\", \"ind\", \"iax\", \"zpr\"):\n            kind = \"-\"\n        elif base in (\"RMB\", \"SMB\") or m in RMW:\n            kind = \"M\"\n        elif m in WRITE:\n            kind = \"W\"\n        elif m in READ:\n            kind = \"R\"\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(hex(op))\n        out.append(dict(op=op, m=base, bit=bit, mode=mode, kind=kind,\n                        rockwell=base in (\"RMB\", \"SMB\", \"BBR\", \"BBS\")))\n    return out\n\n\nMODES = [\"imp\", \"acc\", \"imm\", \"zp\", \"zpx\", \"zpy\", \"abs\", \"absx\", \"absy\",\n         \"indx\", \"indy\", \"izp\", \"ind\", \"iax\", \"rel\", \"zpr\", \"nop1\", \"spc\"]\n\n\ndef emit_c(t):\n    mnems = sorted({r[\"m\"] for r in t})\n    kinds = {\"-\": \"C_NONE\", \"R\": \"C_READ\", \"W\": \"C_WRITE\", \"M\": \"C_RMW\"}\n    o = [\"/* Generated by docs/emulation-lynx/opcodes65c02.py --c. Do not edit. */\",\n         \"#ifndef OPCODE_TABLE_65C02_H\", \"#define OPCODE_TABLE_65C02_H\", \"\"]\n    o.append(\"enum c_mnem { \" + \", \".join(\"C_\" + m for m in mnems) + \" };\")\n    o.append(\"enum c_mode { \" + \", \".join(\"CA_\" + m.upper() for m in MODES) + \" };\")\n    o.append(\"enum c_kind { C_NONE, C_READ, C_WRITE, C_RMW };\")\n    for name, f in ((\"c_mnem_tab\", lambda r: \"C_\" + r[\"m\"]),\n                    (\"c_mode_tab\", lambda r: \"CA_\" + r[\"mode\"].upper()),\n                    (\"c_kind_tab\", lambda r: kinds[r[\"kind\"]])):\n        o.append(\"static const unsigned char %s[256] = {\" % name)\n        o += [\"  \" + \", \".join(f(r) for r in t[i:i + 8]) + \",\" for i in range(0, 256, 8)]\n        o.append(\"};\")\n    o.append(\"static const char c_name_tab[256][5] = {\")\n    o += [\"  \" + \", \".join('\"%s\"' % (r[\"m\"] + (str(r[\"bit\"]) if r[\"rockwell\"] else \"\"))\n                           for r in t[i:i + 8]) + \",\" for i in range(0, 256, 8)]\n    o.append(\"};\")\n    o += [\"\", \"#endif\", \"\"]\n    return \"\\n\".join(o)\n\n\ndef emit_text(t):\n    lines = [\"op  mnem  mode  kind  Lynx I\"]\n    for r in t:\n        name = r[\"m\"] + (str(r[\"bit\"]) if r[\"rockwell\"] else \"\")\n        lines.append(\"%02X  %-5s %-5s %s     %s\" % (r[\"op\"], name, r[\"mode\"], r[\"kind\"],\n                                                  \"NOP (1 byte, 1 cycle)\" if r[\"rockwell\"] else \"\"))\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    t = table()\n    sys.stdout.write(emit_c(t) if \"--c\" in sys.argv else emit_text(t) + \"\\n\")</code></pre></section>","interface":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface\">Core interface: Atari Lynx</h3>\n<p>How to read this document: sizes are in bytes; times are in <b>ticks</b> (the 16 MHz system clock, 16,000,000 a second) counted from power on; frames are calls of <code>lynx_run_frame</code>. Integers are 32-bit unless a type says otherwise; <code>uint64</code> results arrive in JavaScript as <code>BigInt</code>. Addresses and byte values written like <code>FF</code> or <code>0000-FFFF</code> are hexadecimal; every other number is decimal.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.1</b> (the module). The Atari Lynx core is one WebAssembly module, <code>lynx.wasm</code>, with no imports and no C library.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> It is compiled freestanding from <code>src/lynx/</code> with the 65C02 (<code>src/cpu65c02.c</code>) and the shared helpers (<code>src/rt.c</code>). The same sources compiled natively are the board machine the tests score; the two are checked against each other by hash (<code>tests/machines_lynx.sh</code>).</p></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.2</b> (words). A <em>cartridge</em> is the game's firmware image, a byte array, normally a .lnx file (a 64-byte header, then the image). The <em>boot firmware</em> is the console's own 512 bytes: the user may supply it; the core never needs it, because without it the core runs the project's own boot firmware, which performs the documented boot (it decrypts the cartridge's loader and runs it: <a href=\"#atarilynxemu-08-boot\"><code>docs/emulation-lynx/08-boot.md</code></a>).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.3</b> (memory and instances). Memory, instances, buffers and <code>BigInt</code> results must work exactly as for the 2600 core (<a href=\"#atari2600emu-interface\"><code>docs/INTERFACE.md</code></a>, \"Memory and instances\"), with <code>lynx_</code> in place of <code>atari_</code>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface--functions\">Functions</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.4</b> (the functions). The core must export these functions. All take the context first, except the ones marked (no context).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>lynx_sizeof_context()</code></td><td>Bytes in one context (no context)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_create()</code></td><td>New machine, no cartridge, already powered on (address, 0 on failure; \"Creating and destroying\", below)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_init(ctx)</code> / <code>lynx_destroy(ctx)</code></td><td>Initialise a context in memory the host owns (it powers on); mark a context dead. Below</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_set_firmware(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>Supply the boot firmware (512 bytes); size 0 removes it. Takes effect at the next power on (a cartridge load powers on). 0, or -1 for another size</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_has_firmware(ctx)</code></td><td>1 when the user's own firmware is supplied</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_load_cartridge(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>Copy <code>size</code> bytes from <code>ptr</code> (at most 2 MB and a header) and power on. The file is identified in the order of Rule Lynx:5.4. A file with the Lynx reference emulator's LNX2 header is read with its bank descriptors, cartridge RAM included. A file without the .lnx header (or whose header's version is not 1) is taken as a raw image of one bank, with pages of size / 256 (1 KB for an unusual size). A BLL program (a <code>.o</code> file: \"BS93\" at bytes 6-9) is put into RAM and run at every power on without the boot, and so is the loader of a headerless Epyx image (410 or 512 zero bytes first) (<a href=\"#atarilynxemu-05-cartridge\"><code>docs/emulation-lynx/05-cartridge.md</code></a>). 0, or -1 (<code>lynx_load_reason</code> says why); a refused file changes nothing but the reason text: the cartridge already loaded stays, and the machine is not powered on</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_load_reason(ctx)</code></td><td>Address of a NUL-terminated text saying how the cartridge was read (the texts are under \"Load reasons\", below; nothing scored reads them)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_eeprom_size(ctx)</code></td><td>Bytes in the cartridge's EEPROM (the .lnx header's byte 60), 0 if it has none</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_set_eeprom(ctx, type)</code></td><td>Name the EEPROM when the header does not (the header's byte 60 form: 1-5 for 93C46-93C86, plus 128 for the 8-bit organisation; 0 none). After loading; the chip starts erased. 0, or -1 for a byte naming no part: Rule Lynx:5.15 gives which bytes are refused (80 and C0 among them)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_eeprom_type(ctx)</code></td><td>The header's byte 60 as accepted (0: no EEPROM; a header's 80 or C0: Rule Lynx:5.15)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_eeprom_read(ctx, ptr, cap)</code></td><td>Copy the EEPROM's contents to <code>ptr</code> (at most <code>cap</code> bytes); returns the count. A 16-bit word n is bytes 2n (low) and 2n+1 (high), the layout the second Lynx emulator and the Lynx reference emulator save</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_eeprom_write(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>Set the contents (bytes past <code>size</code> become FF). Call after loading the cartridge, before running. 0, or -1 if there is no EEPROM</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_eeprom_changes(ctx)</code></td><td>Erase and write operations the program has made since the load (uint32): when it moves, the host saves the contents</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_rotation(ctx)</code></td><td>The header's rotation: 0 none, 1 left, 2 right (the host turns the picture)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_powered(ctx)</code></td><td>1 while the console is on; 0 once the program has switched it off (SYSCTL1 bit 1 written 0): what then happens, until the next power on, is Rule Lynx:4.21</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_format(ctx)</code></td><td>What the file was: 0 raw, 1 .lnx, 2 LNX2, 3 BLL program, 4 headerless Epyx image (an LNX2, .lnx or raw file that passes Rule Lynx:5.20). A new context with no cartridge (Rule Lynx:H.9) also gives 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_name(ctx)</code> / <code>lynx_cart_maker(ctx)</code></td><td>Addresses of the header's name and manufacturer, NUL-terminated (empty without a header)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_model(ctx)</code></td><td>2 when an LNX2 header's flags say the file is made for the Lynx II, else 0. The core does not act on it: the host may call <code>lynx_set_model</code> and <code>lynx_power_on</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_set_cart_ram(ctx, kind)</code></td><td>After loading a .lnx or raw file: kind 1 or 2 as Rules Lynx:5.26 and 5.27 say, 0 neither. 0, or -1 when not possible (an LNX2 file declares its own; a BLL program or a headerless Epyx image, Rule Lynx:5.20, has no cartridge port to add to; kind 2 on a file with a bank 1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_ram_kind(ctx)</code></td><td>0 none, 1 the second Lynx emulator's, 2 the 8 KB, 3 declared by an LNX2 header</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_ram_size(ctx)</code></td><td>Bytes of kept (battery-backed) cartridge RAM, 0 if none: the 8 KB bank, or an LNX2 header's kept banks in bank order</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_ram_read(ctx, ptr, cap)</code> / <code>lynx_cart_ram_write(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>As for the EEPROM: read returns the count; write (after loading, before running; bytes past <code>size</code> become FF) returns 0, or -1 if there is none</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_cart_ram_changes(ctx)</code></td><td>Writes to the kept RAM since the load (uint32): when it moves, the host saves the contents</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_set_model(ctx, model)</code> / <code>lynx_model(ctx)</code></td><td>1 Lynx I, 2 Lynx II (default). Takes effect at the next power on; <code>lynx_model</code> gives the model running</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_power_on(ctx)</code></td><td>Power cycle with the loaded cartridge</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_run_frame(ctx)</code></td><td>Run until the display starts visible line 0. 1 if a frame was completed, 0 if the display is not running (the call then returns at Rule Lynx:1.22's cut)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_step_instruction(ctx)</code></td><td>Run one CPU instruction (or one 8-tick step of sleep)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_raster(ctx)</code></td><td>Address of the picture: 160 x 102 uint16, each a 12-bit colour, green in bits 11-8, blue 7-4, red 3-0, unrotated</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_frame_count(ctx)</code></td><td>Calls of <code>lynx_run_frame</code> since power on, completed or not (uint64)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_ticks(ctx)</code></td><td>System ticks since power on (uint64; 16,000,000 per second)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_set_buttons(ctx, bits)</code></td><td>1 A, 2 B, 4 option 2, 8 option 1, 16 right, 32 left, 64 down, 128 up, 256 pause; 1 = pressed. Directions are the console's own (unrotated)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_audio_available(ctx)</code></td><td>Sample pairs waiting</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_audio_read(ctx, ptr, max)</code></td><td>Copy up to <code>max</code> pairs (int16 left, int16 right) to <code>ptr</code>; returns the count. 44,100 pairs a second, each the mix averaged over its ticks; the buffer: Rule Lynx:6.7</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_link(a, b)</code></td><td>Put two contexts on one ComLynx cable (each first leaves any other link). The console behind joins the other's time. 0, or -1 for a bad pair</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_unlink(ctx)</code> / <code>lynx_linked(ctx)</code></td><td>Take the cable out (both ends); the partner's address, 0 if none</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_link_step(ctx)</code></td><td>Run one instruction (or step of sleep) of whichever linked console is behind in the pair's time (<code>ctx</code> on a tie); returns the one stepped. Unlinked, it steps <code>ctx</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_link_run_frame(ctx)</code></td><td>As <code>lynx_run_frame</code> for <code>ctx</code>, the partner kept in lockstep and brought to the same time at the end; the partner's frame count goes up by the frames it completed. Unlinked, it is <code>lynx_run_frame</code>. Running one console of a pair alone lets them drift apart</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_state_size()</code></td><td>Bytes in a save state (no context)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_state_save(ctx, ptr)</code> / <code>lynx_state_load(ctx, ptr)</code></td><td>As for the 2600 core; load returns 0, or -1 if the bytes are not a state</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_trace_line(ctx, ptr, cap)</code></td><td>\"ticks pc a x y s p\" for the instruction about to run: NUL-terminated, no newline; returns the length, or -1 if <code>cap</code> is too small (chapter 11, \"The trace line\")</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_peek(ctx, addr)</code></td><td>A byte of RAM, 0000-FFFF (what the CPU would read with every region set to RAM)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_poke(ctx, addr, value)</code></td><td>Cheats: writes one byte of the RAM itself (0000-FFFF, never a register mapped over it), no other effect; 0 written, -1 outside. Nothing kept in the context.</td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx_audio_regs(ctx, ptr)</code></td><td>10 bytes: the four channels' outputs, ATTEN 0-3, MPAN, MSTEREO, as the CPU would read them (tools)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface--the-c-header\">The C header</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.5</b> (the C declarations). The native build and any core meant to be scored with the project's runner must use these C declarations (the header needs <code>&lt;stdint.h&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;stddef.h&gt;</code>). The context is an opaque type: <code>typedef struct lynx lynx;</code>, always passed as <code>lynx *</code> (or <code>const lynx *</code> where marked const below).</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.6</b> (constants).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>LYNX_W</code></td><td>160</td><td>picture width (a <code>#define</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_H</code></td><td>102</td><td>picture height (a <code>#define</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_MODEL_1</code>, <code>LYNX_MODEL_2</code></td><td>1, 2</td><td><code>enum lynx_model</code>: Lynx I, Lynx II</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_A</code>, <code>LYNX_B</code>, <code>LYNX_OPT2</code>, <code>LYNX_OPT1</code></td><td>1, 2, 4, 8</td><td>buttons (an unnamed enum)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_RIGHT</code>, <code>LYNX_LEFT</code>, <code>LYNX_DOWN</code>, <code>LYNX_UP</code></td><td>16, 32, 64, 128</td><td>directions</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_PAUSE</code></td><td>256</td><td>pause</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_FORMAT_RAW</code>, <code>LYNX_FORMAT_LYNX</code>, <code>LYNX_FORMAT_LNX2</code>, <code>LYNX_FORMAT_BLL</code>, <code>LYNX_FORMAT_EPYX</code></td><td>0, 1, 2, 3, 4</td><td>what <code>lynx_cart_format</code> returns (an unnamed enum)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_CART_RAM_NONE</code></td><td>0</td><td>no cartridge RAM added (this and the three below: one unnamed enum)</td></tr><tr><td>(kind 1)</td><td>1</td><td>the second Lynx emulator's bank 1 (its name in our header carries that emulator's name, which this tab does not print; call it what you like, for example <code>LYNX_CART_RAM_BANK1</code>: the runner passes the number)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_CART_RAM_8K</code></td><td>2</td><td>the 8 KB of kept RAM as bank 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>LYNX_CART_RAM_HEADER</code></td><td>3</td><td>RAM declared by an LNX2 header (reported only)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So only <code>LYNX_W</code> and <code>LYNX_H</code> are macros; every other constant is an enum member, and a runner never tests one with <code>#ifdef</code>. The header's include guard is <code>LYNX_API_H</code>, not <code>LYNX_H</code>: <code>LYNX_H</code> is the picture height, so a header guarded by <code>LYNX_H</code> would clash with it (a compiler warns of the redefinition). Use <code>LYNX_API_H</code> or any other name.</p>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.7</b> (function declarations), in the same order as the table of Rule Lynx:H.4:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Declaration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_sizeof_context(void);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx *lynx_create(void);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_destroy(lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_init(lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_set_firmware(lynx *m, const uint8_t *data, size_t size);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_has_firmware(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_load_cartridge(lynx *m, const uint8_t *data, size_t size);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>const char *lynx_load_reason(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_rotation(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_cart_format(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>const char *lynx_cart_name(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>const char *lynx_cart_maker(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_cart_model(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_set_cart_ram(lynx *m, int kind);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_cart_ram_kind(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_cart_ram_size(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>uint32_t lynx_cart_ram_changes(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_cart_ram_read(const lynx *m, uint8_t *out, size_t cap);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_cart_ram_write(lynx *m, const uint8_t *in, size_t size);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_eeprom_size(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_set_eeprom(lynx *m, int type);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_eeprom_type(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>uint32_t lynx_eeprom_changes(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_eeprom_read(const lynx *m, uint8_t *out, size_t cap);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_eeprom_write(lynx *m, const uint8_t *in, size_t size);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_powered(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_set_model(lynx *m, int model);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_model(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_power_on(lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_run_frame(lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_step_instruction(lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>const uint16_t *lynx_raster(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>uint64_t lynx_frame_count(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>uint64_t lynx_ticks(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_set_buttons(lynx *m, int bits);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_audio_available(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_audio_read(lynx *m, int16_t *out, size_t max_pairs);</code> (<code>out</code> holds 2 x <code>max_pairs</code> int16)</td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_link(lynx *a, lynx *b);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_unlink(lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx *lynx_linked(const lynx *m);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lynx *lynx_link_step(lynx *a);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_link_run_frame(lynx *a);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size_t lynx_state_size(void);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_state_save(const lynx *m, void *out);</code> (<code>out</code> holds <code>lynx_state_size()</code> bytes)</td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_state_load(lynx *m, const void *in);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_trace_line(const lynx *m, char *out, size_t cap);</code> (chapter 11 gives the text and the return value)</td></tr><tr><td><code>uint8_t lynx_peek(const lynx *m, int addr);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int lynx_poke(lynx *m, int addr, int value);</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>void lynx_audio_regs(const lynx *m, uint8_t out[10]);</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.8</b> (load reasons). The core's texts, word for word (a core of its own may word them as it likes; they are for people, and the runner only prints them):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a new context</td><td><code>no cartridge</code></td></tr><tr><td>a .lnx header, version 1</td><td><code>from the .lnx header</code>, or <code>from the .lnx header (an SD card cartridge: the card is not emulated)</code> when byte 60 bit 6 is set</td></tr><tr><td>an LNX2 header</td><td><code>from the LNX2 header</code>, <code>from the LNX2 header (with cartridge RAM)</code>, or <code>from the LNX2 header (an SD card cartridge: the card is not emulated)</code></td></tr><tr><td>no header, size a power of two from 64 KB to 512 KB</td><td><code>no .lnx header: a raw image, pages of size / 256</code></td></tr><tr><td>no header, another size</td><td><code>no .lnx header: a raw image of an unusual size, taken as 1 KB pages</code></td></tr><tr><td>no header, a raw image that the core's own short table of known raw images (kept by SHA-256, not printed on this tab; its two boards are in Rule Lynx:5.6) names</td><td>that row's own text, naming the board it gives</td></tr><tr><td>a .lnx header of another version</td><td><code>a .lnx header of a version other than 1: read as no header (as both references)</code></td></tr><tr><td>a BLL program</td><td><code>a BLL program (BS93 header): put into RAM and run without the boot</code></td></tr><tr><td>a headerless Epyx image</td><td><code>an Epyx image without the boot's frame (410 zero bytes): its loader decrypted to 0200 and run without the boot</code> (or <code>512 zero bytes</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>refused: LNX2 version</td><td><code>an LNX2 header of a version other than 1</code></td></tr><tr><td>refused: size</td><td><code>image size out of range</code></td></tr><tr><td>refused: page size</td><td><code>bank 0 page size is not a power of two up to 2 KB</code> (or <code>bank 1 ...</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>refused: BLL size</td><td><code>a BLL program whose header gives no code (size 10 or less)</code></td></tr><tr><td>after <code>lynx_state_load</code></td><td><code>restored from a save state</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.9</b> (creating and destroying). <code>lynx_init</code> must clear a context, set the next model to the Lynx II and power on at once, with no cartridge: the built-in firmware (or none supplied yet) runs from its reset vector, the cartridge ports read FF, and the load reason is \"no cartridge\". Nothing special happens after that: the firmware does exactly what it does with a cartridge. <code>lynx_create</code> gets memory for one context and calls <code>lynx_init</code>, so a new machine is already running.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Build</th><th><code>lynx_create</code></th><th><code>lynx_destroy</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the WebAssembly module</td><td>grows memory</td><td>only marks the context dead (its pages stay with the module; <code>lynx_init</code> can reuse them)</td></tr><tr><td>a native build</td><td>allocates with <code>malloc</code></td><td>frees: call it only on a context <code>lynx_create</code> made, never on memory the host owns and initialised with <code>lynx_init</code> (the host releases that itself)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Worked example: with our built-in firmware and no cartridge, the firmware reads the frame's first byte as FF (one block), decrypts 51 bytes of FF into 50 bytes at 0200 (meaningless code), reaches 0200 after 4,837,565 ticks (FE4A at 3,420,103) and runs whatever those bytes do; the display keeps running and <code>lynx_run_frame</code> keeps returning 1.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> Nothing scored runs without a cartridge.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface--timing\">Timing</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.10</b> (pacing). A frame is not a fixed length: the cartridge programs the display timers (most choose about 60 or 75 frames a second). A host that paces by wall time should count <code>lynx_ticks</code> (16 MHz) and run frames until it has caught up, as the test page does.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface--what-the-host-provides\">What the host provides</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.11</b> (what the host provides). The host provides the cartridge bytes, the buttons, optionally the boot firmware, and, for a cartridge with an EEPROM or kept cartridge RAM, its contents from the last session. Nothing is fetched or remembered by the core; a save state holds the whole machine, including the cartridge image (its size in bytes is what <code>lynx_state_size</code> returns), and is tied to the core that made it.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The test page keeps each set of contents in the browser's local storage, one entry per cartridge, keyed by a hash of the file; the RAM's key also names its kind. The test page's \"Cartridge RAM\" choice offers the two kinds a host may add to a .lnx or raw file. The page keys its save-state slots by the module's hash.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atarilynxemu-interface--two-consoles\">Two consoles</h4>\n<p><b>Rule Lynx:H.12</b> (a host that offers the ComLynx cable), in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>create a second context;</li><li>load it (the same cartridge or another) when the cable goes in, so the two are not switched on together (programs that choose a master and a slave rely on that, as on hardware);</li><li>call <code>lynx_link</code>;</li><li>from then on run <code>lynx_link_run_frame</code> on the first instead of <code>lynx_run_frame</code>, giving each console its own buttons.</li></ol>\n<p>A power cycle or a state load of either keeps the link; a save state holds one console.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Explanation.</b> The test page shows the two side by side; the keys drive console 1, console 2, or both (console 2 on W A S D, G for A, H for B); only console 1 is heard and has its EEPROM kept.</p></div></section>"},"lent":{"2600|interface":{"label":"Atari 2600","title":"Core interface","html":"<section class=\"sect atariemu-chapter\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface\">Core interface</h3>\n<p>The Atari 2600 core is one WebAssembly module, <code>atari2600.wasm</code>, compiled freestanding from the C sources in <code>src/</code> (no imports, no C library). The same sources compiled natively are the board machine the tests score; the two are checked against each other by hash (<code>tests/machines.sh</code>).</p>\n<p>Words: a <em>cartridge</em> is the game's firmware image, a byte array.</p>\n<p>How to read this part: each binding statement is a numbered rule (Rule 2600:H.1, 2600:H.2 ...); a box headed \"Explanation\" holds background only. Units: sizes in bytes, memory in 64 KB (65,536-byte) pages, cycles in CPU cycles from power on, rows of the raster from 0, samples one byte each.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--memory-and-instances\">Memory and instances</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.1</b> (memory and instances).</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The module exports its <code>memory</code>. It imports nothing.</li><li><code>atari_create()</code> grows memory by enough pages for one machine context and returns its address. Call it once per machine; any number of machines can run side by side. No function keeps state outside its context.</li><li><code>atari_destroy(ctx)</code> marks a context dead. Its pages stay with the module (wasm memory cannot shrink); <code>atari_init(ctx)</code> can reuse them.</li><li>The host owns every other buffer. To pass bytes in (a cartridge, a save state) or get bytes out (audio, a save state), grow memory yourself (<code>memory.grow(n)</code> returns the old page count; the new region starts at <code>old * 65536</code>) and pass that address. Re-take <code>memory.buffer</code> after any growth: the old <code>ArrayBuffer</code> is detached.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--functions\">Functions</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.2</b> (the functions). All take the context first. Integers are 32-bit unless noted; <code>uint64</code> results arrive in JavaScript as <code>BigInt</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>atari_sizeof_context()</code></td><td>Bytes in one context</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_create()</code></td><td>New powered-on machine, no cartridge (address, 0 on failure)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_init(ctx)</code></td><td>Initialise a context in memory you own</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_destroy(ctx)</code></td><td>See above</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_load_cartridge(ctx, ptr, size, scheme)</code></td><td>Copy <code>size</code> bytes (up to 512 KB) from <code>ptr</code>, pick the scheme (0 = detect), power on. Returns the scheme number or -1 (a load that fails: chapter 4, Rule 2600:4.13). For a multicart scheme the host names (2IN1..128IN1) it loads the game <code>atari_set_multicart_game</code> named and returns that game's scheme (2K, 4K, or the one detected for it; Rule 2600:4.60); a 4IN1 found by its hash with <code>scheme</code> 0: Rule 2600:4.9</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_multicart_game(ctx, n)</code></td><td>The game (0-based) the next multicart load takes</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_scheme_reason(ctx)</code></td><td>Address of a NUL-terminated text saying why that scheme was chosen</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_scheme_name(n)</code></td><td>Address of the scheme's name</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_region(ctx, r)</code></td><td>0 NTSC, 1 PAL, 2 SECAM. Set before loading. Affects the frame-length fallback and the DPC oscillator; the palette is the host's</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_power_on(ctx)</code></td><td>Power cycle with the loaded cartridge</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_run_frame(ctx)</code></td><td>Run until a frame completes. Returns rows drawn</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_run_part(ctx, cycles, begin)</code></td><td>Run part of a frame, so input can change at the moment it happened (a program may read a button twice in one frame). With <code>begin</code> set a new frame starts first; then the CPU runs until <code>cycles</code> more CPU cycles have passed (stopping on the instruction boundary at or after that) or the frame ends. Returns 1 when the frame ended, else 0; <code>cycles</code> 0 runs nothing (Rule 2600:10.6). <code>atari_run_frame</code> is this with <code>begin</code> set and no cycle limit; no state is added</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_step_instruction(ctx)</code></td><td>Run one CPU instruction</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_raster(ctx)</code></td><td>Address of the completed frame: 160 x 320 bytes</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_raster_lines(ctx)</code></td><td>Rows drawn in that frame</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_frame_scanlines(ctx)</code></td><td>Its length in scanlines (VSYNC end to VSYNC end)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_frame_count(ctx)</code></td><td>Frames completed (uint64)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_cpu_cycles(ctx)</code></td><td>CPU cycles since power on (uint64)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_joystick(ctx, port, bits)</code></td><td>Bits: 1 up, 2 down, 4 left, 8 right, 16 fire, 32 the second button on pin 5, 64 the third on pin 9 (Booster Grip, Genesis pad, Joy2B+) (1 = pressed). Ports 2 and 3 are the QuadTari's second joysticks</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_switches(ctx, bits)</code></td><td>1 reset, 2 select (1 = pressed), 8 colour (1) / B&amp;W (0), 64 left difficulty A, 128 right difficulty A</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_controller(ctx, port, type)</code></td><td>0 joystick, 1 paddles, 2 driving, 3 keypad, 4 CompuMate (both ports; loading a CM cartridge selects it, loading another restores joysticks), 5 Booster Grip, 6 Genesis pad, 7 Joy2B+, 8 CX22/CX80 trackball, 9 Atari ST mouse, 10 Amiga mouse, 11 SaveKey, 12 AtariVox, 13 light gun, 14 MindLink, 15 QuadTari, 16 nothing, 17 SNES2Atari, 18 Kid Vid (right port only; on the left port it gives 16, nothing; loading another image puts a joystick back: Rule 2600:7.38) (<a href=\"#atari2600emu-07-controllers\"><code>docs/emulation/07-controllers.md</code></a>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_paddle(ctx, n, pos)</code></td><td>Paddle 0..3 (0, 1 left port; 2, 3 right), position 0..65535, 0 = fully clockwise (least resistance)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_paddle_fire(ctx, n, pressed)</code></td><td>Paddle button</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_driving(ctx, port, step, fire)</code></td><td>Wheel position as a count; its low two bits select the Gray code. Adding one turns the wheel clockwise (to the right)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_keypad(ctx, port, mask)</code></td><td>12 bits, keys 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 * 0 # from bit 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_move_pointer(ctx, port, dx, dy)</code></td><td>Trackball and mice: the motion of the coming frame in device steps (x right, y down); repeated each frame until the host gives another. Fire through <code>atari_set_joystick</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_lightgun(ctx, port, x, y, trigger)</code></td><td>Aim at raster pixel x (0..159) on raster row y; trigger 1 = pulled</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_lightgun_offset(ctx, dx, dy)</code></td><td>The beam test's offset (default -23 colour clocks, +1 line; titles differ)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_mindlink(ctx, port, position)</code></td><td>MindLink position, 0x0B00..0x6500; bit 15 for the button some hosts add</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_snes(ctx, port, buttons)</code></td><td>SNES pad through the SNES2Atari adaptor: 1 B, 2 Y, 4 Select, 8 Start, 16 up, 32 down, 64 left, 128 right, 256 A, 512 X, 1024 L, 2048 R</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_eeprom(ctx)</code></td><td>Address of the SaveKey/AtariVox EEPROM (32768 bytes). The host keeps it between sessions by reading and writing it; it survives power cycles and loads. A new one reads FF</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_eeprom_changed(ctx)</code></td><td>1 when the program wrote the EEPROM since the last call</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_avox_read(ctx, ptr, max)</code></td><td>AtariVox: copy up to <code>max</code> bytes sent to the speech processor (not spoken); returns the count</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_compumate_key(ctx, key, pressed)</code></td><td>CompuMate key: column * 4 + row (the matrix in docs/emulation/04-cartridges.md), 40 FUNC, 41 SHIFT</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_nvram_size(ctx)</code></td><td>Bytes of cartridge non-volatile memory (FA2 flash, CTY EEPROM: 256), 0 for other cartridges</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_nvram(ctx)</code></td><td>Address of it. The host reads it to persist it (whenever it likes; it changes only through the cartridge's save operations)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_nvram_load(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>Copy saved contents in, after loading the cartridge. 0, or -1 if <code>size</code> is not <code>atari_nvram_size</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_supercharger_firmware(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>The console's own 2 KB Supercharger firmware, supplied by the user (optional; kept across loads and power cycles; takes effect at the next load or power cycle). With it, Supercharger loads arrive as the cassette's audio on the firmware's audio input, synthesised from the file (chapter 4, \"AR\"). <code>size</code> 0 goes back to the project's own loader. 0, or -1 if <code>size</code> is not 2048</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_has_supercharger_firmware(ctx)</code></td><td>1 while the user's own firmware is set</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_set_supercharger_tape(ctx, ptr, size)</code></td><td>A recording of a Supercharger tape, optional: a WAV file (RIFF WAVE; PCM 8, 16, 24 or 32 bits, or 32-bit float; any rate; the first channel is read). With the user's own firmware, the audio input reads the recording instead of the signal made from the file: from power on, between samples the line between them, high above the level halfway between the recording's low and high (its 1st and 99th percentiles, taken exactly as Rule 2600:4.69 gives), high past its end; the tape's loads arrive in turn, as from a real tape. The bytes stay the host's, in the module's memory, and must stay in place while the tape is in; they are not in save states (a load keeps the tape that is in). Give it after <code>atari_set_supercharger_firmware</code>; <code>size</code> 0 takes it out. 0, or -1 if the file is not a WAV of those kinds</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_has_supercharger_tape(ctx)</code></td><td>1 while a tape recording is in</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_kidvid_game(ctx)</code></td><td>The Kid Vid cartridge loaded: 0 none, 1 Smurfs Save the Day (files <code>KVSHARED.WAV</code>, <code>KVS1.WAV</code> to <code>KVS3.WAV</code>), 2 Berenstain Bears (<code>KVSHARED.WAV</code>, <code>KVB1.WAV</code> to <code>KVB3.WAV</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_kidvid_file_min_size(file)</code></td><td>The least audio data (bytes after the WAV header) file <code>file</code> must hold: 0 <code>KVSHARED</code>, 1 to 3 <code>KVS1</code> to <code>KVS3</code>, 4 to 6 <code>KVB1</code> to <code>KVB3</code> (<code>ATARI_KV_*</code>). A shorter file is not the recording the position tables fit</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_kidvid_set_tapes(ctx, present)</code></td><td>The host holds the game's four tape audio files (each 8-bit mono PCM and at least the size above): 1 after loading the cartridge and after each power on (a state load keeps the live setting; a state carries 0). From the next tape chosen, each narration lasts as long as its recording (chapter 7). Without it, the silent behaviour of chapter 7 (the tapes' audio absent)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_kidvid_speech(ctx, ptr_offset, ptr_count)</code></td><td>After each <code>atari_run_frame</code>: the file whose narration bytes the frame played (-1 none), with their first byte's offset in that file's audio data (after the header) and their count (uint32 each at the two pointers). One byte a scanline: mix byte <code>k</code> into the frame's TIA samples <code>2k</code> and <code>2k + 1</code> (unsigned, 128 is silence)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_load_movie(ctx, ptr, size, stream_size)</code></td><td>Load a Movie Cart stream (scheme MVC): <code>ptr</code>/<code>size</code> are the stream's first bytes (at least 4, the \"MVC\", 0 signature; a whole first block of 4096 bytes if the host has it), <code>stream_size</code> (uint64) the file's length. The core keeps no more of the stream than it asks for. Returns the scheme number (54) or -1</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_movie_wanted(ctx, ptr, max)</code></td><td>Write up to <code>max</code> block numbers (uint32 each) the core wants to <code>ptr</code>; returns the count. The first is the block needed next: it is chosen when a field ends and used when the following field ends, with a frame boundary between, so a host that asks after every <code>atari_run_frame</code> and supplies before the next is never late. The others are read-ahead</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_movie_supply(ctx, block, ptr, size)</code></td><td>Hand over block <code>block</code> (bytes <code>block * 4096</code> of the file, <code>size</code> at least 4096). 0, or -1 (not a Movie Cart, block past the end, too short). The core keeps six blocks</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_movie_underruns(ctx)</code></td><td>Fields shown black and silent because their block had not arrived</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_movie_field(ctx)</code></td><td>The block being shown, -1 during the title</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_audio_available(ctx)</code></td><td>Samples waiting</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_audio_read(ctx, ptr, max)</code></td><td>Copy up to <code>max</code> samples to <code>ptr</code>; returns the count</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_state_size()</code></td><td>Bytes in a save state</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_state_save(ctx, ptr)</code></td><td>Write a save state to <code>ptr</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_state_load(ctx, ptr)</code></td><td>Restore one; 0 on success, -1 if the bytes are not a state</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_trace_line(ctx, ptr, cap)</code></td><td>Write \"cycle pc a x y s p\" for the next instruction (the exact form: chapter 10, Rule 2600:10.6)</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_peek_ram(ctx, addr)</code></td><td>One of the 128 RIOT RAM bytes</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_poke_ram(ctx, addr, value)</code></td><td>Cheats: writes one RIOT RAM byte, no other effect. 0x80-0xFF (CPU address) or 0x00-0x7F (offset) both reach byte addr &amp; 0x7F; 0 written, -1 outside 0x00-0xFF. Nothing kept in the context.</td></tr><tr><td><code>atari_cpu_pc(ctx)</code></td><td>The CPU's 16-bit program counter as it holds it (for example <code>$F000</code>); the trace line prints the 13-bit address (<code>$1000</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.3</b> (scheme numbers). Scheme numbers follow <code>enum atari_scheme</code> in <code>src/atari.h</code> (0 AUTO, 1 2K, ... 16 3E, then 17 AR to 53 128IN1, then 54 MVC, then 55 DPC+, 56 CDF, 57 BUS, 58 ELF, in the order <code>atari_scheme_name</code> gives); numbers never change once given. A Supercharger (AR) cartridge loads through the core's own loader firmware and takes some frames to start (chapter 4); with the user's own firmware it loads from the synthesised cassette signal in real tape time (about 20 seconds a load).</p>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.4</b> (the Movie Cart stream). A Movie Cart stream is never an image for <code>atari_load_cartridge</code> (which refuses the MVC signature and names <code>atari_load_movie</code>). The stream is a file of 4096-byte blocks, one field each, usually hundreds of megabytes; the host keeps it (the native runner reads it with <code>fseek</code>/<code>fread</code>, the test page with <code>File.slice()</code>) and supplies what the core asks for:</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>atari_load_movie(m, head, head_size, file_size);\nloop:\n  atari_run_frame(m);\n  n = atari_movie_wanted(m, list, 8);\n  for each block b in list: read bytes b * 4096 .. + 4096; atari_movie_supply(m, b, data, 4096);</code></pre>\n<p>An asynchronous host (the page) may supply a block after the next frame has started: the core takes it whenever it arrives, and only a block still missing when it is needed counts as late.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--video\">Video</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.5</b> (video). The raster is every row the beam drew between VSYNC and the next VSYNC, starting at the first whole line after VSYNC ends: 320 rows of 160 bytes. Each byte is the TIA colour value (bit 0 always 0); turn it into RGB with a palette (<code>web/palette.js</code>, generated by <a href=\"#atari2600emu-palette-py\"><code>docs/emulation/palette.py</code></a>). Rows not drawn in a frame are 0. A television would show only part of it (the rows the test page shows: chapter 8, Rule 2600:8.4). Present the pixels twice as wide as they are tall.</p>\n<p>A program that never produces VSYNC still gets frames (the core cuts frame 0 after 51 lines and every later one after 372 lines, chapter 5 \"Frames\"), so the picture holds still.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--audio\">Audio</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.6</b> (audio). The samples are chapter 6's, Rule 2600:6.6 (two per scanline, NTSC having 15,700 lines per second; each byte holds both channels' levels, both 0..15). Mixing and resampling are the host's (Rule 2600:6.7, which describes the test page's mix). The ring of samples the core keeps is chapter 10's, Rule 2600:10.6; read them every frame.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--timing\">Timing</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.7</b> (timing). One frame is whatever the program makes it: normally 262 lines NTSC, 312 PAL, but a title can differ, even from frame to frame. A frame at 262 lines is 19,912 CPU cycles. Pace by CPU cycles against wall time, not by a fixed frame rate (chapter 1, Rule 2600:1.10).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--save-states\">Save states</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.8</b> (save states). A state is the whole context, copied flat (the only pointer inside is a text address, which a load replaces). A state is valid only for the core that wrote it: the handoff names the core hash, and states from another build must be treated as unknown.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"atari2600emu-interface--core-identity\">Core identity</h4>\n<p><b>Rule 2600:H.9</b> (core identity). <code>dist/atari2600.wasm.sha256</code> holds the SHA-256 of the module the documentation describes. <code>dist/HANDOFF.md</code> states it with the board results.</p></section>"}}};

/* The Atari Lynx guide alone. Its links into another console's chapters
 * resolve only in the drawer the dispatcher composes, so the site calls
 * atariEmulationTabHTML(d), not this. */
function atarilynxEmulationTabHTML(d) {
  var h = ATARI_EMU_LYNX.head;
  for (var i = 0; i < ATARI_EMU_LYNX.order.length; i++) h += "\n" + ATARI_EMU_LYNX.units[ATARI_EMU_LYNX.order[i]];
  return '<div class="gbemu atariemu atariemu-lynx" id="atarilynxemu-guide">' + h + '</div>';
}
